/* Product Master 1.10.40 · Why not this one? reasons (EN) · _w19/learn_build · loaded on demand by the LB block */
window.__PM_LBW_en={v:"556256f0",q:{"0":{"0":{"0":"The course is far bigger than 5 stages: 38 stages, 23 of them core, plus book chapters as enrichment.","1":"There are far more than 10: the core alone is 23 stages, and each one ends with a quiz, not a summary exercise.","3":"There are no 100 stages split by quarters. The course has 38 stages, 23 of them core, and 36 book chapters."},"1":{"0":"Stages aren't video-only. Each has theory, a story-driven case study and a quiz, so there is hands-on practice.","2":"The quiz is only one part. Each stage opens with theory and a case study before the questions.","3":"The theory is interactive: you click on terms, follow Michal's case and answer a quiz."},"2":{"0":"Cagan writes about product teams; the stage presents him as the author of Inspired and Empowered, not a language creator.","2":"The stage lists Cagan's background at HP, Netscape and eBay and his books on Product Discovery. Facebook isn't part of it.","3":"Cagan worked at HP, Netscape and eBay and founded SVPG. He's known for Inspired and Empowered, not Windows."},"3":{"0":"Rachitsky, a former PM at Airbnb, writes about Retention, Growth and PMF, not graphic design.","2":"In the course, AI belongs to Karpathy. Rachitsky's focus is Retention, Growth and Product-Market Fit.","3":"Rachitsky isn't a finance specialist. His newsletter covers Retention, Growth and growth levers."},"4":{"0":"This flips Yang's principle. By his measure, a team that ships 10 features nobody uses has failed.","2":"Code over Design isn't Yang's principle. He emphasizes Outcome over Output, Product Sense and First Principles.","3":"Yang's principle is about measuring impact, not speed at any cost. Outcome over Output judges by results."},"5":{"0":"Karpathy is an AI figure, an OpenAI co-founder and former head of AI at Tesla, not a marketing author.","2":"Design Systems aren't Karpathy's field. He explains AI concepts: Software 2.0, LLMs and Tokenization.","3":"Agile and Scrum belong to other leaders in the course. Karpathy teaches AI in an intuitive way."},"6":{"0":"The ranks are product titles, not military ones. Finishing the 23 core stages earns Pro Product Manager.","2":"There's no doctorate in the system. The ranks are symbolic: the 23 core stages earn Pro Product Manager.","3":"The system does award ranks, from Product Management Student up to Senior Product Manager, to show progress."},"7":{"0":"Skipping theory misses the base each section builds on. Even quiz mistakes teach through their explanations.","2":"Copying answers skips the learning. There's no passing grade, so the score itself isn't the point.","3":"A one-day marathon leads to fast forgetting. One stage a day gives time to digest the material."},"8":{"0":"There is a difference in format: the course adds cases, quizzes with feedback and progression, active not passive learning.","2":"The course includes book chapters as enrichment too. Its edge is active learning, not narrower coverage.","3":"The course goes deep: 23 core stages, plus advanced sections and book chapters."},"9":{"0":"They're tied together: understanding the domain is how a PM turns real needs into product requirements.","2":"A PM doesn't need to operate every system. They need to understand the context, the processes and the users.","3":"Promotion isn't the reason. Domain knowledge is what lets a PM build products that truly solve problems."},"10":{"0":"The goal isn't entertainment or scores; there's no passing grade. A case turns reading into active learning.","2":"The case doesn't fill gaps in the theory. It shows how the theory is applied in practice.","3":"It's not that there's no alternative. The course chose a case because active learning deepens understanding."},"11":{"0":"Timelines are project management. The tree links business goals to user needs and to solutions.","2":"It doesn't allocate budgets. It's a visual map from goals, through user needs, to solutions.","3":"It isn't an employee review tool. It gives structure to ongoing Discovery: goal, needs and solutions."},"12":{"0":"The system doesn't block you. You can start from any stage and come back whenever you like; the pace is a recommendation.","2":"There's no 24-hour requirement and no time pressure. The point is time to digest and retain the knowledge.","3":"There's no passing grade, and more stages a day don't lower a score. The pace is about learning that lasts."},"13":{"0":"Ries argues the opposite: build a minimal version and learn fast, instead of perfecting a product in a vacuum.","2":"Ries tests demand with an MVP and quick cycles, not by spending the whole budget on marketing first.","3":"A big team building fast isn't learning. Ries's core is Build-Measure-Learn with a minimal product."},"14":{"0":"They worked at different places: Cagan at HP, Netscape and eBay, Rachitsky at Airbnb, Karpathy at OpenAI and Tesla.","2":"They don't put code first. The common thread is understanding the customer and the problem.","3":"They don't reject data: Rachitsky and Yang lean on metrics. What unites them is a focus on the customer and the problem."}},"1":{"0":{"0":"There are five branches, not three: Staff, Sea, Intelligence, Equipment (MATZAD) and Personnel.","2":"Branches aren't split by base. The five are Staff, Sea, Intelligence, Equipment and Personnel; the bases host units.","3":"There isn't a branch per unit. Units such as MAPTACH or YASHI sit under the five branches."},"1":{"0":"The naval commando is Flotilla 13, based in Atlit. Flotilla 3 is the missile-ship flotilla.","2":"The submarines are Flotilla 7. Flotilla 3 operates the Sa'ar corvettes, the Navy's main striking force.","3":"Training takes place at Training Base 600 in Haifa. Flotilla 3 is the missile-ship striking force."},"2":{"0":"Vessel maintenance is the Navy Shipyard's job. GASHI patrols from the air using UAVs.","2":"Recruitment and screening belong to the Personnel Branch. GASHI builds a naval picture from the air.","3":"The stage gives GASHI an aerial mission: patrol, observation and early warning with the Heron 1 UAV, not supply."},"3":{"0":"MAPTACH develops software and computing, not munitions: the name stands for Software and Computing Development Center.","2":"MAPTACH isn't a command headquarters. It's a software and computing development center under the Equipment Branch.","3":"It isn't a projects department. MAPTACH is the Software and Computing Development Center."},"4":{"0":"3 is too few: MAPTACH has 7 sections, from Software Development and Cyber to C2.","1":"There are 7, not 5: Software Development, Cyber, Information Systems, Digital, Integrations, Infrastructure and C2.","3":"There are 7 sections, not 12. Each covers a domain, from Cyber to Integrations and C2."},"5":{"0":"The stage stresses they differ: a mispan is a headquarters division, a mispana is the physical maintenance facility.","2":"It's not about size. A mispan is an organizational division; a mispana is a maintenance facility.","3":"Both exist today. The five branches run the headquarters, and the Shipyard has operated continuously since 1948."},"6":{"0":"Flotilla 3 is the missile-ship flotilla. The submarines belong to Flotilla 7.","2":"Flotilla 13 is the naval commando, in Atlit. The submarines are Flotilla 7, based in Haifa.","3":"The stage describes only Flotillas 3, 7 and 13, and the submarines belong to Flotilla 7."},"7":{"0":"Blockade enforcement is one of the Navy's missions, but the stage says Sa'ar 6 was built specifically to protect the gas rigs.","2":"Routine patrol falls mainly to the patrol squadrons and their boats. Sa'ar 6 was built for the gas rigs.","3":"Moving forces and equipment is the job of the LSV landing craft. Sa'ar 6 protects the gas rigs."},"8":{"0":"MAPTACH develops operational software for every platform; it doesn't only maintain physical equipment.","2":"The placement has a logic: developing systems is force buildup, R&D and procurement, the Equipment Branch's domain.","3":"MAPTACH is closely tied to operations: it builds combat and C2 systems for ships and submarines."},"9":{"0":"The MASHRAM's role is merging the three theater pictures into one; the stage gives decisions to the Commander of the Navy.","2":"Communications support is the job of Waves Battalion 397. The MASHRAM builds one nationwide picture.","3":"The Shipyard maintains vessels, under the Equipment Branch. The MASHRAM sits in YASHI and builds the picture."}},"2":{"0":{"0":"Introduction is the search for PMF with few users. Velox already has 94% retention and grows 15% a month.","2":"Maturity means a saturated market and slowing growth. 15% a month is rapid growth, and 10K users is tiny in a $20B market.","3":"Decline means demand is dropping. Velox adds users at 15% a month, the opposite of decline."},"1":{"0":"Paying for a plan is a different conversion metric. Activation counts sign-ups who reach the first value moment, paid or not.","2":"Satisfaction surveys measure sentiment. Activation measures behavior: did a new sign-up actually reach the Aha Moment.","3":"Coming back in the second month is Retention. Activation happens earlier: reaching first value right after sign-up."},"2":{"0":"Budget isn't the point. The video was a deliberate test: it checked demand within days, before months of development.","2":"Buzz was a side effect, not the goal. The 70,000 wait-list sign-ups mattered as proof the problem was real.","3":"Team availability wasn't the reason. A Video MVP tests the demand assumption before investing in building."},"3":{"0":"Innovators are the 2.5% who chase new tech. Velox pitches a business gain, half the price of Asana, which fits Early Adopters.","2":"The Early Majority wants a proven, stable solution with references. With 10K users in a $20B market, Velox isn't there yet.","3":"Laggards adopt only when there's no choice left. Leaving the familiar Asana for a young tool is taking a risk, their opposite."},"4":{"0":"A Star holds a high market share. Here the share is low, so the product hasn't earned the leader's spot yet.","1":"A Cash Cow holds a high share in a market that stopped growing. Here the market grows and the share is low: both axes flip.","3":"A Dog also has a low share, but in a market that isn't growing. Here the market grows, so investing is still on the table."},"5":{"0":"The stage doesn't tie B2B2C to price. Its defining trait is the route: sold to a business, reaching consumers through it.","2":"Nothing in B2B2C removes sales. Stripe and Twilio still sell to businesses; the difference is who ends up using it.","3":"B2B2C is actually more complex: the product must satisfy both the business and the end consumer, not one interface."},"6":{"0":"Functional is one of the three MVP requirements: it has to perform the core action at a basic level.","1":"Reliable is one of the three MVP requirements: people must be able to rely on it in real use.","3":"Usable is one of the three MVP requirements: users must understand how to use it without special training."},"7":{"0":"Fit needs the whole Value Map to answer the Customer Profile. Gains without the Jobs leave the core need unanswered.","2":"Price is just one Pain Reliever. Fit is about answering the customer's Jobs, Pains and Gains, not beating rivals on cost.","3":"Sign-ups measure usage, not Fit. The canvas checks whether what the product offers answers the Jobs, Pains and Gains."},"8":{"0":"50 customers say nothing about who they are. Innovators are a type of person, tech lovers, not a customer count.","1":"Early Adopters are defined by mindset, not a number. Without market size and who the customers are, you can't place them.","2":"Pragmatists who wait for proof may or may not be among the 50. The count alone doesn't reveal the stage."},"9":{"0":"They differ in scope: a product solves a complete problem with standalone value, a Feature is one capability inside it.","2":"The difference isn't code size. A product solves a whole problem for a defined audience; a Feature is one capability in it.","3":"A Feature lives inside a product with no standalone value. Reporting police is part of Waze, not something sold alone."},"10":{"0":"Decline means demand drops across the market. Here the product thrives with startups; the gap is in one audience.","2":"The product already works well for startups. The Enterprise gap fits the Chasm: they want a Whole Product, not just good tech.","3":"The stage treats Enterprise as the Early Majority, reached by crossing the Chasm with a Whole Product such as SSO and support."},"11":{"0":"Churn alone doesn't prove failure. At Velox only 5 of 12 stayed, but they couldn't stop using it: the first sign of PMF.","2":"The stage sets no 80% retention bar for an MVP. What counts is how engaged the users who stayed are.","3":"An MVP must be usable: Functional, Reliable and Usable. It's minimal in scope, not a product nobody can use."},"12":{"0":"A Cash Cow needs minimal investment. Pouring resources in to revive peak growth fights the market's natural slowdown.","2":"Maturity isn't failure. A high share in a stable market is a Cash Cow that generates steady revenue.","3":"A new name doesn't change the market's growth rate. The BCG move is to harvest the steady revenue and fund new products."},"13":{"0":"Skipping a warehouse was a side benefit. The main gain was testing whether people would buy shoes online at all.","2":"The stage never says manual beats automation. A manual MVP is a cheap test; once demand is proven, you build the system.","3":"Build speed isn't the point, and the stage promises no one-day build. The point is testing real demand with minimal investment."},"14":{"0":"All three pillars must hold together. Without Viable there's no business model to justify the investment.","2":"Competitors aren't the issue. Not Viable means no business model justifies the investment, with or without rivals.","3":"Viable isn't the only pillar. Without Desirable there's no demand, without Feasible nothing gets built; all three are needed."}},"3":{"0":{"0":"A quarterly Roadmap in week one means deciding before knowing the product, the users and the team.","2":"Jumping on one bug turns week one into firefighting. That week is for learning the product, users and team before acting.","3":"Reordering priorities before understanding the product and team is deciding blind. Week one is for getting acquainted."},"1":{"0":"The Roadmap is a business detail for later. A first meeting builds the partnership, the foundation for everything else.","2":"Bugs and timelines are operational details. The first meeting with the Tech Lead is for getting to know each other.","3":"Imposing a new methodology at a first meeting opens the partnership with orders. First get to know the team and its dynamics."},"2":{"0":"Market and competitor research gets only 5%. Strategy, at 17%, is the largest slice in the McKinsey study.","2":"Pricing and packaging is the smallest slice, just 2%, even though it can be the most impactful thing.","3":"Tracking metrics takes 8%, less than half of the 17% that goes to defining strategy."},"3":{"0":"The stage stresses it's not a hierarchy but two lenses on the same work. The difference is focus, not pay or rank.","2":"A project can finish on time and budget and nobody needs it. The PM exists for value, the PjM for execution.","3":"A PM works with every stakeholder: leadership, marketing, sales, Customer Success. The split is what vs how, not with whom."},"4":{"0":"A Thinker fits the introduction stage, the search for PMF. Growth needs someone who decides fast and executes.","2":"An Improver fits maturity: optimizing existing processes. In growth the priority is fast execution and leading teams.","3":"A Producer fits the retention stage, focused on quality and upkeep. A growing product needs a Builder's pace."},"5":{"0":"Marketing, sales and support are stakeholders the PM serves. Eriksson's three circles are Business, Technology and UX.","2":"Design, development and testing are stages of building. Eriksson's circles are Business, Technology and UX.","3":"These are work modes, not Eriksson's circles. His Venn diagram places the PM between Business, Technology and UX."},"6":{"0":"The opposite: a PM must influence others all the time, since nobody reports to them and decisions need buy-in.","2":"Without authority, a PM can't simply decide alone. They have to persuade developers, designers and stakeholders.","3":"Nobody leads alone. Leading without authority means bringing the team and stakeholders along through data and trust."},"7":{"0":"Close project control is the PjM's job. A PM spending 60% on schedules and budget neglects the what and the why.","2":"Meeting dates says nothing about value. A project can finish on time and budget and still deliver nothing anyone needs.","3":"Time and budget are execution questions. Product decisions start from value to the user and the business."},"8":{"0":"Blaming development misses the PM's role: making sure the team builds the right thing through clear vision and priorities.","2":"The PM's job is to understand the need, not wait for customers to ask. An unrequested feature points to unclear priorities.","3":"Treating waste as inevitable skips the lesson. A clear vision and Backlog management are exactly what prevents it."},"9":{"0":"Defining the vision and strategic direction is a core PM duty: the PM owns the what and the why.","2":"Analyzing usage data is part of the job: the PM decides from data and spots user behavior patterns.","3":"Stakeholder communication is central: the PM is the busiest node on the org map, aligning every department."},"10":{"0":"Hours aren't the issue. The team shipped plenty; it measured how much it built, not what changed for users.","2":"More output won't help: 15 features already moved nothing. The fix is to measure Outcome, not to ship more.","3":"Budget wasn't the gap. The features shipped but solved no real problem, the classic Feature Team pattern."},"11":{"0":"Blind execution is the Feature Team pattern. Even an urgent CEO request deserves a check of which problem it solves.","2":"An empowered PM doesn't refuse outright either. They understand the problem behind the request and validate it.","3":"Passing it on skips the PM's own job: owning the what and the why, and checking the problem before building."},"12":{"0":"That is exactly the sequence Cagan rejects. The trio works together from the first moment, not in turns.","2":"Deciding alone and informing the others breaks the trio. Each owns a different risk: value, usability and feasibility.","3":"No one leads alone. The PM owns value and viability, the Designer usability, the Tech Lead feasibility, all together."},"13":{"0":"The stage says it's natural for a PM in a small org to manage projects too. The problem starts only when it takes over.","2":"The stage sets no company-size threshold. The signal is time: when coordination crowds out strategy and discovery.","3":"Combining the roles isn't always better. When schedules eat most of the time, strategy and discovery suffer."},"14":{"0":"Yang's point isn't about coding. A PM doesn't need to write code; they need to own the problem and the outcome.","2":"It's not about effort or docs. The problem is the mindset: waiting for orders instead of owning the problem.","3":"Owning the product doesn't mean ignoring leadership and customers. It means leading from understanding, not compliance."}},"4":{"0":{"0":"Users describe solutions, not problems. Logging a CSV button without asking why may build the wrong fix.","2":"Shutting the request down stops the learning. The interview is a chance to find the problem behind the request.","3":"Promising a build before understanding the need commits to a solution that may not solve the real problem."},"1":{"0":"Sarah means big deals, but Alex is the Decision Maker: if he adopts the tool, his whole team follows.","1":"Mike uses the tool daily but doesn't decide for the team. Alex does, and he also bridges Sarah's and Mike's needs.","3":"Equal resources for every persona means focus on none. Personas exist for sharp choices, not serving everyone at once."},"2":{"0":"1-2 interviews can't show a pattern: a pattern is something that repeats. Repetition usually starts only around 5.","2":"In-depth interviews aren't meant for statistical significance; that's the job of quantitative research with a large sample.","3":"After 5-8 interviews you reach saturation: more barely reveal anything new, and beyond that is wasted time at this stage."},"3":{"0":"A survey validates patterns but rarely reveals problems users aren't aware of. In Discovery, observations come first.","1":"Observations shine in Discovery, but a small sample can't show how big a problem is. That's where surveys come in.","3":"They answer different questions: observations reveal why and how, surveys measure how much. The results aren't interchangeable."},"4":{"0":"Awareness is about bringing people in. These users already signed up; 55% drop off after sign-up, before reaching value.","2":"Retention is 94%, so those who reach value stay. The leak happens earlier, in Onboarding.","3":"Advocacy comes once users are happy. The break is earlier: 55% of sign-ups never reach the moment of value."},"5":{"0":"Asking about a hypothetical future invites a polite yes. The Mom Test asks about specific past behavior instead.","2":"A deck turns the interview into selling. People praise what they're shown; past behavior is what reveals demand.","3":"Closed survey questions about an idea still collect opinions. The Mom Test looks for facts: what people actually did."},"6":{"0":"A sprint task list is a Backlog. JTBD is about users: the job they hire a product to do.","2":"That describes project management. JTBD is a theory of why people use products, not how teams split work.","3":"JTBD has nothing to do with employee performance. It explains the motivation behind customers' use of a product."},"7":{"0":"Numbers first means measuring before knowing what to measure. Qualitative research first uncovers the problem itself.","2":"The order matters: discovery answers what the problem is, and only then can validation measure how big it is.","3":"Each answers a different question, why versus how much. Skip one and either the problem or its size stays unknown."},"8":{"0":"Request count doesn't make the solution right. Customers describe solutions; the need behind them may have a better fix.","2":"Never listening goes too far. Customers are the source of needs; the work is digging for the problem behind the request.","3":"Ease of building doesn't prove value. Even a one-week feature is waste if it doesn't answer the real need."},"9":{"0":"Observation still needs planning. Its advantage is what it reveals: behavior users don't notice in themselves.","2":"Interviews capture what people say, and people don't always report their behavior accurately. Observation fills that gap.","3":"Speed isn't the edge. Observation shows the gap between what people say and what they actually do."},"10":{"0":"It's not a lie but a cognitive bias: people misreport their own behavior. Interviews stay useful when combined with observation.","2":"Nothing suggests the instructions changed. The gap between saying and doing is a common, natural pattern.","3":"Writing off the observation as a fluke misses the lesson: what people say they do often differs from what they do."},"11":{"0":"Data isn't the limit. The rule exists for focus: with too many personas you serve no one well.","2":"Cost isn't the reason. Ten personas dilute focus, so product decisions stop being sharp.","3":"The limit has nothing to do with board space. It keeps the focus on the most important segments."},"12":{"0":"Pretty charts are still a solution. 5 Whys digs beneath the dashboard to the need: knowing what's happening.","2":"That's a generalization, not a need. 5 Whys asks why this user wants the data, and the answer is visibility.","3":"Taking the request literally skips 5 Whys. A dashboard is a solution; smart alerts or a daily update may serve the need better."},"13":{"0":"Speed isn't the main risk. The bigger cost is building, fast and well, something nobody needs.","2":"You are not the user: even an experienced PM has cognitive biases. Intuition alone can miss the real problem.","3":"The risk isn't looks. Correct functionality is worth nothing if the feature solves a problem that doesn't exist."},"14":{"0":"It isn't cheaper; it means going to where the work happens. Its value is seeing real constraints and tools.","2":"Contextual Inquiry fits any product, software included. Go to the Gemba means going to where the work happens.","3":"The number of participants isn't the point. It reveals the environment, tools and habits a regular interview misses."}},"5":{"0":{"0":"The win/loss data points elsewhere: the losses come from engineering teams who say they prefer Linear's UI, not its price.","2":"Velox loses not because of a missing feature but because of experience: speed, keyboard shortcuts and the feel of the UI.","3":"Win/loss interviews point to the product itself: developers prefer Linear's UI. Awareness doesn't explain losing head-to-head deals."},"1":{"0":"SOM is what's realistic in 2-3 years given competition and resources. No marketing budget makes it reachable tomorrow.","2":"The whole global market is TAM, about $20B for Velox. SOM is the narrow slice: Series A-C startups in the US.","3":"SOM is the number that goes into business forecasts. Investors are wary of a huge TAM without a realistic SAM and SOM."},"2":{"0":"Needing everything isn't positioning. Positioning picks who you serve and on which axis you win; covering it all blurs that.","2":"Competing on cheapest is a race to the bottom. Positioning should rest on value: simple, fast and Remote-first.","3":"Different from everyone says nothing specific. Positioning names the audience, the need and the rival you beat."},"3":{"0":"Market penetration means selling more to the same market, startups. Enterprise is a new market for Velox.","2":"Velox isn't building a new product. It adds SSO, permissions and Audit Logs to the existing one to reach Enterprise.","3":"Diversification is a new product in a new market, like an AI assistant. Enterprise Readiness keeps the existing product."},"4":{"0":"Chasing everything means fighting giants on their turf. ClickUp tries to do it all and suffers from performance problems.","2":"Big players don't make a niche hopeless. Focus lets a company win its niche, as Linear does with engineering teams.","3":"There's no need to flee the category. Velox has a niche, distributed teams, where it can be best in class."},"5":{"0":"Those are financial metrics of one company. Porter looks at the industry: rivals, new entrants, substitutes, suppliers, buyers.","2":"Those are product development stages. The five forces analyze the competitive pressure on a whole industry's profitability.","3":"Those are Rogers' adoption groups. Porter's five forces describe competition in an industry, not types of customers."},"6":{"0":"Bottom-up counts potential customers, not current ones. Multiplying today's paying customers gives revenue, not market size.","2":"Neither method starts from company targets or sales reps. TAM sizes the market, not the internal forecast.","3":"Interviews and surveys are research methods. Top-down starts from market reports; Bottom-up counts potential customers times price."},"7":{"0":"Strengths is the S in SWOT: the internal strengths within the organization's control.","1":"Weaknesses is the W in SWOT: internal weaknesses, such as Velox's DX lagging behind Linear's.","3":"Threats is the T in SWOT: external threats from the business environment, outside the organization's control."},"8":{"0":"$50B is far above the $1B+ investors look for. The problem is the reverse: size says nothing about what you can win.","2":"Experienced investors see a huge TAM without a realistic SAM and SOM as a red flag, not a reason to invest.","3":"TAM is still relevant: it measures the size of the opportunity. It just has to be narrowed to SAM and SOM."},"9":{"0":"Blind copying is a common mistake. First check whether it's a real need of your customers and fits the vision.","2":"Ignoring it misses a signal: customers are asking for it. The move is to understand why, not to look away.","3":"A price cut doesn't answer the need, and competing on price is a race to the bottom. First understand what customers want."},"10":{"0":"Cost Leadership means being the cheapest, like Walmart or Ryanair. Choosing a single niche is a different strategy.","1":"Differentiation stands out across the broad market, like Apple. Narrowing to a single niche is Focus.","3":"Stuck in the Middle is the absence of a choice. Focusing only on engineering teams is a clear strategic choice."},"11":{"0":"Lower unit cost with size is Economies of Scale, like AWS. Network Effects is about value rising with users.","2":"Brand is a separate moat, like Apple's identity. Network Effects grows with the number of users, like WhatsApp.","3":"Switching Costs are a separate moat, like Salesforce. Network Effects means more users make the product worth more."},"12":{"0":"Cirque du Soleil didn't win by being cheaper. It dropped the animals, added story and music, and reached a new audience.","1":"Red Ocean means fighting rivals over an existing market. Cirque du Soleil stepped out of that fight into a new category.","3":"It didn't just stand out among circuses; it created a whole new category with no direct competitors."},"13":{"0":"A small niche is the Focus strategy, a valid choice. Stuck in the Middle is failing to choose any strategy at all.","2":"Porter's trap isn't about moving from B2C to B2B. It's having no clear edge: not cheapest, not distinct, not focused.","3":"Copying the leaders is a different mistake. Stuck in the Middle means choosing none of the three strategies."},"14":{"0":"Budget doesn't define differentiation. The test is whether customers pay more for it and rivals struggle to copy it.","2":"Differentiation can be experience: Linear wins on speed and UX. What counts is that customers will pay for it.","3":"Price level isn't the test. Differentiation is what customers value enough to pay for, and rivals struggle to copy."}},"6":{"0":{"0":"Mobile can wait a quarter. Dropping Activation leaves a $500K yearly loss that hits every new user.","1":"Notifications are an improvement, not a game-changer. Enterprise brings $250K+ right away and opens a new market.","3":"Neither moves the needle most: mobile can wait a quarter and notifications are polish. The money sits in Enterprise and Activation."},"1":{"0":"More features doesn't name the real blocker. 94% retention proves the product works; most sign-ups just never reach it.","2":"Blaming strong rivals dodges the challenge. The diagnosis must name what blocks growth: 45% Activation.","3":"More sign-ups would pour into the same leak: 55% never reach value. The blocker is Activation, not traffic."},"2":{"0":"Doing everything more slowly is still not choosing. Focus means giving things up, like mobile this quarter.","2":"Copying competitors is reacting, not choosing. Focus means picking what you'll excel at and dropping the rest.","3":"Listening only to big customers is a bias, not a strategic choice. Focus is giving up the good for the excellent."},"3":{"0":"A coefficient above 1 means each user brings more than one new user: growth that feeds itself, not slow growth.","2":"Above 1, organic growth feeds itself without depending on paid marketing.","3":"1.2 is above the bar of 1, which marks growth that feeds itself. The stage counts it as a strong PMF sign."},"4":{"0":"The Sean Ellis Test doesn't measure payment. It asks how disappointed users would be if the product shut down.","2":"A sign-up count says nothing about need. The test counts how many would be very disappointed without the product.","3":"Profit isn't the test. Sean Ellis measures attachment: 40%+ very disappointed if the product shut down."},"5":{"0":"Rumelt separates them: reaching 60% Activation is a goal, while focusing on onboarding is a strategy, the how.","2":"The difference isn't time span. A goal is a desired result; a strategy is the path: diagnosis, policy and actions.","3":"Rumelt's distinction isn't about audience. A goal says what, a strategy says how, and both matter across the org."},"6":{"0":"Desirable, Feasible and Viable are the three product pillars, not Biddle's DHM.","1":"That isn't what DHM stands for. In Biddle's framework the letters are Delight, Hard to Copy and Margin-enhancing.","3":"Design, Handoff, Monitoring describes a workflow. Biddle's DHM tests initiatives: Delight, Hard to Copy, Margin-enhancing."},"7":{"0":"Cagan's split isn't about team size or seniority. It's about what the team gets: a feature list or a problem to solve.","2":"Empowered Teams still have a PM; the Product Trio leads discovery together. The difference is a problem vs a feature list.","3":"The methodology isn't the point. A Feature Team can run two-week sprints and still just execute a feature list."},"8":{"0":"More features would make it worse. The KR counts output, and the real question is what changed for users.","2":"It looks measurable, but it counts features, not results. And improve the product is too vague to guide anyone.","3":"More KRs won't fix it. The problem is their kind: Output measures instead of Outcome, under a vague Objective."},"9":{"0":"Vision and budget aren't a strategy. Rumelt's Kernel needs a diagnosis, a guiding policy and coherent actions.","2":"A list of goals and KPIs is goals mistaken for strategy. It says what you want, not how you'll get there.","3":"Being best at everything avoids choosing. When everything is top priority, nothing is."},"10":{"0":"Developers don't pick Linear for price but for DX. Undercutting it doesn't fix that gap.","2":"Chasing low cost and differentiation together is the Stuck in the Middle trap: you fail at both.","3":"Copying Linear means fighting on its strongest ground, DX. Velox's edge is its own niche: distributed teams."},"11":{"0":"The same audience and features at a lower price is a Red Ocean: fighting over an existing market.","2":"Waiting for rivals to fail isn't a strategy. Blue Ocean means creating a new market yourself.","3":"Adding everything is the opposite of Blue Ocean, which asks what to eliminate and reduce, not only what to add."},"12":{"0":"Economies of Scale lower the cost per unit. Here what grows is the value for each user as teammates join.","1":"A brand builds trust and preference, but users bringing in colleagues because value grows is Network Effects.","3":"Switching costs stop users from leaving. The tell here is different: value that grows with each new teammate."},"13":{"0":"Experienced investors aren't won over by TAM alone; without SAM and SOM it signals a weak grasp of the market.","2":"Investors do care about market size, as long as it's realistic: SOM shows how much you can actually capture.","3":"SAM and SOM matter to startups too: SOM shows what you can realistically capture with existing resources."},"14":{"0":"Rushing to copy is an emotional response. First check whether your customers need it and whether it fits the strategy.","2":"Not every competitor move is a direct threat. A strategic response asks what your own customers need.","3":"Watching competitors is part of the job; the mistake is copying blindly. Look, then decide by your strategy."},"15":{"0":"A feature list is a backlog, not a vision. A vision describes the problem and the future state the product aims for.","1":"A revenue target like $10M is a result that comes if the vision comes true, not the vision itself.","3":"Architecture is how you build, and users don't buy architecture. A vision starts from the human problem."},"16":{"0":"Waiting for instructions is how a Feature Team acts. An empowered team owns the problem and proposes the next direction.","1":"Past investment doesn't make a failing idea work. Continuing only spends more on something shown not to work.","3":"Hiding failure kills the learning. Cagan's test is the opposite: saying openly we tested it and it doesn't work."},"17":{"0":"Discovery doesn't finish first; that's Waterfall in disguise. The tracks run at the same time, 1-2 sprints apart.","2":"Building first and testing later is exactly what Discovery prevents: it validates ideas before Delivery builds them.","3":"The tracks don't take turns; they run in the same week, with Discovery working 1-2 sprints ahead."},"18":{"0":"Counting features shipped is Output. A team that ships 10 features nobody uses has failed.","2":"Perfection before release isn't the principle. Outcome over Output is about measuring impact, not polish.","3":"Code quality versus speed is a different trade-off. The principle is about measuring whether the problem got solved."},"19":{"0":"Early profit alone doesn't prove fit. Rachitsky looks for signals like word of mouth that nobody asked for.","1":"A sign-up count doesn't show need. Unprompted recommendations show the product truly solves a problem.","3":"An NPS score alone doesn't prove fit. Recommendations nobody asked for are a stronger sign, per Rachitsky."}},"7":{"0":{"1":"240 multiplies only Reach by Impact, and even that math is off: it skips Confidence and the division by Effort.","2":"Confidence is multiplied, not divided: it shrinks the score by how sure you are, so 200 cannot come out.","3":"800 forgets to divide by Effort. Without that, an expensive feature and a cheap one look the same."},"1":{"0":"Waiting for the quarter to end means paying the 30% slowdown all quarter: about 3.6 lost weeks, more than the 2-week fix.","2":"The product works, but every feature takes 30% longer. Debt that slows the team is a business cost, not a spare-time chore.","3":"Debt that slows every feature delays the whole Roadmap. The stage frames it as a business problem the PM must prioritize."},"2":{"0":"A Must-have is something users leave without. Nobody leaves a product because it lacks Dark Mode.","1":"Performance grows the more you invest, like speed. Dark Mode is on or off; more of it adds no satisfaction.","3":"Kano exists exactly for this: you classify by how most users react, even when tastes differ."},"3":{"0":"Building it right away skips the trade-off: $100K now comes at the expense of SSO, which brings in $150K+ every week.","2":"Ignoring the CEO closes the conversation. RICE is a starting point for discussion, not a verdict to hide behind.","3":"Pushing it to next quarter with no discussion is still a blind commitment, with no trade-off for the CEO to weigh."},"4":{"0":"It's the other way around: RICE calculates a numerical score, and MoSCoW sorts requirements into four categories.","2":"They serve different needs: RICE for precise comparison, MoSCoW for aligning scope with stakeholders in plain language.","3":"The stage picks a model by situation, not company size: RICE for quarterly roadmaps, MoSCoW for stakeholder alignment."},"5":{"0":"User Story detail belongs to Now. Later is six months out and beyond, where uncertainty is too high for precise dates.","2":"Detail drops as the horizon grows: Now detailed, Next medium, Later general. Equal detail would fake a certainty nobody has.","3":"Later is part of the structure: it holds high-level themes for six months and beyond, more intent than promise."},"6":{"0":"ICE multiplies its three components, it doesn't add them: 8+6+4 gives 18, but 8×6×4 gives 192.","1":"Ease is one of the three factors in ICE. Dropping it leaves 8×6=48 and ignores how easy the feature is to build.","3":"ICE has no step that halves the result. A low Ease already lowers the score through the multiplication itself."},"7":{"0":"That is development cost. Cost of Delay measures what the business loses for each month the feature is late.","2":"That describes a team speed metric. Cost of Delay is about the business loss from waiting, not the pace of work.","3":"Maintenance cost belongs to a feature that already exists. Cost of Delay prices the loss of not having it yet."},"8":{"0":"Giving in to seniority swaps prioritization for the loudest voice, the very contest prioritization models exist to prevent.","2":"Ignoring a senior stakeholder breaks trust. The stage calls for transparency: show the scores and explain the order.","3":"Squeezing it in without moving anything overloads the sprint. Every yes is a quiet no to something else, said or not."},"9":{"0":"They differ in horizon and detail: the Roadmap shows where and why, the Backlog holds what the team does today.","2":"The Roadmap is a communication tool for many audiences: leadership, the team and customers each get their own view.","3":"It runs the other way: the Roadmap translates strategy into a plan, and the detailed Backlog tasks follow from it."},"10":{"0":"High Reach alone doesn't decide. A 50% Confidence halves the score, a sign the evidence is still thin.","2":"Confidence is a multiplier in RICE: 50% cuts the score in half, so it changes the result directly.","3":"Low Confidence lowers the score; it doesn't erase the feature. The move is to gather data and raise the Confidence."},"11":{"0":"That name has nothing to do with prioritization. CD3 is a formula: Cost of Delay divided by Duration, not a discovery method.","1":"Continuous discovery is a different idea. CD3 is a score: the cost of delay divided by development time.","3":"CD3 is not a dashboard. It divides the monthly cost of delay by the time it takes to build, to set an order."},"12":{"0":"The model does weigh cost: in the stage's example Effort is a 15% criterion, inverted so less effort earns more points.","2":"The stage recommends Weighted Scoring exactly when several stakeholders have conflicting requirements, not only in startups.","3":"The stage never calls the method outdated. Its limit is the inputs: weights and scores set by human estimation."},"13":{"0":"Showing the framework and data is the recommended move: it turns a clash of opinions into a talk about assumptions.","1":"Letting the stakeholder feel like a partner is part of the right approach: it lowers resistance instead of creating it.","3":"Documenting what was decided and why is good practice: the decision stays transparent after the meeting ends."},"14":{"0":"A Quick Win is the opposite corner: high value and low effort, the work you do first.","1":"A Big Bet has high value alongside the high effort, which is why it's worth planning carefully.","2":"A Fill-in is low value but also low effort, so it can fill free time. With high effort it becomes a Time Sink."}},"8":{"0":{"0":"Overtime hides the problem: SSO alone fills the whole Velocity, leaving no room for bugs. Plan to real capacity.","2":"Dropping SSO gives up a strategic item. Breaking it into stages keeps it moving within the team's Velocity.","3":"Velocity is the team's average pace, and planning rests on it. Splitting SSO into stages fits it into the plan with no temporary boost."},"1":{"0":"Dropping everything for Dark Mode mid-sprint hurts the strategic SSO. The CEO deserves the trade-off, not blind execution.","2":"Ignoring the CEO closes the conversation. The right move explains the cost and offers the next sprint.","3":"Overnight extra work overloads a full sprint and hides the real trade-off: Dark Mode now means delaying SSO."},"2":{"0":"Hunting down who owns each missed task turns the retro into blame. The goal is continuous improvement.","2":"Looking for someone to blame kills honesty. A retro asks what to change, not who is at fault.","3":"Focusing on the CEO points outward. A retro looks at what the team itself can change in the next sprint."},"3":{"0":"This is a justification with no user and no value. A User Story says who wants what, and why.","2":"Priority and deadline are planning data, not a story. It says nothing about who the user is or what value they get.","3":"That's a technical task spec. A User Story describes the user and the value, not SAML and Story Points."},"4":{"0":"Sprints don't suit support, which fits Kanban's continuous flow. The problem is too much work in progress at once.","2":"More people would only open more tickets in parallel. The fix is finishing what's started before taking new work.","3":"Fewer hours don't shrink the 14 open tickets. Multitasking is the drain, and a WIP Limit is what addresses it."},"5":{"0":"Acceptance Criteria define what one Story does. The dispute here is about the shared quality bar: tests and Code Review.","2":"A Sprint Goal says what the sprint aims for. It doesn't settle whether code without tests counts as done.","3":"Velocity is about how much to commit to. Here the question is what done means, which a DoD defines for every item."},"6":{"0":"This one is true: Velocity is a planning tool that helps estimate how much work the team can take on.","1":"This one is true: Velocity is the team's average of completed Story Points per sprint over time.","3":"This one is true: a stable Velocity across several sprints is a sign of a mature team."},"7":{"0":"OKR sets a goal and its measures, but doesn't map user problems to solutions and tests. That's exactly what Michal needs.","2":"A Roadmap runs from vision down to the Backlog. It doesn't link the problems research surfaced to solutions and experiments.","3":"A Story Map lays out the flow of a solution already chosen. Michal wants to step back from the Wizard to the problems."},"8":{"0":"The sprint length isn't the issue: the team picked too much for it. Plan by Velocity, not by hope.","1":"Skill isn't the likely cause. Over-commitment in planning is the most common problem.","3":"Surprises happen, which is why you add a buffer. Missing 40% points to over-commitment, not bad luck."},"9":{"0":"Dual-Track isn't about fewer hours. Its value is discovering what to build while delivering what was validated.","2":"Dual-Track doesn't replace Scrum. Delivery can still run in sprints, with Discovery running alongside it.","3":"The PM is central: the PM and designer drive Discovery. The tracks run in parallel, not without a PM."},"10":{"0":"Ceremonies alone aren't Agile. Refusing to change direction breaks a core value: responding to change over following a plan.","1":"Agile is defined by values, not rituals. Stand-ups and sprints with no openness to change are Waterfall in disguise.","3":"A certified Scrum Master isn't the point. Ceremonies with no response to change aren't Agile either way."},"11":{"0":"Waterfall in Disguise means all requirements are written upfront with no discovery. The symptom here is that nobody measures.","2":"More features are the disease, not the cure. The problem is that nobody measures whether features work.","3":"A DoD covers quality before release. The gap here is after launch: nobody checks whether the feature hit its goal."},"12":{"0":"100% Discovery means shipping nothing. Research has to run alongside building, not instead of it.","1":"The rule of thumb is about 70% Delivery and 30% Discovery, not an even split between the tracks.","3":"Discovery once a quarter is far too rare. The key point is that it happens every week."},"13":{"0":"Committing to the highest Velocity to impress leads to over-commitment. Planning should rest on the real average.","1":"Dictating tasks and estimates erases the team's part: the how and the how much. It hurts realism and buy-in.","3":"Stuffing in the whole backlog ignores capacity. Planning picks what fits within the Velocity."},"14":{"0":"Retros and Sprint Planning are Scrum ceremonies. Continuous Discovery is about weekly contact with customers.","2":"Shipping features is Delivery. Continuous Discovery is research: interviews, prototype tests and experiments.","3":"Writing stories is Backlog work. Continuous Discovery means touching customers every week through small research."}},"9":{"0":{"0":"Looks aren't the point. Out of Scope settles in advance what won't be built, so no one demands it midway.","2":"It's not a CEO formality. Out of Scope is the section that saves the most arguments about scope.","3":"Even with ample resources it matters: it stops the natural urge to keep adding, and settles disputes early."},"1":{"1":"Work well and load fast can't be tested. Good AC are specific and measurable, like 3 Templates and 3 seconds.","2":"Satisfaction and NPS are outcomes, not acceptance tests. AC check concrete behavior the team can verify.","3":"This is a build plan with vague words like modern and smooth. AC state a testable condition: Given, When, Then."},"2":{"0":"Saving money is a side effect. The point is the MVP: test the core quickly, learn, then expand.","2":"Developer comfort isn't the goal. The simplest version lets you learn fast and cut risk before investing more.","3":"Finding out while building is costly. Asking up front may show that Templates alone are enough as an MVP."},"3":{"0":"Templates chosen is a Leading Indicator that helps explain why. Activation is the Outcome that moves the business.","2":"Skip clicks help diagnose the flow, but they're a supporting signal. The goal Onboarding v2 serves is Activation.","3":"Time on page can rise for bad reasons, like confusion. The Outcome that matters is Activation, from 45% to 60%."},"4":{"0":"The difference isn't language or audience location. The MRD covers why and for whom; the PRD covers what to build.","2":"The MRD comes first and is no technical appendix. It defines the market opportunity the PRD is built on.","3":"This swaps the two: the MRD is the market opportunity, why and for whom, and the PRD is the solution, what to build."},"5":{"0":"This is a good question: the cost of not solving tests whether the problem is real and significant.","2":"This is a good question: it checks that the solution was chosen against alternatives, not by default.","3":"This is a good question: what happens if we wait 6 months tests strategic urgency, the why now."},"6":{"0":"This describes a solution with design details, but no user and no value. A User Story starts from who and why.","2":"Priority and timing are planning data. It's missing who the user is and what value skipping gives them.","3":"Close, but it's missing parts of the format: a specific user and the so that, the value of skipping."},"7":{"0":"The PRD can't come before the MRD: you define the solution only after understanding why and for whom.","1":"Starting with the Tech Spec means deciding how to build before knowing why or what. The MRD comes first.","3":"This runs backwards: sprint stories come last, after the why, the what, the look and the how are defined."},"8":{"0":"Story Points don't go inside the story sentence. The issue is the content: a dictated solution and no value.","2":"It breaks the format: there's no so that, and it dictates a design, a blue button, instead of a need.","3":"A personal name isn't needed; a role is enough. What's missing is the need and the value behind it."},"9":{"0":"They differ in scope: AC belong to one Story, while a DoD is a standard every task must meet.","2":"Both serve the whole team. The difference is scope: AC per Story, a DoD for every piece of work.","3":"There's no fixed order between them. AC describe what a Story does; a DoD says when any work is truly finished."},"10":{"0":"Detail doesn't guarantee understanding when no one reads it. A lean 2-3 pages answers the key questions.","2":"Longer is worse: the stage puts a good PRD at 2-3 precise pages, and even 20 go unread.","3":"Translation isn't the issue. The length is: 20 pages means the document doesn't get read."},"11":{"0":"The user's action is the When. Given describes the starting state before any action.","1":"The expected result is the Then. Given is the initial state the scenario starts from.","3":"Given isn't a name or a role. It's the state of the system or user before the action begins."},"12":{"0":"Timing comes later. First ask whether the problem is real; otherwise even a fast build is waste.","2":"Technology is a how question for later. First check that the problem is real and worth solving.","3":"Staffing is an execution question. If the problem isn't real, no number of developers makes it worth building."},"13":{"0":"Cost alone shows only the expense. Management also needs expected revenue, ROI and risk scenarios.","2":"Features with AC belong in the PRD. A Business Case argues money: cost, revenue, ROI and scenarios.","3":"A competitor comparison shows the market exists, not that this investment pays off. That takes cost, revenue and ROI."},"14":{"0":"Effort estimates rest on the PRD and the team, not the MRD. The MRD exists to understand the opportunity.","2":"The MRD holds no technical requirements; those live in the Tech Spec. The MRD covers why and for whom.","3":"The stage ties the MRD to the market, not to regulation. Its job is making sure the problem is worth solving."}},"10":{"0":{"0":"Missing the target by 2pp doesn't erase a +13pp gain at 99% significance. Perfect is the enemy of good.","2":"Waiting a month for the exact target delays a proven win. Ship now and keep improving.","3":"Starting over throws away a version that already works: +13pp with 99% confidence."},"1":{"0":"Removing the step loses its value: users who invite show 40% higher Retention. Fix the step, don't cut it.","1":"Forcing an invite turns a drop-off point into a wall. Better to show its value and make inviting easier.","3":"The data already shows the step's value, +40% Retention, and its cost, 25% drop-off. That's enough to act."},"2":{"0":"That's about experiment design. The principle is about the decision: check segments before killing a feature.","2":"It's the reverse: you segment to find hidden wins, like +11pp in small teams under a +2.6pp overall result.","3":"Defining a target segment is good practice, but the principle is about analyzing results before discarding a feature."},"3":{"0":"It's a product experiment, not facilities management. The door is a button or link to a feature that doesn't exist yet.","2":"It's not a security test. It measures demand: how many click on a feature that hasn't been built.","3":"An A/B test on existing buttons tweaks the interface. A Fake Door tests demand for a feature not yet built."},"4":{"0":"There's no metric and no timeframe, so you can't tell if it was validated. Research and competitors aren't a test.","2":"It starts well, but because we got requests isn't a measure. It needs a metric, from X to Y, and by when.","3":"Copying competitors isn't a hypothesis, and happy customers isn't measurable. It needs a metric and a deadline."},"5":{"0":"There is a difference: in Concierge the customer knows it's manual; only in Wizard of Oz is it hidden.","2":"Wizard of Oz isn't automated; a person works behind the scenes. The difference is whether the user knows.","3":"Wizard of Oz needs no automated back end; it only looks automated. That's how it tests demand cheaply."},"6":{"0":"Lean Startup starts small: a minimal MVP, not a complete product. Waiting for profits slows the learning.","2":"A business plan for investors isn't the loop. It builds an MVP, measures real usage and decides Pivot or Persevere.","3":"The loop is about learning on the product, not building a team. Build an MVP, measure real metrics, then decide."},"7":{"0":"The threshold is reversed: significance means a p-value below 0.05. At 0.03 the result is significant.","2":"The p-value isn't the size of the improvement. It says how likely a difference this large would be by chance.","3":"The p-value doesn't describe a share of users. It's the chance of a result this extreme if there were no real difference."},"8":{"0":"Sign-ups show interest, but only 15 of 200 came back, 7.5%. The metric that matters is who got value and returned.","2":"More sign-ups would fill the same leaky bucket. The problem is that users don't come back.","3":"7.5% returning on day two is a red flag. The next step is to interview the 185 who didn't return."},"9":{"1":"The terms differ: a Pivot changes direction, like B2C to B2B; an Iteration improves within the same direction.","2":"It's the other way around: an Iteration is a small step, a Pivot is a fundamental change of direction.","3":"Both rest on learning, but they differ in scope: a strategic turn versus a gradual improvement."},"10":{"0":"A weekly A/B test on every feature isn't Torres's minimum. Her baseline is one 30-minute customer interview.","2":"A 100-user survey isn't the habit she describes. The minimum is one 30-minute interview a week.","3":"A PRD is a delivery document, not discovery. Torres's minimum is talking with one customer every week."},"11":{"0":"Hiding that a person does the work is Wizard of Oz. In Concierge the customer knows the service is manual.","2":"Concierge is about learning the process up close before automating. Testing price alone doesn't need that.","3":"Concierge comes before automation. If the product is already built and automated, that stage is past."},"12":{"0":"Whether users want it and will pay is Value. Feasibility asks whether it can be built on time and on budget.","2":"Using the interface without help is Usability. Feasibility is about whether it can be built technically.","3":"Enough revenue to justify the investment is Viability. Feasibility asks whether it can be built at all."},"13":{"0":"Developer curiosity isn't learning about customers. A Pivot follows data that disproves the hypothesis.","2":"A new competitor alone doesn't disprove your hypothesis. Pivot when your own data shows the direction is failing.","3":"A Pivot without data is a guess. It should follow evidence that the hypothesis was disproven."},"14":{"0":"Cost isn't the issue with a short test. The risk is missing weekly usage patterns, including the weekend.","2":"The opposite risk is real: a Novelty Effect, users reacting because it's new. A short test can't tell the difference.","3":"Internal policy isn't the point. A short test misses a full weekly cycle and can be skewed by novelty."}},"11":{"0":{"0":"These KRs are actions, not outcomes. Running a test or redesigning a screen says nothing about whether things improved.","2":"100% and zero drop-off are unrealistic. A good OKR is ambitious yet reachable; around 70% counts as success.","3":"Shipping features and tooltips is Output. A KR should measure an Outcome, such as Activation."},"1":{"0":"Size isn't the issue. Even a million sign-ups would be vanity if they don't show who is active or paying.","2":"The number may be perfectly accurate. It's vanity because it doesn't reflect value: activity or payment.","3":"Being large isn't the problem either. It's vanity because a total count hides how many are active and paying."},"2":{"0":"Burnout isn't what Goodhart warns about. The risk is gaming the number: closing easy bugs to reach 10.","2":"Goodhart's Law is exactly the risk: once a count becomes a target, people produce the number, not the value.","3":"The incentive runs the other way: to reach 10 quickly, the team picks easy bugs, not critical ones."},"3":{"0":"A metric chosen to impress is often a vanity metric. A North Star reflects the value users actually get.","2":"Total downloads is a vanity number that says nothing about ongoing value. A North Star reflects everyday value.","3":"A North Star is stable and aligns every team. A metric that changes each quarter can't point everyone the same way."},"4":{"0":"Referral comes after Retention: users recommend once they keep coming back and getting value.","1":"Activation can't come before Acquisition: users must first arrive before they can experience value.","3":"Awareness isn't part of AARRR, and Referral is missing. The five are Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue."},"5":{"0":"That's an imitation strategy, not the bias. Survivorship Bias is drawing conclusions only from those who stayed.","2":"Favoring veteran users is a choice, not this bias. The bias is ignoring those who left when analyzing.","3":"Focusing on payers is a business choice. Survivorship Bias means missing why others left."},"6":{"0":"They serve different purposes: a KPI asks how we're performing, an OKR asks where we're aiming.","2":"Neither depends on team size. A KPI is an ongoing metric; an OKR is a time-bound objective, usually quarterly.","3":"A KPI is ongoing, not strictly monthly, and an OKR is usually quarterly, not annual."},"7":{"0":"Strong correlation still doesn't prove cause. Motivated users might both watch the tutorial and use the product more.","2":"They differ: correlation is moving together, causation is one driving the other. Only a controlled experiment proves it.","3":"It's very practical: a feature built on a false link wastes development. That's what A/B tests are for."},"8":{"0":"The metrics don't always move together. If Activation is loosely defined, it can rise without real value or revenue.","2":"They are connected: every metric should ultimately link to Revenue. The gap is a signal worth investigating.","3":"Activation matters, but only when defined as real value. Here the definition may count users who merely try it."},"9":{"0":"Three days may lack sample size and significance, and a Novelty Effect can inflate early results.","2":"There's no fixed one-month rule. What matters is sample size, a p-value below 0.05 and a full weekly cycle.","3":"A permanent 50/50 split isn't a decision. First confirm the result is significant, then ship or drop it."},"10":{"1":"Both matter: Leading lets you act early, Lagging confirms what happened. A smart PM tracks both.","2":"They measure different things: sign-ups and Activation predict, while revenue and Churn report what already happened.","3":"Neither is tied to B2C or B2B. The difference is timing: predicting the future versus reflecting the past."},"11":{"0":"Lines of code is another output measure, and it can be gamed too. A counter-metric should guard quality.","2":"Meeting count doesn't check what speed might break. The counterweight to speed is quality: bugs and incidents.","3":"Slack messages don't balance speed. The risk of chasing Velocity is buggy code, so you measure quality."},"12":{"0":"Activation Rate is the output being targeted, not an input. Inputs are what the team directly controls.","2":"Revenue is an output, and a lagging one. Inputs are actions the team controls, such as experiments.","3":"Day-30 Retention is an output metric. The team can't move it directly; it moves inputs like experiments."},"13":{"0":"Numbers show what is happening, not why. Without context and users it's easy to draw the wrong conclusion.","2":"Combining data with intuition and users is exactly the Data-Informed approach Yang recommends, not the risk.","3":"Team size isn't Yang's concern. The risk is deciding by numbers alone without understanding why."},"14":{"0":"Lying isn't the concern. Hitting 100% every time simply shows the goals didn't stretch the team.","2":"There's no rule about round values. The point is that 70% counts as good, so 100% signals low ambition.","3":"Burnout isn't the reason. OKRs are meant to stretch the team; a steady 100% means they didn't."}},"12":{"0":{"0":"Error Prevention stops mistakes before they happen, like a confirm before deleting. Priority colors inform, they don't prevent.","2":"Flexibility means shortcuts for experienced users. Fixed priority levels show status at a glance instead.","3":"Help is in-context guidance, like a tip by a field. Three color-coded levels need no explanation: they make status visible."},"1":{"0":"This dictates a visual detail, a color, instead of a need. Product feedback says what the user needs and why.","2":"Not pretty is vague and not actionable. Good feedback names a need and its context.","3":"Cool animations are a taste-driven solution with no user need behind them. Feedback should start from the need."},"2":{"0":"More notifications sent isn't success; it could just mean more noise. Measure what users do with them.","1":"The designer's satisfaction isn't a product metric. Success shows in user behavior: complaints, clicks and NPS.","3":"Visual fit is a design question. Success is measured by complaints, Click Rate and NPS together."},"3":{"0":"This runs backwards: the Prototype is the most detailed and costly stage, so it comes last, not first.","2":"Starting from a Mockup skips the cheap stages. Fidelity climbs step by step: Sketch first, Prototype last.","3":"The Sketch is the roughest, cheapest stage and comes first. Here it sits third, after the Prototype."},"4":{"0":"That's the opposite of the heuristic: forcing users to memorize commands is exactly what it aims to avoid.","2":"Auto-filling forms is a different convenience. Recognition over Recall is about showing options instead of forcing recall.","3":"There's no memory test in Nielsen's list. The principle reduces memory load by showing the options."},"5":{"0":"This is good feedback: it names a problem, the CTA competing for attention, and leaves the fix to the designer.","1":"This is good feedback: it sets a measurable need, understanding within 3 seconds, without dictating a design.","3":"This is good feedback: it states a need, one-click access, not how the button should look."},"6":{"0":"Empathize comes first, not last. You can't define the problem before understanding the users.","2":"Starting with ideas skips understanding. The process opens with Empathize and Define, then Ideate.","3":"Building a prototype first means building before you know the problem. Empathize opens the process."},"7":{"0":"A brand style guide covers marketing identity. A Design System is a product component library: buttons, forms, typography.","2":"Figma or Sketch are tools. A Design System is the shared set of components the team reuses across the product.","3":"Usability principles are a checklist for judging screens. A Design System is a library of reusable components."},"8":{"0":"Jakob's Law warns that users expect familiar patterns. Innovation should rest on evidence, not be approved on looks.","1":"Never changing anything blocks improvement. A usability test shows whether the new navigation works.","3":"The CEO's approval doesn't tell you whether users can navigate it. A test with 5 users does."},"9":{"0":"They differ: UX is the whole journey and how it feels, UI is the visual layer of buttons, colors and type.","2":"It's the other way around: UX is the broader experience, and UI is one layer within it.","3":"UX applies to every digital product, not just mobile apps: it's the experience of using any product."},"10":{"0":"Clear error messages help after the fact. Error Prevention prefers stopping the mistake before it happens.","2":"Hiding errors leaves users confused. The heuristic is about preventing mistakes, not concealing them.","3":"No restrictions at all invites mistakes. Error Prevention adds guards, like a disabled button until the form is valid."},"11":{"0":"Neither is the interactive stage; that's the Prototype. A Wireframe shows structure, a Mockup the final look.","2":"They're different stages: the Wireframe tests structure and flow, the Mockup tests look and feel.","3":"Paper comes even earlier, at the Sketch. The Wireframe comes before the Mockup, not after it."},"12":{"0":"Generating ideas before understanding why users struggle may solve the wrong problem. Empathize comes first.","1":"A prototype that moves the feature assumes location is the problem. Maybe the name is unclear; understand first.","3":"A test checks solutions you don't have yet. First learn from users why they struggle."},"13":{"0":"Taste, trends and animations stay with the designer. The PM checks business goals, technical constraints and user needs.","2":"Counting screens or file size says nothing about value. The filters are business, technical and user.","3":"Preferences and seniority aren't criteria. The PM asks if the design serves the business, can be built and solves the user's problem."},"14":{"0":"The stage notes that in B2B, enterprise customers often ask for an accessibility report, and without one a deal can stall.","2":"Accessibility isn't a niche: it's a product responsibility that broadens the user base.","3":"The stage puts accessibility into every feature's Acceptance Criteria, not as a separate task for later."}},"13":{"0":{"0":"Waiting until every bug is fixed means losing 3 deals with deadlines. A controlled launch with an ETA keeps them.","1":"A quiet launch to everyone, with no word on known bugs, risks trust with all. Transparency plus an ETA builds it instead.","3":"Hiding known bugs burns trust the moment customers hit them. Transparency and an ETA beat silence."},"1":{"0":"The price is per user, $15 a user, not per task created. Usage-based would charge by actual activity.","2":"A flat rate ignores user count, but here the price is per user, and it sits within three packages.","3":"Velox has paid tiers, Pro and Enterprise. Freemium with no upgrade contradicts a $15-per-user Enterprise plan."},"2":{"0":"QA with critical bugs closed is a core checklist item: you don't launch on known critical breakage.","1":"Analytics is on the checklist: without it you can't tell after launch whether the feature worked.","3":"A Rollback plan is on the checklist: if something breaks, you need a ready way back."},"3":{"0":"They differ in audience and risk: a Soft Launch tests stability on 5-10% of users, a Big Bang goes to everyone with PR.","2":"The stage doesn't rank them by cost. The choice depends on the level of risk and how ready the product is.","3":"A Soft Launch is quiet by nature and needs no extra advertising. It targets a small group to test stability."},"4":{"0":"Pricing by development cost is the opposite of value-based. The customer cares about value, not your costs.","1":"Competitor averages are competition-based pricing. Value-based starts from what the solution is worth to the customer.","3":"Work hours are a cost input. Value-based pricing asks what the customer will pay for the problem solved."},"5":{"0":"A free version is Freemium, a way to attract users. A Decoy is an option built to be rejected.","2":"A time-limited discount creates urgency, a different tactic. A Decoy works by comparison, like Medium making Large look cheap.","3":"Renaming changes perception through words. A Decoy adds an extra option that steers the choice by comparison."},"6":{"0":"Anchoring and the Decoy Effect are tactics for presenting prices, not research into what customers will pay.","1":"NPS and CSAT measure loyalty and satisfaction, not price. They don't reveal willingness to pay.","3":"Cost-plus and break-even start from your costs. Willingness to Pay research asks customers what the value is worth."},"7":{"0":"This swaps them: Freemium is a limited version free forever; a Free Trial is full access for a limited time.","2":"Freemium's basic tier is free, not discounted, and a Free Trial unlocks all features, not just some.","3":"Freemium relies on upgrades to premium, not necessarily ads. A Free Trial doesn't require paying upfront."},"8":{"0":"Product-Led means a free tier and self-serve, like Slack. A waitlist does the reverse: it limits access to build demand.","1":"Sales-Led runs on demos, pilots and annual contracts. Superhuman's waitlist and invites build demand another way.","3":"Usage-based is a pricing model, not a GTM motion. It doesn't describe how Superhuman built demand."},"9":{"0":"GTM is broader than advertising: it also covers audience, channels, message, pricing and timing.","2":"It's not just a name for ads. GTM connects product to customer: who, through which channels, what message, what price.","3":"Every product needs a way to reach customers, whatever the budget. An excellent product with poor GTM goes nowhere."},"10":{"0":"The rule isn't marketing versus product. It says most launch work, QA, support and Analytics, happens before launch day.","2":"It isn't a budget split. It's about timing: 90% of the work is preparation, so launch day itself runs smoothly.","3":"It's not about who hears in advance. It's about preparation: QA, trained support, Analytics and a Rollback plan."},"11":{"1":"They differ in size and purpose: a Soft Launch tests stability on a small group, a Beta gathers feedback from a larger one.","2":"Neither is tied to a platform. The difference is group size and purpose: stability versus feedback.","3":"This swaps them: the small group testing stability is the Soft Launch; a Beta is larger and gathers feedback."},"12":{"0":"Starting low and raising later is a penetration tactic, not Anchoring. Anchoring shows the high price first.","2":"Anchoring to development cost is cost-plus pricing. The anchor here is the first price the customer sees.","3":"A single price leaves no anchor to compare against. Anchoring relies on showing a high reference point first."},"13":{"0":"More packages don't add revenue when customers can't choose. Choice Paralysis can end with no purchase at all.","2":"The rule of thumb is 3-4 packages at most. Seven create Choice Paralysis, not an ideal.","3":"The main problem is the customer's choice, not maintenance. Too many options make it hard to decide."},"14":{"0":"Van Westendorp asks customers about price perception; it doesn't analyze competitors' prices.","2":"The method doesn't calculate cost. It asks four questions about when a price feels cheap or expensive.","3":"It's used before setting pricing, not to analyze conversion on existing pricing pages."}},"14":{"0":{"0":"10x isn't the bar. Above 3x is healthy for SaaS, and 3.8x even leaves room to invest more in acquisition.","2":"1x means a customer returns only what it cost to acquire them: break-even at best. Healthy SaaS aims for 3x or more.","3":"The ratio is central to unit economics: it shows what each acquisition dollar returns. Here, $3.8 for every $1."},"1":{"0":"Paid ads bring users, but don't change how many each user invites. The coefficient rises through the Invite Flow.","2":"Discounts don't make users invite their team. The viral coefficient rises when inviting is easier and clearer.","3":"More features don't raise invites per user. The central Growth Loop here is inviting the team."},"2":{"0":"The effect isn't linear. Bain & Company found 5% better retention lifts profit 25-95%, since keeping a customer is cheaper.","1":"It's far more than double: the research shows 25-95%, because an existing customer costs 5x less than a new one.","3":"Retention's effect is far from marginal: a 5% improvement can lift profit by 25-95%."},"3":{"0":"Below 1, each user brings fewer than one new user, so the spread fades. Fast organic growth needs K above 1.","2":"0.8 means referrals don't replace users one for one. Steady, self-feeding spread needs K above 1.","3":"The K-factor counts new users each user brings, not visitor conversion. 0.8 means growth that doesn't feed itself."},"4":{"1":"This isn't AARRR. McClure's pirate metrics follow the user: Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue.","2":"These terms describe a recovery process, not growth stages. AARRR tracks users from Acquisition through Revenue.","3":"These are attention terms, not McClure's stages. AARRR covers Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral and Revenue."},"5":{"0":"A North Star reflects value to users, not short-term finance. Monthly revenue is lagging and doesn't capture that value.","2":"Total sign-ups ignore activity, a vanity number. A North Star reflects the value users actually get, like Nights Booked.","3":"A survey score is periodic and indirect. A North Star is a behavioral measure of value, like Time Spent Listening."},"6":{"0":"An overall average mixes long-time users with new ones and hides the change. Compare cohorts by join month.","2":"A survey of active users hears only those who stayed. Cohort retention shows the behavior of everyone who joined.","3":"Sign-ups say nothing about whether new users stay. The Onboarding's effect shows in the March cohort's retention."},"7":{"0":"0.15 is low: only 15% of monthly users show up on a given day, below the typical 20-25%+ target.","2":"DAU/MAU measures stickiness, not growth. Even a fast-growing product can have users who rarely return.","3":"The ratio doesn't compare sign-ups to churn. It measures how many monthly users return on a given day."},"8":{"0":"With 80% churn, more acquisition fills a leaky bucket. First fix the reason people leave.","2":"Equal investment still pours money into a bucket losing 80%. Retention comes first here.","3":"It's not only about budget. With 80% churn, acquisition money drains away, so fixing retention comes first."},"9":{"1":"3 is invites per user, not the coefficient. Multiply by the 30% who join: 3×30% = 0.9.","2":"K is invites times conversion: 3×30% = 0.9, not 1.3. You don't add the user themselves.","3":"0.3 is only the conversion rate. K multiplies it by the 3 invites, so both levers matter."},"10":{"0":"NRR counts revenue, not customer numbers. Upsell from existing customers can push it above 100%.","2":"Low churn alone can't push NRR above 100%. Existing customers must also spend more, via Upsell or Cross-sell.","3":"NRR leaves out new customers. Above 100% means existing customers alone are growing revenue."},"11":{"0":"A strong Day 1 means little when Day 90 hits zero. Retention won't fix itself; nobody finds lasting value.","2":"Curves decline, but a healthy one flattens. One that drops to zero shows no group stays, so the budget would leak.","3":"Users who arrive and leave are a leaky bucket, even in a free app. The curve shows there's no lasting value."},"12":{"0":"They're different metrics: Activation is the first value moment, Retention is coming back over time.","2":"Order alone isn't the reason. Activation predicts Retention because a user who experienced value tends to return.","3":"The direction runs the other way: better Activation predicts better Retention, not the reverse."},"13":{"1":"This swaps them: published templates found through search are content; a direct invite to teammates is viral.","2":"Neither needs ad spend: both run through users. Templates draw people from search, invites bring them in directly.","3":"Both are organic, but the mechanism differs, so the metrics and the weak link to fix differ too."},"14":{"0":"Compounding from retention means less spend per dollar of revenue, not more. A retained customer keeps paying with no new acquisition cost.","2":"A fixed number of new sign-ups each month is linear growth. Compounding comes from customers who stay and expand.","3":"The stage doesn't tie compounding to sales headcount. It comes from retained customers who keep paying, refer and upsell."}},"15":{"0":{"0":"A CEO reads the first two lines. A 5-page doc with no bottom line does not tell them what decision is needed.","2":"A timeline is the whole journey, while the CEO needs only the decision, cost and risk. The bottom line gets buried at the end.","3":"Bring a problem with a recommended fix, not just a problem. An open question hands the CEO all the work, with no plan or trade-off."},"1":{"0":"The CEO asked is borrowed authority: it wins one argument and loses the team over time. Data and a trade-off are missing.","2":"A personal favor and emotional pressure give the VP Eng no context to decide. Influence is built on data and business impact.","3":"A day of work comes from somewhere. Hiding the trade-off creates a surprise later and hurts trust, instead of letting the VP Eng decide."},"2":{"0":"Without a review nobody learns why a known bug was not fixed before launch, so the same crisis can happen again.","2":"Hunting for a culprit is the opposite of a blameless post-mortem. The goal is to improve the process, not to find who to blame.","3":"Leadership needs a BLUF, not 10 pages. And the goal is process improvement with the team, like a stricter DoD, not a report."},"3":{"0":"Close management fits high power and high interest, like the CEO. A low-interest stakeholder does not need a daily update.","1":"They have a lot of power over the product, so they must not be surprised. Brief updates at key moments keep them satisfied.","3":"Weekly reports and monthly decks are flooding. A powerful but low-interest stakeholder needs only a brief update now and then."},"4":{"0":"In RACI, C is Consulted: someone you ask before a decision. Someone who helps do the work is really an R.","2":"Reach, Impact and Confidence belong to RICE-style prioritization. RACI ranks nothing; it defines who does what on each task.","3":"RACI does not track risks. It defines four roles for each task or decision to prevent confusion over ownership."},"5":{"0":"Challenging directly without caring is Obnoxious Aggression. Radical Candor needs both sides together.","2":"Caring without challenging is Ruinous Empathy. Softening or skipping criticism keeps from the person what they need to hear.","3":"Radical Candor joins personal care with direct challenge. Criticism in front of the whole team does not show care for the person."},"6":{"0":"Competing fits emergencies or a clear moral call, but it destroys relationships. A PM manages no one and needs them tomorrow.","1":"Avoiding suits unimportant matters or a cooling-off pause. As a default style, problems go unresolved and get worse.","3":"Accommodating fits when the matter matters only to the other side. A PM who always gives in loses respect instead of building trust."},"7":{"0":"You don't respect people is a judgment, not a description. Such feedback leads to a defensive argument, not a factual talk.","1":"This is SBI in name only: lately, in several meetings is not a specific situation, and looking down is interpretation, not observed behavior.","3":"Raising one person's behavior in front of the whole team turns feedback into public shaming. SBI is a factual talk about a specific recent moment."},"8":{"0":"When the CEO asks why to trust the data, gut feeling does not answer. Trust is built on transparency about how the data was gathered.","2":"Competitor examples dodge the question. The CEO asked about your data, so you show the method, sample and confidence level.","3":"Showing only the supporting data is the opposite of transparency. Once someone finds what was left out, trust erodes."},"9":{"0":"A PM is a leader without authority, and that includes the team dynamic. Ignoring it leaves the team member out of the conversation.","2":"Passing it to the manager skips the personal conversation. A private, empathetic talk is what creates psychological safety.","3":"Forcing someone to speak in front of everyone can deepen the withdrawal. Psychological safety is built in a private talk about what blocks them."},"10":{"0":"The range of possible agreement is ZOPA, not BATNA. BATNA is your alternative if the negotiation fails.","2":"Splitting the difference without exploring interests is a common mistake: both sides end up with half an orange nobody needs.","3":"Uncovering the interest behind a position is a different Getting to Yes tool. BATNA is about your alternative, not the other side's needs."},"11":{"1":"Technical questions are not the mistake; they are how you understand. Accepting at once and dropping the feature is the give-up mistake.","2":"Escalating to a manager is borrowed authority and turns a talk into a fight. Ask the developer what can be done and map alternatives together.","3":"An instant half-feature is splitting the difference without exploring interests. First ask what worries them and what can be built."},"12":{"0":"The problem is not tone but length and format: Slack is for up to 3 sentences, and a detailed document belongs in a Doc.","2":"Splitting into 5 messages still puts a whole document on Slack. That channel fits up to 3 sentences; the detail moves to a Doc.","3":"A BLUF helps, but 5 pages on Slack still won't be read. The channel rule is simple: don't send a Doc on Slack."},"13":{"0":"This is reversed: soft on form, with we language, and firm on content. Flexing on the professional position dissolves the decision itself.","2":"Meeting halfway once there is pushback gives up the content. The principle is to hold the position when data supports it.","3":"The principle does not hand the decision to whoever holds authority. A PM leads without authority and stays firm on a grounded position."},"14":{"0":"Managing Up is a short weekly update, not a detailed daily report. The goal is to prevent surprises, not to show effort.","2":"Bring a problem with a recommended fix, which shows ownership. Pushing every step to the manager earns less autonomy, not more.","3":"Managing Up is effective communication, not flattery or a race for credit. The proactive update builds trust and prevents surprises."}},"16":{"0":{"0":"A sprint is a period in which the team builds, not a meeting. Planning is only its opening ceremony, Sprint Planning.","2":"A sprint is a fixed time-box: it ends on its date even if not every item made it. It is not stretched until all is ready.","3":"A sprint lasts a month or less, usually two weeks, and yields a working Increment. A quarterly document is a plan, not a sprint."},"1":{"0":"The team decides how much it commits to, not the PO. A Story Point target imposed from outside leads to over-commitment.","2":"Breaking items into tasks and estimating them is the how, and that belongs to the development team, not the PM.","3":"The PO does not dictate technical solutions. A PM who dictates how to build hurts performance and the team's ownership."},"2":{"0":"A bug list is a shopping list of tickets. A good Sprint Goal describes one outcome for the user, not a list of items.","2":"A Sprint Goal gives one team direction for one sprint. Comparing teams is exactly the misuse of metrics the stage warns about.","3":"The end date is already fixed by the time-box. The Goal defines what to achieve, so items can be dropped without missing it."},"3":{"0":"15 minutes is the maximum for a Daily, not a problem. A short meeting is exactly what this ceremony is meant to be.","1":"That is the point of the Daily: the team coordinating among themselves. The PM listens and removes blockers, not runs it.","3":"Stand-up literally means meeting standing, to keep it short. That is part of the format, not an anti-pattern."},"4":{"0":"The Review shows the product to stakeholders in a live demo; it is no code review. The Retro discusses process, not next Stories.","2":"This is reversed: the Review shows the Increment to stakeholders, and in the Retro the team discusses its own process.","3":"The Retro is about the process, not rating each developer. Velocity is a planning tool, and measuring individuals with it is misuse."},"5":{"0":"Velocity is simple to compute: the average Story Points completed per sprint. The problem is what happens when it becomes a target.","2":"The stage does not tie Velocity to team size. It is a planning tool for any team; the problem is using it to rate performance.","3":"Velocity is the central planning tool for Scrum sprints, an agile tool. The problem is using it as a performance metric."},"6":{"0":"The 20% buffer is for urgent bugs and mutual support, not new requests. Slipping in one small thing mid-sprint breaks stability.","2":"Only the PO cancels a sprint, and only when the Sprint Goal is no longer relevant. A small stakeholder request does not justify it.","3":"A sprint is a fixed time-box and is not extended. Even Michal and Ido's team did not extend theirs; they pushed an item instead."},"7":{"0":"The DoD includes passing tests, so merged code alone is not enough. Pushing the tests to next sprint creates technical debt.","2":"The DoD is a whole-team agreement that applies to every item. The PO cannot waive clauses to close the sprint on time.","3":"There is no half done: an item meets the DoD or it is not done. Half points inflate the picture and hide the missing work."},"8":{"0":"A failed sprint is a chance to learn. Punishing and cutting Velocity sends blame instead of finding out why it happened.","2":"The stage stresses that a failed sprint is okay and what matters is understanding why. Looking for someone to blame does not answer that.","3":"After a failure the retro matters most: that is where you learn what went wrong. Skipping the retro is a named anti-pattern."},"9":{"0":"Cagan stresses empowered teams: the PM defines what and why, the team decides how. A PM who directs everyone kills ownership.","2":"The PM does not write the code. The how belongs to the developers; the PM's job is to define the problem and the value.","3":"The PO presents items at Planning, answers questions and approves the Increment at Review. Skipping ceremonies cuts them off."},"10":{"0":"Per the Scrum Guide the Scrum Master cannot cancel a sprint. A team missing targets does not cancel either; it learns in the retro.","2":"Management cannot cancel a sprint on its own. If a strategy shift makes the Sprint Goal irrelevant, the decision is the PO's.","3":"The Scrum Guide has no vote to cancel. Only the PO cancels, and only when the Sprint Goal has lost its relevance."},"11":{"0":"Real improvement is usually gradual. A 50% jump right after ranking by Velocity started points to inflation.","2":"A new tool does not explain a 50% jump right when ranking began. The timing points to Goodhart's Law: the measure became a target.","3":"Removing blockers helps gradually. A sharp jump right as ranking began, same team, is the classic sign of Story Point Inflation."},"12":{"0":"In Scope Creep the line rises instead of falling as items are added. Here it stays flat and then drops, a different pattern.","2":"Early Finish is a line that hits zero too early. Here it reaches zero only on the last day, after days with no progress.","3":"The ideal pattern is a smooth diagonal decline. Integration saved for the last day is the problem, not the plan."},"13":{"0":"Scrum of Scrums alone is not enough for 6 teams without a supporting framework, and it is advised 2-3 times a week, not once.","1":"SAFe is built for dozens to hundreds of teams and adds layers of process and roles. For 6 teams that is needless overhead.","3":"Kanban fits unpredictable work like support, not team count. LeSS shows that 6 teams can share Sprint Planning."},"14":{"0":"Even a one-week sprint needs a stable Sprint Goal and upfront planning, impossible with 15-20 urgent bugs arriving each week.","2":"SAFe is a framework for coordinating dozens of teams, not a fix for one support team's unpredictable work.","3":"Scrumban sounds tempting, but in practice it complicates both frameworks and suits teams in transition better."}},"17":{"0":{"0":"Ownership is reversed here: the Product Backlog belongs to the PO, and the Sprint Backlog belongs to the development team.","2":"The difference is scope, not item type. Both backlogs can hold features, bugs and technical debt.","3":"This is reversed: the Product Backlog is a living list that always changes, and the Sprint Backlog stays fixed during the sprint."},"1":{"0":"Given-When-Then is an Acceptance Criteria format. It tests behavior but does not say who the user is or why they need it.","2":"The system shall is the wording of a technical requirement. It names no specific user and no value they get.","3":"A breakdown into components and hours is the developers' Task, the how. Ido asked to move away from exactly that."},"2":{"0":"Bill Wake's INVEST lists qualities of a good Story. Input, Scope and Track are not in it, and T stands for Testable.","2":"A Story need not be innovative or visual. In INVEST, V is Valuable and S is Small, small enough for one sprint.","3":"In INVEST, T is Testable, not Technical. A technical Story with no user value is actually a problem under the V."},"3":{"0":"The parts are swapped: the click is the action and belongs in When. And fast and stable is a result nobody can test.","2":"They are happy is not a testable result. Then must describe something the system does that Ido can verify.","3":"A Then that describes implementation is over-specification: it takes the how from the developer and gives no testable result."},"4":{"0":"Fast, simple and secure cannot be measured. A good AC gives values you can check, like arrival time and expiry.","2":"Calling a service through an endpoint is an implementation detail, the how. An AC describes the behavior the user sees.","3":"Code Review and CI belong to the Definition of Done, which applies to every Story. An AC must be specific to this story."},"5":{"0":"A value-effort matrix ranks single items. It does not show the user's flow, so it cannot reveal what an end-to-end version lacks.","2":"WSJF sorts single items by score. It stays a flat list and does not show which Stories together form a full flow.","3":"A Burndown tracks progress inside a sprint. It does not help decide which Stories go into the MVP."},"6":{"0":"The development team breaks work into Tasks, not the Scrum Master. And the PM writes User Stories too, not only Epics.","2":"A technical solution in the AC is over-specification. The AC describes behavior, and the how stays with the developers.","3":"This is reversed: the PM writes the User Stories, the what and why. Tasks and their order are the team's work."},"7":{"0":"Detailing far-off items is waste: they will change or never be built. Pichler's rule is to detail only what comes next.","2":"Without ready items at the top, Planning stalls. The top 20% need AC before the sprint starts.","3":"Large Epics sit at the bottom of the iceberg as titles. You detail by closeness to the top, not by item size."},"8":{"0":"A technical ticket with no business context is a different anti-pattern. Epic in disguise is about size: a project posing as a Story.","2":"Vague AC like the system will be fast is a separate anti-pattern. Epic in disguise is a giant ticket that needs breaking down.","3":"A Story that dictates code is over-specification, a different anti-pattern. Epic in disguise is a ticket too big for a sprint."},"9":{"0":"For Patton a Story is an invitation to talk, not a perfect document. If the PM and team never discussed it, they don't understand it.","2":"In INVEST, N is Negotiable: a Story is open to discussion, not a contract. A PM unwilling to change details creates a problem.","3":"A Story invites a future conversation between the PM and the team. It is not a record of a past talk with a user."},"10":{"0":"WSJF does not look at Cost of Delay alone but divides it by duration: 30/13 is about 2.3, less than 24/8, which is 3.0.","2":"WSJF is a simple calculation, not a statistical test. Dividing by duration gives 3.0 versus 2.3, so they are not equal.","3":"The formula needs only Cost of Delay and Duration, and both are given. Velocity and headcount are not part of it."},"11":{"0":"Edge Cases define how the feature behaves at extremes. They do not say what not to build, such as Excel export.","2":"Context explains the why and who asked. It does not mark where the work ends, so it does not stop an Excel add-on.","3":"A mockup shows how the screen looks. It does not state what the ticket excludes, so it does not stop scope from growing."},"12":{"0":"200 pre-detailed items waste time on work that likely won't be built, and ordering by arrival means no prioritization.","2":"Detailing every item breaks the Iceberg principle, and old items kept just in case become noise. Sorting by date is not prioritizing.","3":"The Product Backlog is a living list that always changes. A fixed quarterly order and items that never leave make it a burden."},"13":{"0":"Dual-Track rests on evidence. Building against the data spends Delivery on a feature users already showed they don't want.","2":"Rerunning only because the result is unwelcome ignores the evidence. The data is meant to open the CEO conversation, not vanish.","3":"Only items that passed positive validation in Discovery move to the Delivery Backlog. An organizational compromise skips that rule."},"14":{"0":"The customer always first is a crude rule. The tech debt and the UX fix have a Cost of Delay too, and the call depends on the numbers.","2":"Tech debt does not always come first. The Enterprise deadline and the proven 8% are Cost of Delay that must be weighed too.","3":"An A/B Test proves value, but not the deadline's Time Criticality or the 20% slowdown. All three need comparing."}},"18":{"0":{"0":"A Git tag serves as an internal anchor for rollback. The number's deeper meaning faces the consumer: what changed and whether upgrading is safe.","2":"The stage warns against a marketing version 2.0: jumping because it sounds good breaks the meaning of the number.","3":"A release date is only what CalVer tells. The number is first of all a contract: what changed and how safe the upgrade is."},"1":{"0":"In SemVer feature size does not decide. MAJOR rises only on a breaking change, and a large compatible feature is MINOR.","2":"Everything shifted down a level: a new compatible feature is MINOR, a bug fix is PATCH, and MAJOR is kept for a breaking change.","3":"Year and month in the number are CalVer, not SemVer. In SemVer each part describes the kind of change and compatibility."},"2":{"0":"The stage warns against a marketing jump to 2.0 with no real breaking change: it breaks the meaning of the number.","2":"The question is whether an existing user must change anything, not how many sprints it took. A large compatible feature is MINOR.","3":"Compatible bug fixes, even many of them, stay at PATCH. Only a change that may break existing code raises MAJOR."},"3":{"0":"MAJOR rises only when something existing breaks. A new optional field forces no existing user to change anything.","1":"PATCH is reserved for bug fixes. A new field is a new compatible capability, so it is MINOR.","3":"MINOR is right, but raising MINOR resets PATCH to zero. 1.5.2 skips that rule."},"4":{"0":"0.y.z does not say who uses the product. It marks initial development with no stability promise. Internal testing has the alpha label.","2":"In version 0 the API is not yet stable, and even a MINOR can break things. The stability commitment starts only at 1.0.0.","3":"Version 0 promises no compatibility at all, so even a MINOR may break things. The 0 does not mean nothing has broken yet."},"5":{"0":"This is reversed: a pre-release is always lower than the final version, so the final 1.0.0 ranks highest of all.","2":"The final 1.0.0 ranks above every pre-release, and alpha is the lowest. The order here is mixed up.","3":"The suffix does affect precedence: alpha is below beta, which is below rc. Only build metadata after a + has no effect."},"6":{"0":"22.04 is a year and a month. A CalVer number says nothing about backward compatibility, so it cannot show a breaking change.","2":"A running number goes 1, 2, 3. Here the number is a year and month, April 2022, which is exactly CalVer.","3":"A hash is a long commit ID that humans cannot read. 22.04 is a short, readable, date-based number."},"7":{"0":"This is reversed: for a library others depend on, compatibility is critical, so SemVer. A SaaS app can make do with CalVer.","2":"The choice depends on who consumes the software, not the release schedule. The stage warns against choosing without thinking of the consumer.","3":"Open source versus commercial is not the test. The question is whether consumers depend on compatibility, like a library or API."},"8":{"0":"Renaming a field is a breaking change, so it is never MINOR. A detailed changelog does not stop the integration from breaking.","2":"A week with no further notice is not a deprecation period. The usual grace period is 6-12 months, with notice and a migration path.","3":"A canary catches bugs early, but the change still breaks that 5% without warning. A breaking change needs a new version and notice."},"9":{"0":"This is reversed: a Changelog is written for developers, precise and concise. Release Notes are for customers and focus on value.","2":"The difference is the audience, not the version type. A Changelog includes Added and Removed too, not only bug fixes.","3":"Both documents record what was already released. A Changelog is not a plan for upcoming versions."},"10":{"0":"Deprecation does not block right away, nor does it last forever: the capability still works, with notice it will go in a future version.","2":"Recording it after the fact is exactly we upgraded and the feature was gone. Deprecation comes before removal, with time to adapt.","3":"A Feature Flag is a release method that hides code. Switching off gradually without notice gives users no time to adapt."},"11":{"0":"Not every commit bumps PATCH: fix bumps PATCH, feat bumps MINOR, and docs does not change the version at all.","2":"Conventional Commits rely on prefixes that describe the change type, not timestamps. The bump is computed by SemVer.","3":"A ticket ID does not say whether a change is a fix, a feature or a breaking change. The prefix is what lets tools compute the version."},"12":{"0":"The number is a contract, not a slogan. A fake jump to 5.0 erodes developers' trust in the whole scheme, Release Notes or not.","2":"A breaking change in a PATCH is the surprise that destroys trust. Whoever upgrades to a small version finds their code broken.","3":"The version number follows SemVer rules, not a vote. There are breaking changes, so it is a MAJOR."}},"19":{"0":{"0":"Karpathy's distinction is not about where software runs but who writes the rules: a programmer, or a network learning from data.","2":"Network access and external APIs are not the difference. In Software 2.0 the programmer supplies data and a structure, and the network learns the rules.","3":"Licensing and payment have nothing to do with it. The difference is hand-written explicit rules versus rules learned from data."},"1":{"0":"The model does not search the web live. It predicts the next Token from what it learned in training, which is why RAG adds fresh information.","2":"The model has no store of ready answers. Each answer is built Token by Token, as a statistical guess of what likely comes next.","3":"Rules a programmer defined in advance are Software 1.0. A language model learned patterns from data and predicts; it runs no rules."},"2":{"0":"Writing direction does not matter to the model. The issue is that the tokenizer was trained mostly on English, so it splits Hebrew into more Tokens.","2":"Models do produce coherent Hebrew. The problem is cost: the same text in Hebrew splits into more Tokens.","3":"Models process Hebrew directly, with no translation. The difference is that the same text uses more Tokens, so it costs more and fills context faster."},"3":{"0":"This is reversed: Training takes weeks to months on thousands of GPUs, while Inference usually takes seconds per request.","1":"Training costs millions of dollars, which is why most startups never train from scratch. Inference costs cents to dollars per call.","3":"These are two different stages: Training learns the weights once, and Inference uses the trained model on every user request."},"4":{"0":"Building from scratch fits only large AI companies: very expensive and needs a research team. Karpathy says start with an API and prove value.","2":"Fine-tuning is the costliest of the three, used only if RAG also falls short. Starting with it skips the cheap, fast options.","3":"The rule is to start now with an existing API and prove value. Waiting for the next model generation is no part of the graduated approach."},"5":{"0":"A hallucination is not a crash or a code bug. The model answers normally, but the information it states with confidence is wrong.","2":"Spelling errors are easy to spot. A hallucination is dangerous because it sounds credible: made-up quotes and sources stated confidently.","3":"A server error message is a technical failure the user can see. A hallucination is a normal-looking answer that contains wrong information."},"6":{"0":"A bigger model still guesses: the current status is not in what it learned in training. The gain came from feeding the status from the DB as a fact.","2":"More Tokens without the right information just make each call pricier. What the model lacked was the real status from a reliable source.","3":"Manually fixing each answer is human work after the error. RAG prevents it upfront, since the model receives the status as a fact."},"7":{"0":"Model choice matters, but first you must know the product pays off: every call costs money, and at 100K users that adds up fast.","2":"Programming language and framework are team decisions. The consideration unique to an AI product is the Inference cost per interaction.","3":"Most startups use an existing model via API rather than training one. The first question is whether revenue covers the usage cost."},"8":{"0":"This asks which AI before why AI. AI is a tool, and first you need a problem AI solves better than a classic solution.","2":"Budget is a later question. Without a defined problem there is nothing to judge whether the cost is worth anything to users.","3":"An integration timeline assumes it is already clear what to build. The first question is which user problem AI will solve."},"9":{"0":"The difference is real: Fine-Tuning trains the model itself on new data, while Prompt Engineering only changes the instructions to an existing model.","2":"Prompt Engineering is the cheap, fast route. The costlier of the two is Fine-Tuning, which needs data and training cost.","3":"Fine-Tuning is extra training on your own data, so it requires data. Without data there is nothing to train on."},"10":{"0":"Without the actual policy documents the model guesses from general knowledge, which is exactly when it makes up a policy.","2":"Fine-tuning suits a specific style or behavior, and it is costly. For current company information, RAG supplies the document itself as fact.","3":"Building a model from scratch fits only large AI companies. Here it is enough to give an existing model the right documents."},"11":{"1":"The model learned reasoning and code too, not only text. The problem is that the word reaches it as Tokens, so it never sees each r.","2":"The word is too short for memory to be the issue. The model simply does not see single letters, only Tokens that are word pieces.","3":"No words are removed from training data to save space. The difficulty comes from tokenization, which splits words into pieces, not letters."},"12":{"0":"A long answer does not guarantee engagement. What is certain is that every Token the model produces costs money, across 50K users.","2":"Answer length does not affect training, which happens once. It affects Inference, which is paid for on every call.","3":"Long answers do fill the context, but the main implication is financial: every Token produced costs money."},"13":{"0":"The reason is not tech adoption. In law, a hallucination such as a wrong citation of a statute can cause real harm to the client.","2":"The issue is not language. Even reading perfectly, the model may invent a ruling with confidence, so a person must verify.","3":"The stage does not rely on such a regulation. The reason is the domain itself: a hallucination in information people rely on is dangerous."},"14":{"0":"Non-determinism is an inherent trait of AI models, not a bug. The vendor won't fix it, so you design an experience that copes with it.","2":"Fine-tuning is costly and suits style or behavior. It does not turn an inherent model trait into a deterministic one.","3":"Freezing the first answer hides the trait instead of designing for it, and a weak first answer stays forever with no way to retry."},"15":{"0":"Clustering finds groups without prior labels. Here there is a known label, cancelled or not, so it is Classification.","2":"Regression predicts a continuous number like price. The question here is yes or no, and the price paid was never asked.","3":"Object Detection is a vision task that locates objects in an image. There are no images here, just a churn prediction."},"16":{"0":"A Baseline is a different idea: the simple result the model must beat. Ground Truth is the correct label each prediction is checked against.","2":"The share of correct predictions is Accuracy, a metric computed against the GT. Nor is it the main KPI: a PM sets a business KPI.","3":"Ground Truth is the label itself, not the raw data before labeling. Label quality, not data volume, sets the ceiling."},"17":{"0":"Plain STT returns text only. Identifying who spoke when is a separate capability, Speaker Diarization, that must be required.","2":"TTS turns text into voice, the opposite direction. A meeting has a voice recording to turn into text and split by speaker.","3":"Object Detection locates objects in images. Identifying speakers in a recording is an audio task, not a vision task."},"18":{"0":"The data scientists decide what is relevant, not the PM. An irrelevant table can hide a surprising correlation or a strong feature.","2":"Nobody can request a table they don't know exists. Without the full picture it is also hard to spot Data Leakage or build features.","3":"In CRISP-DM, Data Understanding comes before Modeling. The early stages, like EDA, are exactly when all the tables are needed."},"19":{"0":"CRISP-DM is cyclical, not linear: you go back as needed, for example from Evaluation back to Business Understanding.","2":"The five questions for exploring data before modeling are EDA. CRISP-DM is the whole six-stage process, from the problem to Deployment.","3":"Metrics like Accuracy and AUC-ROC belong only to the Evaluation stage. CRISP-DM is a methodology for the whole project."},"20":{"0":"More X-rays with the same labels keep the same shortcut. Only a doctor, with domain knowledge, saw what the model really learned.","2":"The shortcut works technically and scores high, so a deeper network need not drop it. It was a doctor who exposed it.","3":"The shortcut works on the test data too, so another metric would also look good. A doctor's review exposed it, not a metrics table."}},"20":{"0":{"0":"There are real users: the combatant in the field, at sea, in the air or on the border. The hard part is reaching them, not that they don't exist.","2":"The principles are similar but the application differs a lot: regulation, classification, long procurement and many echelons reshape almost every tool.","3":"The soldier wants a simple, fast, reliable system, built for the worst case: dark, rain, gloves. UX is critical, not optional."},"1":{"0":"Many military systems are not online, so there is no analytics tooling. Usage data from the field is usually unavailable.","1":"It is hard to book a 45-minute interview with a combatant on duty. And per the stage, one hour in a debrief is worth ten interviews.","3":"NPS is a civilian feedback tool. In the field feedback comes from debriefs and reports, sometimes only verbally, and that is where failures surface."},"2":{"0":"An ICD describes how the system talks to other systems. It does not describe how the system fits into a shift and field procedures.","2":"An STR adds more technical requirements. The operations manager did not ask what the system does but how it is actually used.","3":"A Traceability Matrix links each requirement to a need, a test and a component. It does not describe use during a stormy shift."},"3":{"0":"Budget is not the reason. Even with money you cannot run two versions side by side in combat and risk the mission for an experiment.","1":"The contract is not the reason. An A/B Test runs different versions in parallel, and in operations the system must work uniformly.","3":"Exercises and simulations are exactly the tools used to validate military systems. The problem is a controlled experiment in combat, not exercise data."},"4":{"0":"This is reversed: FAT happens at the factory, in a controlled environment, and SAT happens at the real site, under field conditions.","2":"Paper and pre-production design reviews are PDR and CDR. FAT and SAT are acceptance tests of a system already built.","3":"A Red Team tries to break the system, a separate tool. FAT and SAT differ by place: factory versus site."},"5":{"0":"A markup on development does not cover the rest. Development is only 20-30% of TCO; the rest is integration, training and years of upkeep.","2":"A license price does not map lifecycle costs: integration, training, security approvals and disposal at the end. That is the rest.","3":"Whoever looks only at development cost misses about 70% of the picture. The project manager asked about exactly that missing part."},"6":{"0":"The budget is fixed with not a shekel more. Explaining value does not change an approved ceiling, so you must design within it.","2":"Cutting the price without cutting scope just moves the overrun into maintenance fees. The budget still doesn't cover the whole list.","3":"A TCO shows cost over time but does not change a fixed 2.5 million budget. The bid still exceeds the ceiling."},"7":{"0":"A senior commander is just one echelon. A military project has at least 6 groups, and the budget echelon, for one, can kill a project.","1":"The dev team and vendor are only part of the picture. Without the user, logistics and budget echelons, a brilliant product can fail.","3":"The field user matters, but the commander decides, the state buys and the treasury funds. Talking only to users misses the decision makers."},"8":{"0":"A senior commander cares about readiness, capabilities and schedule. Technical jargon floods them instead of translating into operational terms.","2":"For the senior echelon the language is operational and concise. A long email with every detail does not give the bottom line.","3":"Methodology is the product team's language. With a commander you talk about operational capabilities, not Sprints or User Stories."},"9":{"0":"The stage separates critical systems from information systems. Velox manages technicians' tasks and does not run the grid, so a v1 is legitimate.","2":"In grid control a failure can bring down the grid, so zero failures are required. There is no fix it next version there, FAT and SAT or not.","3":"The test is the system type, critical or information system, not the maintenance budget. Money does not make a critical system flexible."},"10":{"0":"Recruiting developers is a different challenge. Rotation means people inside the project change roles every 2-3 years, and the knowledge leaves too.","2":"Requirement changes and multi-year plans are procurement challenges. Rotation is about people leaving and the knowledge they take.","3":"Vendor dependence is a COTS drawback, not a rotation one. The problem here is the Champion, SMEs and users who change."},"11":{"0":"Custom development from scratch is the opposite of COTS. It is reserved for needs that truly have no answer in the market.","2":"An adaptation layer is an add-on that fits COTS to needs, and the combination is called Hybrid. COTS itself is the ready commercial product.","3":"A tender is the vendor-selection stage of procurement. COTS describes the kind of solution: a commercial off-the-shelf product."},"12":{"0":"A broad rollout before value is proven raises the risk. The recommended approach is to start small and learn before expanding.","2":"Changing doctrine needs senior command backing, so you first prove value in a small POC and only then change doctrine.","3":"Incremental upgrades are the safe path but do not bring in new technology. The recommendation is controlled innovation through a POC."},"13":{"0":"There is plenty of product work. Many echelons, classification and long approvals simply take most of the time for coordination.","2":"It is not a lack of technical knowledge. Even a very technical PM spends most time coordinating, because a 6-plus echelon setup works that way.","3":"The reason is the organizational structure, not missing tools. Many stakeholders, hierarchy and classification demand constant coordination."},"14":{"0":"The commander, state and treasury are not mere approval steps. Each has its own needs: capabilities, Value for Money and staying in budget.","1":"The Ministry of Defense is the buyer and wants Value for Money. It does not represent the soldier who wants simplicity or the commander who wants capabilities.","3":"The needs differ and even conflict: simplicity for the soldier, capabilities for the commander, Value for Money for the state, budget for the treasury."}},"21":{"0":{"0":"The stage says nothing about pay. The core difference is that decisions lock: molds, components already bought and certifications.","2":"In hardware a product generation takes months to years, and a change after a lock costs many times more. The iteration pace is entirely different.","3":"Michal went to the field with Gilad and saw technicians charge in the vehicle, so the port moved. Understanding users is critical in hardware too."},"1":{"0":"The problem is the assembly process, not the design. Changing the gasket means a mold fix and 6-8 weeks: too strong a medicine for a process problem.","2":"Going on without conditions ships leaking units to the customer. A leak is a physical problem, not a bug you fix in the field.","3":"Lowering the target so the gate passes is skipping the gate. Criteria are set in advance, not once the results are on the table."},"2":{"0":"Even without freight and warranty, (299-180)/299 is about 40%, not 55%. And freight and warranty are a real cost of every device.","2":"Calculating from the BOM alone gives about 40%, but misses freight, duties and warranty, which are a real cost of every unit.","3":"195 out of 299 is about 65%, but that is the cost's share of the price. Gross margin is what is left: 104 out of 299, about 35%."},"3":{"0":"Quite the opposite: the lesson is to design a board that accepts alternative components when choosing parts in EVT, around the chips.","2":"The stage does not suggest making chips yourself. The lesson is a second source or a board that accepts alternatives, decided in EVT.","3":"A hardware product needs physical components. The lesson is not to give up hardware but to avoid relying on one supplier for a critical part."},"4":{"0":"1,800 covers only production. Without stock to repair devices already in the field, the modems run out before the next generation is ready.","2":"Moving straight to the next generation needs redesign, tests and certification from scratch. Meanwhile there are no modems for production or repairs.","3":"A last-time buy is usually NCNR: no cancelling, no returns. Buying the maximum leaves cash stuck in the warehouse."},"5":{"0":"IP is one rating under IEC 60529, not a regional standard. The first digit describes dust protection and the second water protection.","2":"Drops and vibration are tested under other durability standards, like MIL-STD-810. An IP rating covers only dust and water.","3":"An IP rating says nothing about temperature range. Its two digits describe dust and water protection; IP68 is dust-tight and survives long immersion."},"6":{"0":"Skipping a stage because a competitor announced a product is a common mistake. The skip comes back as a bigger cost at the next stage.","2":"Finishing early is no reason to stop. You pass a gate when the criteria set in advance are met, not by the schedule.","3":"Time pressure that skips stages comes back as a bigger cost later. Gate criteria are set in advance and are not erased by a deadline."},"7":{"0":"The staged rollout exists for exactly this. Going to 100% spreads the problem to every device with the alternative battery.","2":"Replacing batteries is a costly physical response to a problem made in software. Rolling back through A/B partitions solves it.","3":"Rolling out to all pushes the problem onto the customer and ignores the preset stop metric. Field crews end up with batteries that drain fast."},"8":{"0":"Hardware is updated after production: OTA fixes logic, interface and battery management. But only if the mechanism was planned in advance.","2":"A technician visit per device is exactly what planning ahead avoids. Without an update mechanism, every fault becomes a field trip.","3":"Replacing a whole device over firmware is the costliest response. An update mechanism planned in EVT prevents it."},"9":{"0":"The change itself is identical at both stages. What grows is everything already locked around it: molds, component stock and finished units.","2":"The cost is not paperwork. At MP you fix expensive molds, update the BOM, repeat tests and scrap inventory.","3":"A CEO approval costs nothing. The cost is molds, scrapped inventory and repeated tests and certifications."},"10":{"0":"The fix is the same, but at MP there are already molds, stock and finished units. That is why the same problem costs far more later.","1":"The rule of thumb is 100 times or more, not 10. At MP you don't just repeat tests; you change molds and scrap stock.","3":"At MP there are not necessarily sold units to recall. The rule of thumb for a problem found after mass production is 100 times."},"11":{"0":"Lowering the price shrinks margin and does not change unit cost. The target is cutting the BOM itself from $180 to $120.","2":"Switching manufacturer without a quality check is risky: what you don't see at the factory you find at the customer. Each such change needs tests.","3":"Waiting does not cut cost. Unit cost is set mainly in design, and cost-down is active work on components, assembly and parts."},"12":{"0":"Certification is done at an accredited lab, not at the manufacturer in days. Pre-compliance, formal tests and approvals take weeks to months.","2":"CE and FCC test safety, EMC and radio. Water and dust resistance is the IP rating, a separate topic.","3":"The problem is time, not accounting. Without certification you cannot sell, and any lab finding needs another round of fixes."},"13":{"0":"A recall comes when there is a safety risk. Here there is no such sign, and the fault is limited to one batch of 600 units.","2":"A firmware update does not fix a worn connector, and raising the charging current could create a new risk.","3":"Waiting for returns leaves crews without a device. When the fault is known and limited to a batch, you swap proactively by serial range."},"14":{"0":"Every component change in hardware is a trade-off. Without checking cost, lead time and schedule impact, you can't tell if it pays.","2":"Sunlight readability is a core requirement for field crews. A sticker does not solve it, and the problem would reach customers in this generation.","3":"A component change requires repeating tests. Skipping to MP without DVT comes back as a bigger cost at a later stage."}},"22":{"0":{"0":"Gamification does not build games. It takes game mechanics into non-game contexts like products, processes and learning.","2":"Animations are cosmetics. Real gamification understands the psychology of motivation and uses it to drive behavior.","3":"Sprints belong to a development methodology. Gamification uses game mechanics to drive user behavior."},"1":{"0":"Money, promotion and recognition are external rewards. SDT is about intrinsic motivation, which lasts after the prize is gone.","2":"Competition and winning mainly drive the Killer player type. SDT defines basic needs for everyone: choice, progress and belonging.","3":"Points, badges and leaderboards are mechanics (PBL), not needs. A good mechanic only serves those needs."},"2":{"1":"The effect is not about how many actions users take. It describes damage to intrinsic motivation when a reward is added to a willing activity.","2":"This is the opposite: the effect shows an external reward can harm intrinsic motivation, not always strengthen it.","3":"It is not about demanding bigger rewards. Children who got a prize for drawing drew less once the prize was removed."},"3":{"0":"An Explorer is driven by curiosity and wants new content, like Easter eggs and hidden content. A ranking does not interest them.","1":"A Socializer wants to share, help and communicate. Mechanics that suit them are chat, sharing and teams, not a ranking.","2":"An Achiever wants to complete and collect, and prefers badges, levels and challenges. They need not beat others to feel accomplished."},"4":{"0":"The split is not extrinsic versus intrinsic. White Hat creates positive motivation where users feel good; Black Hat drives through FOMO and fear.","2":"Social Influence and Ownership are in neither group here. White Hat is meaning, accomplishment and empowerment; Black Hat is scarcity, unpredictability and avoidance.","3":"The split is by feeling, not time horizon: White Hat leaves the user satisfied, Black Hat creates addiction without satisfaction."},"5":{"0":"The Investment is not payment but the data, content and time the user puts in. It raises the product's value and makes leaving harder.","2":"An unpredictable reward is the Variable Reward stage, the third one. Investment follows it and strengthens the next cycle.","3":"A reminder that brings the user back is an external Trigger, the model's first stage. Investment is what the user puts in."},"6":{"0":"DAU up while NPS falls is a known sign: users log in for fear of losing the streak, not for enjoyment. It won't fix itself.","2":"Streaks directly explain the drop: they trigger Avoidance, fear of loss. Users feel obliged to log in but are less satisfied.","3":"The problem is not boredom but a sense of obligation. More mechanics won't fix the fear of losing a streak and may worsen it."},"7":{"0":"Development cost is a risk in any project. The risk unique to B2B gamification is a childish feel and organizational resistance.","2":"The stage does not warn of this. In B2B the drivers are efficiency and career growth, and the risk is that the game feels childish and is rejected.","3":"Competitors copying is not the risk the stage names. The risk is senior salespeople rejecting points and badges as childish."},"8":{"0":"The mechanics differ: streaks and XP on Duolingo, segments and KOMs on Strava, a contribution graph on GitHub. What they share is a link to meaningful activity.","2":"The stage does not credit price for the success. GitHub's graph shows real work, and Duolingo's streak shows persistence in learning.","3":"GitHub serves developers in professional work. Age is not the secret; the link to something already important to the user is."},"9":{"0":"Watching videos is not Flow. Flow is full focus that comes when the challenge level matches the skill level.","2":"Racking up points fast does not create Flow. If the challenge is too easy for the skill, the result is boredom.","3":"Compulsive use that skips food and sleep is a compulsion loop, a dark pattern. Flow is a healthy balance of challenge and skill."},"10":{"0":"A leaderboard shows 95% of users they are not at the top. Instead of motivation, they feel a lack of competence.","2":"The main risk is not cost but the effect on motivation: most users will see they are far from the top.","3":"The stage does not warn of burnout. Its risk is the opposite: most users get discouraged and engage less."},"11":{"1":"The stage sets no user-count limit on gamification. Duolingo and Strava work at huge scale.","2":"Gamification is very common in B2C, like Duolingo and Strava. B2B uses subtler mechanics.","3":"Duolingo is a mobile app with streaks, XP and leagues. Screen size does not prevent gamification."},"12":{"0":"These are product development steps. The Hook Model describes a habit loop: what triggers, an action, a reward and an investment.","2":"This describes a path to a purchase decision. The Hook Model does not end at action: a variable reward and an investment close the loop.","3":"These are stages of a solution design process. Nir Eyal's Hook Model explains how products build a habit that repeats."},"13":{"0":"A leaderboard discourages most users who are not on top, and it rewards speed rather than completion. As a first step it is risky.","2":"Badges work only when they mark a real achievement. A first step should be simple, show progress and suit every player type.","3":"A discount is an external reward that can weaken intrinsic motivation, and it is more complex. Start simple and measure."},"14":{"0":"Paying more is no ethics test, and Pay to Win is itself a dark pattern. The test is how users feel once they understand the system.","2":"DAU can rise from manipulation too, like streaks that work on fear. A high number does not mean the system is fair.","3":"Competitors using a mechanic does not make it ethical. A dark pattern stays a dark pattern even when common."}},"23":{"0":{"1":"These are different layers: UI is the visual design, and IA is how information is structured. IA is mainly the PM's responsibility.","2":"The difference is not the platform. Every product, web or app, has both an information architecture and a visual design.","3":"IA is the whole structure of information: hierarchy, navigation and how content is organized across the product, not just the main menu."},"1":{"0":"Colors and fonts are Visual Design, the designer's field. The PM owns the why, the what and the IA.","2":"Icons and animations belong to visual design, the designer's job. The PM defines the product framework.","3":"Figma mockups are the designer's work. The PM defines user goals and information structure, and the designer turns them into a visual experience."},"2":{"0":"These are screen sizes. Screen states describe the data condition: empty, loading, partial, full or error.","2":"This is a story structure, not a screen's. A spec defines what shows with no data, while loading, with partial data, full data and an error.","3":"These are display variants of the same screen. The five states are about what happens to the data, such as what shows when nothing exists yet."},"3":{"0":"Org structure is exactly what IA should not mirror. Card Sorting showed users put Reports next to Settings, not Dashboard.","2":"IA is the PM's product decision, not a design call. And it should mirror users' mental model, not one team member's opinion.","3":"The support team's gut feeling is still a guess. Card Sorting lets about 15 users sort for themselves and reveals their mental model."},"4":{"0":"Hierarchical fits layered content, from general to specific. Here these are steps in a process with a start and an end, not sub-levels.","2":"Matrix fits filtering and sorting on several axes, like Amazon. This flow is a sequence of steps, not a search by attributes.","3":"Tag-Based fits content with many links, like hashtags. A project-creation flow must lead the user step by step."},"5":{"0":"These are task board columns: they describe a task's status, not the conditions under which a feature counts as done.","2":"This is a cycle for improving a process. It does not define a starting state, an action and a testable expected result.","3":"This sorts team habits to change. It says nothing about what the screen must do or how to test that it works."},"6":{"0":"Small rounds are not the problem: 2 rounds of 5 users beat one of 10. The mistake lies in who you test with.","2":"The stage does not list remote testing as a mistake. The common one is friends and family, who are too nice and know the product through you.","3":"What matters is documenting everything: what they said, did and where they got stuck. The medium matters less than who is tested."},"7":{"0":"One or two users are not enough to spot a pattern. A real problem is a place where 3 or more users got stuck.","2":"Usability testing is qualitative research that finds patterns, not a statistical sample. Just 5 users find about 85% of problems.","3":"Each extra user adds less and less. 100 users in one round waste time better spent on fixing and another round."},"8":{"0":"There is a difference: a Sitemap answers which screens exist, and a User Flow answers how a user gets from A to B.","2":"The difference is not the platform. Both serve websites and apps alike, and show a broad picture versus a focused path.","3":"The difference is not the audience. The PM builds a Sitemap to check all needs are covered, and a User Flow shows one path to a goal."},"9":{"0":"An instruction like change it to green dictates a solution. The PM explains the problem, and the designer knows how to solve it.","2":"Not pretty does not tell the designer what is wrong. Good feedback explains the why, for example that the screen is inconsistent with the product.","3":"The PM is a partner who brings the why: the user's goal and what came up in interviews. Skipping feedback leaves the designer without context."},"10":{"1":"NPS measures willingness to recommend, not usability. Sharon asked for a number that compares usability itself, before and after.","2":"6 participants reveal patterns, but they are not statistics you can compare with an industry. Qualitative research gives no benchmark.","3":"A 5-Second Test checks first impressions: whether it is clear what the screen does. It gives no usability score to compare to an average."},"11":{"0":"Empty State is when there is no data yet, like a first team. Here the user already clicked and the request is on its way.","2":"Partial State shows only some of the information. This is not about how much data there is but a request still waiting on the server.","3":"Error State comes only if the request failed. Right after the click it is not yet known whether it will succeed."},"12":{"0":"User goals and the persona's mental model are part of the why and the what, so they are the PM's responsibility.","2":"IA and navigation depth are a product decision, mainly the PM's, with the designer as a partner.","3":"Acceptance Criteria are written by the PM and serve as the basis for QA. They are not the designer's field."},"13":{"0":"SUS is filled in after working with the screen and measures usability. Michal wants to check a first impression, before investing in hi-fi.","2":"Card Sorting tests how users organize information, that is, IA. It does not test whether a given screen conveys what the product does.","3":"A performance test measures load speed, not communication. A screen that loads fast can still be unclear."},"14":{"0":"Overview is the spec's first section and the frame for the rest, so it is hard to forget. Edge cases are what get dropped.","1":"Interface components are the basis for design and almost always appear. What gets forgotten is what happens when the server fails or data is empty.","3":"Acceptance Criteria are the basis for QA and a familiar section. The stage marks Edge Cases as the one most PMs forget."}},"24":{"0":{"0":"The difference is not the platform. Websites and apps both have a UI for people and an API that systems talk through.","2":"UI and API are not defined by department. A UI is an interface for humans, and an API is an interface between systems.","3":"Both are interfaces, but for entirely different users: screens and buttons for humans, versus a contract other systems call."},"1":{"0":"SOAP rests on formal XML and suits legacy financial and government systems. It is not chosen for performance.","1":"REST with JSON is simple, common and suits a Public API. Between internal services gRPC is 5-10 times faster.","3":"WebSocket is a persistent two-way connection for real time, like chats and games. Performance between microservices is gRPC's domain."},"2":{"0":"A Rate Limit caps request volume but cannot tell a retry from a new request. Duplicates continue, and legitimate requests get blocked.","2":"GET is for reading only, not for creating resources. You cannot create a task with GET, so this does not solve it.","3":"Retrying after a network drop is reasonable client behavior. A nightly script leaves duplicates all day and pushes the problem onto customers."},"3":{"0":"Adding an optional response field breaks no existing client; they simply ignore it. It is a Non-Breaking change.","1":"A new endpoint changes nothing clients already use. It is a Non-Breaking change.","3":"A new optional header forces no client to change code. Clients that don't send it keep working as before."},"4":{"0":"This is where GraphQL is weak: it is less cache-friendly. REST is the cacheable one.","2":"GraphQL is more complex to maintain. For a simple API and a fast launch, REST fits better.","3":"For maximum speed between internal microservices you use gRPC. GraphQL solves over-fetching, not performance."},"5":{"0":"Faster polling just adds load and wasted requests, and there is still a delay. A Webhook sends the event the moment it happens.","1":"GraphQL improves the shape of a query, not its timing. Their server would still have to ask again and again whether anything changed.","3":"A nightly file arrives a full day later. The firm asked to know the moment a task closes, not the next morning."},"6":{"0":"A hand-updated document goes stale fast and generates no SDKs, mock server or conformance tests. It does not stay in sync with the code.","2":"Asking everyone to wait defeats the goal. A Mock Server generated from OpenAPI lets them start building before the backend is ready.","3":"JSON Schema describes only an object's structure. Without endpoints there is no contract to generate docs, SDKs or a mock from."},"7":{"0":"Renaming the service each version confuses and breaks integrations. URL Versioning keeps the name and adds v1 or v2 to the path.","2":"Replacing the server kills the old version. In any strategy, supporting the old version alongside the new one is a must.","3":"Updating the existing version with a breaking change brings clients down. That is why you version and support the old one in parallel."},"8":{"0":"200 OK means the request succeeded. It is the opposite of an invalid request.","1":"404 means the resource was not found. The request itself can be perfectly valid.","3":"500 is an error on the server side. An invalid request is a client-side problem, like a missing parameter."},"9":{"0":"Limiting data access by Scopes is Authorization. Rate Limiting caps the number of requests, not what a client may see.","2":"Preventing duplicates after a retry is the job of an Idempotency Key. Rate Limiting protects the system from overload.","3":"Measuring Latency and Error Rate is monitoring. Rate Limiting does not measure; it blocks a client that consumes too much."},"10":{"0":"Basic Auth sends a username and password and is advised only for internal HTTPS. A third-party app should never get a password.","1":"A fixed API Key suits internal services and prototypes. It does not tell users apart and has no Scopes.","3":"A Public API is open to third parties, not open to everyone without identity. Without authentication there is no knowing who accesses what."},"11":{"0":"The time from request to response is Latency. Time to First Hello World starts at sign-up and ends at the first successful request.","2":"Payment is a business metric. Time to First Hello World is a DX metric: how fast a new developer gets the API working.","3":"That measures the internal dev team. Time to First Hello World measures the experience of an outside developer who signs up."},"12":{"0":"JSON is human-readable and easy to debug, but not the most compact. A 100KB record in JSON is about 35KB in Protobuf.","1":"XML is actually the heaviest: the same record takes 130KB, versus 35KB in Protobuf.","3":"HTML is for showing pages to people, not for moving data between servers. It is not a compact data format."},"13":{"0":"Renaming a field is breaking, even by one letter. Every client using userName will fail, so a PATCH does not fit.","2":"Even 5% of clients are real clients who will break. A breaking change needs a new version no matter how many use the field.","3":"The diagnosis is right, but a forced switch next release breaks whoever can't update in time. You need v2 alongside v1 with a support period."},"14":{"0":"A connection that waits for minutes breaks easily and hurts the experience, even with a long timeout. Anything over 2-3 seconds should be async.","2":"A server upgrade does not guarantee a 2-minute job drops to 3 seconds. An async design solves it without betting on hardware.","3":"Two one-minute calls are still long waits, far above 2-3 seconds. And splitting a report in two complicates the client."}},"25":{"0":{"0":"Jumping straight to a solution is the number one reason PM interviews fail. A fast fix without knowing the user and their pain is a guess.","2":"Past experience belongs to behavioral questions. A Product Sense question starts with understanding the question and the user.","3":"A wireframe is already a solution. Before sketching you need to clarify what exactly is asked and who you are building for."},"1":{"0":"A single idea with no user or pain points is jumping to a solution, mistake number one. A clear structure is what earns a high score.","2":"Company background does not answer the question. The right structure starts with the user and their pains, then moves to solutions and metrics.","3":"This is the reverse order: ideas before a user means jumping to a solution. First check who you build for and what hurts, then propose."},"2":{"0":"An answer with no metrics marks an immature candidate. The interviewer wants a primary metric, secondary ones and a guardrail.","2":"NPS alone is a general score. It lacks a primary metric tied to the feature's goal and a guardrail that checks nothing was harmed.","3":"Counting clicks does not show whether users got value, and there is no guardrail to check that nothing else was harmed."},"3":{"0":"Not knowing the company hurts, but mistake number one is jumping to a solution without understanding for whom and why.","2":"Brevity is not the main issue. Even a long answer fails if it starts with a solution instead of the user and the problem.","3":"Candidates don't fail for lack of knowledge but for lack of structure. And the most common mistake is jumping to a solution."},"4":{"0":"These are strategic planning terms. STAR tells a story: the situation, the task, what you actually did and what came of it.","2":"Strengths and threats come from SWOT, a strategy analysis tool. STAR is a structure for a behavioral story.","3":"This lacks Action, the most important part of STAR: what you specifically did. And without Result there is no measurable outcome."},"5":{"0":"The easiest to build ignores Impact. The interviewer wants criteria, a reasoned comparison and what you gave up.","2":"An Execution question tests your own ability to prioritize. Passing the decision to the CEO shows no systematic thinking.","3":"I'll do all three is a lack of prioritization, a common mistake. The question asks you to choose and explain the trade-off."},"6":{"0":"1-2 interviews are not enough to build structure and confidence. The practical rule is 50 written questions and 20 mock interviews.","1":"Reading theory alone is not enough. You must practice out loud, like a sport, until the moves become instinct.","3":"The stage does not call for 100. By its practical rule, 50 written questions and 20 mock interviews are enough to be ready."},"7":{"0":"The method recommends 3-5 ideas, not 10. What's missing is why the other two were given up.","2":"A wireframe is not part of the structure. After listing solutions comes Evaluate: comparing trade-offs.","3":"Competitor analysis is not needed at this point. Interviewers expect to hear why one was chosen and the cost of giving up the others."},"8":{"0":"I love it because it's good is exactly the weak answer. They test whether you break a product down: user, pain, solution and why it works.","2":"The question does not ask you to pitch the product but to analyze it: who it is for, why it succeeds and what you would improve.","3":"Market-trend knowledge is not what's tested here. They test Product Sense: understanding why a product works and where the opportunity is."},"9":{"0":"Picking the easiest to build ignores Impact and risk. Interviewers want to see defined criteria, not a shortcut.","2":"Adopting the manager's view shows no thinking framework. Interviewers test how you make a decision yourselves.","3":"The question assumes there isn't enough data. A good PM decides under uncertainty, with a stated confidence level and a way to check later."},"10":{"0":"Five ideas before choosing a user is jumping to a solution. After the clarifying questions comes identifying the customer.","2":"Competitor analysis is not the next CIRCLES step. After Comprehend comes Identify: choosing a persona with a reason.","3":"A wireframe is already a solution. Without a chosen persona, designing for all students means designing for no one."},"11":{"0":"Technical depth does not make the answer good. Missing are the situation, what you actually did and the measurable result.","2":"The company name and job title don't tell what you did. The key part of STAR is the Action, followed by a result in numbers.","3":"References come at the end of the hiring process, not inside the answer. The answer itself needs a STAR structure with a measurable result."},"12":{"0":"The lowest effort without high Impact delivers little value. You first prioritize what has both high impact and low effort.","2":"The highest Impact regardless of Effort ignores half the matrix. You start with what gives the most value for the least work.","3":"Handing the choice to the interviewer gives up what they came to test: a reasoned process and trade-offs you explain."},"13":{"0":"Originality is not what's tested. Waze is fine; the problem is the answer does not analyze who it's for, why it works and what to improve.","2":"The question is about a product you love, from any field. What's tested is the depth of analysis, not the product's field.","3":"The question asks for a product you love, others' included. The weakness is the shallow analysis, not choosing a product you didn't build."},"14":{"0":"Calculating the financial loss does not show where the problem is. To propose a fix you need to know where exactly users get stuck.","2":"An industry comparison tells whether 45% is good, but not where the other 55% stop. That is what points to the right fix.","3":"A development-time estimate assumes a solution already exists. First understand where in the funnel users drop off and why."}},"26":{"0":{"0":"Staying in the time frame is part of the test and shows judgment. A 35-hour prototype that missed the question lost to a focused submission.","2":"Speed is not what's tested. A partial analysis misses what reviewers look for: framing, trade-offs and one clear recommendation.","3":"Covering every option in depth is a common mistake, solving everything. The brief set a frame, and ignoring a stated constraint is an error."},"1":{"0":"History and competitors delay the bottom line. When the recommendation is missing from minute one, reviewers guess where you're going.","1":"A personal story does not say what you recommend. BLUF opens with the recommendation, the metric it moves and why.","3":"A chronological walk through every option buries the recommendation at the end. Open with the bottom line and test the rest against it."},"2":{"1":"An email campaign is jumping to a fix. It is not yet known whether the drop is real, who it hit or why.","2":"A rollback before checking the data is a guess. The measurement may have changed, or the cause may be external, not the release.","3":"External causes like competitors come later. First confirm what exactly is measured and that the drop is real, then segment."},"3":{"0":"Every single source is biased. Levels.fyi, for example, is strong on large companies and thin on startups, so it can't carry a range alone.","1":"The top of a recruiter's range is one role's budget, not market data. A researched range is built from independent sources.","2":"An old figure plus a fixed percentage is a guess. The stage recommends sources from the last 12 months, comparing like with like."},"4":{"0":"The CS years are her strongest proof. Recruiters ask which product decisions you've made, and courses alone don't answer that.","2":"A client list shows scope, not product work. Missing are the pains she spotted, the recommendation she made and what changed.","3":"A title she never held is a risk: a reference check will expose the gap. Write the real title and show the product work in the bullets."},"5":{"0":"Was responsible for describes an area, not an achievement. A measurable result showing what changed because of you is missing.","1":"Generic traits like passion and communication are things any candidate can write. There is no verb, action or result here.","3":"A count of features and meetings is workload, not outcome. A strong bullet shows what you did and what improved, in numbers."},"6":{"0":"The stage suggests a How I worked slide: sources, what you checked and where AI helped. Hiding it hurts credibility if it comes out.","1":"Reviewers ask about every slide, and every number gets checked. Candidate B lost credibility over an unchecked 73% figure from an AI tool.","2":"Companies set different rules: some encourage AI and some restrict it. So you read the instructions rather than avoid it by default."},"7":{"1":"Picking a model is a detail, not a strategy. Even the largest model errs; the question is how you measure quality and handle doubt.","2":"Promising to answer everything ignores the cost of a billing error. A strong answer plans for when the model is unsure, including a human handoff.","3":"In AI product sense, defining quality and evals is part of the PM's job, not just engineering's. A nice interface won't save a wrong answer."},"8":{"0":"Typing speed and tool names are not assessed. The tool is just a tool; what's measured is your judgment.","1":"Checking the output critically is exactly what's assessed. Trusting the tool without checking misses the point of the exercise.","3":"That is not the criterion. They assess how you frame, direct, check and decide, and they expect you to think out loud."},"9":{"0":"A general account of how you usually act is a red flag. A behavioral question asks for one real case.","2":"Blaming the other side is a red flag. The focus should be on what you did and the result.","3":"Lessons and general advice don't replace a real case. Missing are the situation, the task, your actions and the result."},"10":{"0":"APM programs have few openings and many candidates, and need a standout portfolio. Limiting yourself to them lowers your odds.","1":"Quitting closes the most realistic door, an internal move. Recruiters ask which product decisions you've made, and a course won't answer that.","2":"Waiting passively builds no proof. Real product tasks now are the material for your CV and interviews."},"11":{"1":"The stage describes the take-home as a common step at many companies. A strong CV gets you into the process, it does not replace it.","2":"A process at one company usually takes 3-6 weeks over several rounds. An offer right after the recruiter call is not the norm.","3":"The end of the process usually has 2-3 reference calls. Pick people who saw you work up close in advance and brief them."},"12":{"0":"Base alone ignores the study fund, bonus and equity. In the stage's example, the offer with the lower base totalled more over a year.","1":"Options on paper are not money until an exit, and can be worth anywhere from zero up. Weigh their risk and vesting.","3":"Matching base against base misses the other components, and atmosphere is no comparison method. Compare total compensation."},"13":{"0":"The stage does not credit the ranking to slide design. Candidate A answered the question asked, with a clear user and a primary metric.","2":"The ranking rested on the submission itself, not seniority. Candidate C's prototype solved a different problem and took four times as long.","3":"The ranking did not come from matching a prior plan. Candidate A framed the problem, showed trade-offs and kept to the 8 hours."},"14":{"0":"A confident guess erodes trust once it proves wrong. I don't know yet, here's how I'd check signals maturity.","1":"Apologizing for your English makes you smaller before you start, and doesn't answer the question. Better to say you'll check and follow up.","2":"Jumping to another slide is dodging, and the panel notices. Honesty plus a way to check builds trust."},"15":{"0":"The data is not old: Levels.fyi is from November 2026. The gap comes from what is counted: total compensation versus base pay only.","1":"Most PM Live respondents work at product companies, not small ones specifically. The main difference is that it reports base pay only.","3":"Both sources report gross pay. The gap is because Levels.fyi includes bonus and stock, while PM Live reports base only."},"16":{"1":"A US figure is total comp with stock, in another currency. With an Israeli company you anchor in Israeli sources, base against base.","2":"The dollar rose about 15% in half a year, and the figure includes RSUs, other taxes and living costs. Converting at today's rate does not make it fair.","3":"The figure is not worthless: it matters when weighing relocation or remote work for a foreign company. It just isn't an anchor for local talks."},"17":{"0":"A study fund is not required by law, so check it's in the contract. In PM Live's survey only 71% reported having one.","2":"A 13th salary is not required by law. It is owed only under a contract, a collective agreement, an extension order or an established practice.","3":"A pension is required by law, and the employer cannot skip it. What's worth checking is which pay the contributions are based on."}},"27":{"0":{"0":"Fifty questions picked for a demo don't represent real use. 94% in a demo says nothing about hard cases like Hebrew and permissions.","2":"A bigger model does not guarantee the mistakes vanish, nor show where the product fails. Only testing real questions by slice reveals that.","3":"A note that answers may be wrong does not measure where or how badly. Mistakes in the hard slices still reach every customer."},"1":{"0":"Auto-approving refunds combines a costly money mistake with one that is hard to spot. That is not a good first bet.","1":"Moving tasks between employees combines a costly fairness mistake with one that is hard to spot. No person checks before it happens.","2":"The forecast is tempting for its high value, but there is little history and it goes straight to the customer. A mistake surfaces too late."},"2":{"0":"Generic metrics sound professional but miss your product's specific failures. The categories should grow out of reading conversations.","1":"An LLM judge with no definitions produces meaningless numbers. First learn which mistakes happen, then define what it should check.","3":"Few users click thumbs up or down, mostly the very happy or the very angry. That is no basis for a decision."},"3":{"1":"Even the strongest model errs, and judges have known tendencies like preferring long answers. Calibrating against human labels is a must.","2":"A 1 to 10 scale is less consistent than pass or fail. More detail does not mean more reliability.","3":"A judge tends to favor its own style. Letting it grade its own answers amplifies the bias instead of checking it."},"4":{"0":"An average that rose can hide a slice that collapsed, and in an Israeli product Hebrew is no marginal slice. Check thresholds per slice.","1":"By the time complaints arrive, customers have already been hurt. The drop is known now, so you stop before release.","3":"Deleting the Hebrew examples only hides the problem. Hebrew users will still get worse answers."},"5":{"1":"When the steps are known in advance, an autonomous agent adds complexity and risk for no gain. A fixed process is more predictable, cheaper and easier to test.","2":"Several agents add more complexity to a process whose steps are fixed. AI is needed only in the summary step, not in every step.","3":"The summary step is exactly where AI adds value. Writing by hand gives that up, while a human check before sending already covers the risk."},"6":{"0":"95% per step does not mean 95% for the task. The chance all ten steps succeed is 0.95 to the power of 10, about 60%.","2":"Nothing in the sketch makes the steps check each other. Without checkpoints, errors compound rather than cancel out.","3":"Each step fails 5% of the time, not 10%, and the chances multiply: 0.95 to the power of 10 is about 60%, not 10%."},"7":{"0":"99% approvals don't prove the assistant is right, only that nobody is reading. Dropping approval removes the last line of defense.","1":"A monthly reminder won't change the pattern. Improve how changes are displayed and measure review time and rejection rate.","2":"Approval time is actually the key signal: two seconds almost always means no real review, that is, automation bias."},"8":{"1":"An answer based on similar projects looks helpful but is really a polite hallucination. The user will trust information that doesn't exist.","2":"An error message pushes the problem onto the user. The issue is missing information, not the question's wording or language.","3":"An uncalibrated confidence percentage gives a false sense of precision. Better to say plainly nothing was found and offer a next step."},"9":{"0":"A prompt instruction is a reminder, not a safeguard: it can be bypassed or lost in an edit. Permissions are enforced in code.","2":"A judge can miss things, and the data has already reached the model. What the model never received, it cannot expose.","3":"Training on private data only raises the risk of leaking it. The defense is filtering by permissions before data reaches the model."},"10":{"0":"A hallucination is invented information; here a malicious instruction hides in content. Lowering temperature reduces randomness, not manipulation.","1":"Retraining on recent data does not stop a malicious instruction hidden in content the model reads. This is a prompt injection risk.","2":"Rebalancing training data is irrelevant here. The danger is hostile text inside a task, so you limit tool permissions and approve sensitive actions."},"11":{"0":"The prompt is part of the product: it sets tone, boundaries and behavior. Changes to it go through versions, review and regression checks like code.","1":"You may change a prompt after launch, just not without testing. The problem here is changing it without evals and review.","3":"A shorter prompt is not necessarily more accurate. In the case, the cut dropped a line that was hiding a permissions bug."},"12":{"0":"Removing the feature for everyone punishes the 95% who don't drive most of the cost. Address the few, don't cancel for all.","1":"A flat increase looks fair but punishes most users for a few. A quota with overage payment targets those who drive the cost.","3":"A cheap model with no quality check damages every user's trust. The problem sits with 5%, so the fix should be targeted."},"13":{"1":"More conversations can come from frustration: users asking again because they got no answer. A count alone can't tell value from frustration.","2":"A failure cannot be concluded from this number alone either. You need to check successful tasks, repeat use and negative feedback.","3":"The feature's north star is successful tasks per active user, not conversations. A conversation target could reward frustration."},"14":{"0":"Even an expensive judge doesn't check permissions nobody asked it to check. It measures only the criterion it was given.","2":"There is no sign of a language issue. The judge was asked whether the answer relied on the sources, and it did.","3":"The judge rated three answers that exposed private tasks, so such questions did exist. The problem is the criterion didn't cover permissions."}},"28":{"0":{"0":"Waiting means learning the team under pressure, instead of knowing in advance where the first request might get stuck.","1":"A roadmap document talks about you and your priorities; it teaches you nothing about what each team is measured on or worries about.","3":"A list of opponents turns colleagues into rivals. Most no answers come from protecting a metric, not from opposing the product."},"1":{"1":"Legal drafts contracts, not security controls. A customer's security question needs whoever actually holds security.","2":"A reasonable-sounding answer that isn't accurate becomes a commitment to the customer. Even without a CISO, someone owns the area.","3":"Opening a position doesn't answer the customer now. In a small company you look for who owns the area, not for a title."},"2":{"0":"DORA itself warns that targets on its metrics invite gaming. A measure that becomes a target stops being a good measure.","2":"Nothing makes it drop necessarily: artificial splitting moves the number, not the quality. Speed and stability are read together.","3":"The risk is the opposite: people do respond to targets, so the number will rise. The question is whether real improvement rises with it."},"3":{"0":"SRE isn't stalling; it protects the availability it's measured on. Escalating to the CEO changes no one's metric.","1":"Guy acts on his metric, deals that close this quarter. Canceling the promise hurts his number and doesn't resolve the conflict.","2":"It isn't a personal clash but two metrics pulling in different directions. Mediation changes no one's metric."},"4":{"0":"Engineering managers are measured on predictable delivery and team health. Calling it personal preference misses the metric behind it.","2":"Cloud budget has nothing to do with a mid-sprint change. What gets hurt is the sprint commitment and the team's focus.","3":"One feature doesn't change the developer to QA ratio. What changes is that the sprint commitment breaks."},"5":{"0":"Commits are a single activity metric: they invite gaming and penalize uncounted work like code reviews and mentoring.","1":"Tickets closed is just another single activity metric. SPACE recommends combining dimensions, never ranking by one activity count.","3":"Logged hours measure presence, not impact. They're a single activity metric too, and the target moves the number, not the contribution."},"6":{"1":"At Google the error budget is a rule agreed in advance: when it's gone, releases slow down. No penalty is needed for it to apply.","2":"Tightening to 99.99% shrinks the budget tenfold. It isn't a punishment tool, and the PM sets the SLO together with SRE.","3":"The error budget doesn't transfer ownership of development. The team keeps building and invests in reliability until it recovers."},"7":{"0":"About 4.3 minutes matches 99.99%, one more nine. 0.1% of 43,200 minutes is about 43 minutes.","2":"About 7 hours matches 99%, a budget ten times larger. At 99.9% only about 43 minutes remain.","3":"Three days is about 10% of the month, roughly 90% availability. That's far from 99.9%."},"8":{"0":"The PM doesn't carry the pager. The team that built the service knows it best, so it carries on-call at first.","1":"A production service with no on-call puts customers at risk: a 2 a.m. failure waits until morning.","2":"SRE doesn't have to take every service. At Google a readiness review is the first step before SRE takes one on."},"9":{"0":"Checking by hand each morning is the heroism the DataOps Manifesto says to reduce, and it catches the error only after it happened.","1":"Banning changes paralyzes the product. A data contract allows change and stops only a breaking one before it reaches consumers.","3":"Faster refresh just shows a wrong number sooner. It doesn't prevent the break itself."},"10":{"0":"Competitors don't define the need. If users look once a week, fast refresh just costs more in compute and incidents.","2":"Choosing technology before knowing the need starts from capability. Freshness costs compute, complexity and incidents.","3":"The first question is whether 5 minutes is needed at all. Without knowing what users decide, there's no reason to demand it."},"11":{"1":"The problem isn't the study's length; the decision is already closed, so the study can't change anything.","2":"The frustration isn't about budget. Researchers are measured on impact, and a study confirming a closed decision can't have any.","3":"Researchers do take part in decisions; that's the very impact they're measured on: recommendations adopted and shipped."},"12":{"0":"FP&A is measured on budget variance and forecast accuracy. Spending without approval is the exact surprise they prevent, and it burns trust.","1":"Borrowing another team's budget without involving it burns trust, with that team and with Finance.","2":"FP&A must judge whether the spend is justified. Without a unit cost and a gate before expanding, they can't assess the variance."},"13":{"0":"General authorization doesn't remove notice: under GDPR Article 28, the processor must inform the customer and allow objection.","1":"A vendor's standard terms may let it use the data for its own purposes, which is why legal reviews them. Customer notice still applies.","3":"General authorization exists so a new contract isn't needed. It requires notice and a chance to object, not re-signing."},"14":{"1":"Lowering the bar hurts the team for a long time. What actually slows time to fill is the pace of interviews and feedback.","2":"Sourcing around talent breaks the process it owns, and doesn't speed up interviews or feedback.","3":"Waiting for a perfect candidate who meets every requirement stretches the search, and every day counts on talent's metric."}},"29":{"0":{"1":"An instant commitment over a dollar figure creates a promise that may not be kept, before you understand the problem or if it truly blocks.","2":"An automatic no breaks the relationship with sales and misses field intel. A request with a dollar sign is data, not a decision.","3":"An estimated date given to the customer becomes a promise, and if it isn't kept it surfaces only near the renewal."},"1":{"1":"Customer size has nothing to do with it. The pressure comes from one deal crossing the quota line and changing the AE's income.","2":"Commission isn't limited to the first quarter. Year-end pressure comes from the annual quota and the accelerators above it.","3":"AEs are paid on bookings, not on NRR. And a discount lowers revenue rather than raising it."},"2":{"0":"For CSMs the rule of thumb is 70:30 to 80:20 base to variable, so most of the pay is base.","1":"For SEs the rule of thumb is 70:30 to 80:20, and no pay is set mostly by the number of demos.","2":"Support pay is mostly base. CSAT is a metric support is measured on, not most of the pay."},"3":{"0":"The values are swapped: GRR excludes expansion, so it can never exceed 100%. Here it's 90%.","2":"92% and 107% come out if you forget the $20K of downgrades. A downgrade is lost revenue too.","3":"115% adds expansion without subtracting churn and downgrades. NRR is 1,000 minus 100 plus 150."},"4":{"0":"SDRs don't close deals; they create meetings and pipeline. And closing discounts aren't the PM's to give.","1":"SDRs aren't measured on satisfaction. What gets them replies is pains in the customers' own language.","2":"Product adoption concerns existing customers. SDRs reach new prospects and are measured on meetings and pipeline."},"5":{"1":"A demo of something that doesn't exist, made to look live, creates a hidden promise the customer will expect.","2":"Slides only weaken the demo and waste a discovery chance with a real customer. What exists can be shown on their own data.","3":"Evening work doesn't change the problem: a view that looks live but doesn't exist is still a hidden promise."},"6":{"0":"Scripts with no tests, docs or owner are a shadow product. Leaving them grows the risk even before anyone complains.","1":"Handing everything to engineering bloats the product with one-off code. Each script needs its own decision.","3":"That leaves the shadow product undecided: SEs aren't owners of code customers depend on over time."},"7":{"0":"Jumping to the feature assumes a product gap. But the champion left here, which may be an adoption problem.","2":"A discount is a commercial decision, not the PM's. And the cause has to be found first, not assumed to be price.","3":"Churn risk can come from a product gap, which is squarely product's concern. Find the cause before deciding who handles it."},"8":{"0":"In the common split the CSM owns adoption, health and value. Knowing the customer doesn't make them the commercial owner.","2":"Support resolves issues and is measured on response times and CSAT. Renewal and expansion negotiation isn't its job.","3":"The PM shapes the product, but the commercial renewal and expansion negotiation belongs to the account's commercial owner."},"9":{"1":"Reply time is support's operational metric. It doesn't show what in the product causes the tickets.","2":"Headcount and hours are support staffing data; they don't reveal where the product confuses or breaks.","3":"The noisy-customer list is a starting point, not the full picture. It shows no root causes and no trend."},"10":{"0":"The team's skill isn't the issue: the same work repeats for every customer, which signals a missing product capability.","1":"What repeats in every implementation is a product backlog in disguise. An import tool would shorten time to first value.","3":"Raising the price keeps the shadow product of scripts. The shared goal is more ARR, not more hours."},"11":{"0":"People's memories are biased, and reconstruction gives no data you can sum in dollars and track over time.","2":"A general market survey won't tell you why you lost specific deals. The missing data lives in the CRM.","3":"A revenue report by quarter and region holds no loss reasons, so it can't show deals lost over the integration."},"12":{"0":"Partner involvement doesn't make a deal sourced. In the second, the opportunity already existed and the partner helped advance it.","1":"It's reversed: in the first the partner brought the opportunity; in the second it helped one the AE had already opened.","2":"Partners count when they bring or advance an opportunity, not only when they sign. Pay is set by that classification too."},"13":{"0":"Sunday is the weekend in New York. The real overlap is Monday to Thursday.","1":"The hour suits New York, but Friday is the weekend in Israel.","3":"23:00 burns out the Israeli team, and most decisions can go in writing by morning anyway."},"14":{"0":"Pulling the beta forward solved this case, but it doesn't prevent the next promise from passing with no owner.","1":"The CIO call handled one customer. The root cause was promises passed hand to hand with no record and no owner.","2":"The demo avoided a hidden promise in one deal, but created no mechanism that records every commitment for the CSM."}},"30":{"0":{"0":"Backlog priority is day-to-day work for product teams, within the policy the board set.","1":"Price negotiation with a customer is day-to-day management for the CEO and leadership, not a power Section 92 reserves to the board.","2":"Choosing cloud vendors and tools is a daily operational call for management, not a statutory board duty."},"1":{"0":"A director wears two hats, but as a director the duties of care and loyalty run to the company and all shareholders, not the fund.","1":"Founders get no priority in fiduciary duties. The director owes them to the company and all shareholders, founders among them.","2":"Employees matter, but a director's fiduciary duties run to the company and all shareholders, not to one group."},"2":{"0":"An annual budget and hiring plan almost always need board approval; they are the plan the board approves.","1":"Equity grants, including a bigger option pool and new-hire options, almost always need board approval.","3":"Raising money, equity or credit, is among the decisions that almost always need board approval."},"3":{"1":"A dated feature list creates commitments, and the board doesn't manage the roadmap.","2":"Screenshots say nothing about the plan. The board needs to know whether product advances the approved goals.","3":"Story points are an internal team metric. They show no metric against target and no link to company goals."},"4":{"0":"$260K is the bookings: the whole contract with the one-time fee. ARR counts only recurring revenue at an annual rate.","1":"$240K is two years of subscription. ARR is an annual rate, so it gets divided by two.","3":"$10K a month is the MRR. ARR is that recurring revenue at an annual rate."},"5":{"0":"Inference cost incurred on every use is COGS, not R&D. It raises COGS to $6.","2":"90% counts only the $2 of AI and forgets the $4 of cloud, support and services.","3":"The salaries of developers who build the feature are R&D, not COGS, so they don't enter gross margin."},"6":{"1":"Dividing by total expenses is gross burn, a scenario where revenue disappears. Worth knowing, but it isn't the runway.","2":"Dividing by revenue says nothing about the burn rate. Runway is based on net burn: expenses minus collections.","3":"Runway is cash divided by net burn. The time fundraising takes affects when you start raising, not the calculation."},"7":{"0":"In a 30-day month, 99.9% allows about 43 minutes and 99.95% only about 22. That's half, often with duplicated infrastructure.","2":"Service credits are paid only when you miss the target, not as a fixed monthly penalty.","3":"It's as much a product and economic call as a legal one: it needs infrastructure and on-call that land in COGS, so it must be priced."},"8":{"0":"Across-the-board cuts also hit what creates value. If usage rose 60%, a 30% rise in the bill is actually an improvement.","1":"Centralizing decisions in finance cuts engineers out, while FinOps is built on engineering and finance working together.","2":"A long commitment pays off only on stable usage you already understand. Here the cause of the rise is still unknown."},"9":{"0":"Team preferences are a consideration, not a basis. You build when the capability differentiates, after counting the full cost.","1":"Comparing a license with two weeks of development forgets maintenance, security and updates over the years.","3":"What competitors did is a consideration, not a basis. Billing is usually shared infrastructure, so the default leans to buying."},"10":{"1":"The board doesn't interview candidates. It approves the budget and the hiring plan, and an extra hire touches them.","2":"There's no such legal rule. A hire touches the budget because each employee is a recurring monthly commitment.","3":"New engineers do need ramp-up time, but that's not why the decision touches the budget. The recurring cost is."},"11":{"0":"Commit should hold only fairly certain deals. One that depends on an unapproved capability isn't there, however well the rep knows the customer.","1":"The deal isn't lost, just uncertain. It moves from Commit to Best Case, not out of the forecast.","2":"Adding a capability to the roadmap to fix a forecast is exactly a roadmap written by deals."},"12":{"1":"Being on the roadmap is not a date. A clause like this turns a product promise into revenue risk.","2":"There's no basis to assume the customer won't be loyal. When a commitment is truly needed, there's a path: check, price, deal desk.","3":"Growing the team to meet any date adds recurring burn and still leaves the promise as revenue risk."},"13":{"0":"Deal size alone doesn't decide. One customer's request is a data point; a theme appears when it recurs and fits strategy.","2":"Building all three is exactly a roadmap written by deals. One-off requests get alternatives: a partner, a workaround or a covering price.","3":"Blanket refusal ignores valuable field signal. When many deals ask for one capability in the strategy's direction, it's a theme."},"14":{"0":"Day-to-day project decisions belong to management, not the board. The pause waited because it wasn't needed to meet the target.","2":"Pausing the bet does save about $45K a month. It waited because it costs future growth and wasn't needed.","3":"No fund objection held it back: the three other levers brought gross margin to about 76%, more than the board asked for."}},"31":{"0":{"1":"Individual OKRs address direction, but the problem is when and where you check and decide. They don't separate reporting from deciding.","2":"Splitting into two forums doubles the meetings without moving status into writing. Decisions still get pushed.","3":"A 1:1 with each manager removes the shared place where issues get decided. The problem is rhythm, not personal contact."},"1":{"0":"A new company goal neither maps nor coordinates the dependencies. It's a structure problem: who works with whom, on what.","1":"Faster decisions don't help when the teams don't know they depend on each other. The dependencies must be drawn first.","3":"A weekly steering committee adds coordination instead of reducing it, and still doesn't map the dependencies in advance."},"2":{"0":"An OKR doesn't say who has the final say on price. The problem is that nobody knows if it was decided, or by whom.","2":"A longer meeting doesn't set who decides, and without a decision log, the agreement will be forgotten next month too.","3":"A new team is a big structural change, and it still doesn't say who approves a price change or where it's recorded."},"3":{"0":"A reorganization chooses no goals. Ten top-priority goals are a wish list: this is a direction problem.","1":"Individual OKRs tied to a bonus push people toward safe goals (Steven Kerr, 1975), and don't shrink the wish list.","2":"A daily status meeting chooses nothing for anyone; it only adds reporting. What's missing is a few clear company goals."},"4":{"0":"A monthly spec review doesn't replace the conversation. The review surprise built up in every talk where nobody spoke up.","2":"DACI defines who decides, not who gives feedback. The problem here is feedback that wasn't given in time.","3":"Moving him to another team just moves the problem. What's missing is a regular conversation and specific, timely feedback."},"5":{"1":"The risk the method card warns of isn't writing time; when the score sets money, people set easy goals.","2":"The opposite is expected: when the score sets money, people set goals that are easy to hit, not ambitious ones.","3":"Google and John Doerr explicitly recommend separating OKRs from pay. A bonus link rewards one thing while hoping for another."},"6":{"0":"There's no automatic extension: that's the circuit breaker that stops endless projects. The cool-down isn't for that either.","1":"Adding people recalls Brooks's Law: more people on a late project delay it further, through coordination and onboarding.","3":"Continuing because you've already invested is a sunk cost. In Shape Up time is fixed, and the problem goes back to shaping first."},"7":{"0":"According to Jeremiah Lee, a former Spotify PM, the model was never fully implemented there, so there's nothing to copy as is.","1":"The problems weren't the names: chapter leads had no delivery accountability, and there was no shared cross-team process.","2":"The stage doesn't tie the model to media companies. The lesson is the question of keeping both autonomy and alignment."},"8":{"0":"In RACI, Informed is only kept up to date, while counsel must confirm. And RACI is less sharp on who has the final say.","2":"Here someone must confirm legal compliance and someone decides. Consent passes a proposal absent reasoned objection; it suits experiments.","3":"In DACI the VP Product would be the Approver, not a Contributor. And a Driver moves the decision forward; it doesn't confirm legality."},"9":{"1":"Rewording with a way back is a two-way door: it can be decided quickly, by one person or a small group.","2":"A time-boxed A/B test is reversible by design, so it's a two-way door to decide quickly.","3":"A feature flag exists precisely so it can be turned off, so the decision is reversible: a two-way door."},"10":{"0":"Silence in the room and objection in the chat are the very sign of unclear decision rights. Bezos expects it said openly.","1":"Full consensus isn't the condition. Whoever isn't convinced says so, then commits to the Approver's decision.","2":"Raising it at every meeting reopens a decision already made. After voicing the objection, you commit."},"11":{"0":"In Consult the decision is still the manager's: she listens, then decides. Here Yaara decides alone.","1":"In Sell the manager decides and persuades. Here the decision is entirely Yaara's; Michal only wants to know afterwards.","2":"In Agree they decide together. Here Michal isn't part of the decision; she only hears the outcome."},"12":{"1":"Maturity belongs to the task, not the person: engineering seniority isn't interview experience. Goal-and-check suits task maturity.","2":"Withholding the task develops no one. Start with closer support and loosen as her performance on this task improves.","3":"A course alone doesn't replace support on the job. Task maturity builds through doing, with close direction at first."},"13":{"0":"Per Meyer both are egalitarian in leading, but on negative feedback Israel is direct and the US more indirect. There it sounds like an attack.","1":"Skipping the objection on the call gives up the candor. Keep the content and directness; change only the wrapping.","3":"Passing it to the CEO doesn't fix the style gap and takes the PM out of the talk. What needs changing is the wrapping, not the speaker."},"14":{"0":"Rolling the gap into the performance review is exactly what the stage warns against. Re-plan the goals openly.","2":"Postponing every decision freezes the team for five weeks. A deputy and a handover keep knowledge out of one head.","3":"Remote approvals during service keep knowledge in one head and don't respect the service. That's what a deputy is for."}},"32":{"0":{"0":"Sharing documents is enthusiasm, not power. A champion is tested by action, and one who won't reach the decision maker acts like a coach.","1":"Dropping the request leaves the EB unknown to the end. The right step is to ask again, with material that helps her present.","3":"Going around her secretly damages the trust of your inside helper. Better to ask again and equip her with material."},"1":{"0":"The company story is about you, not him. You bring the EB a business outcome: his problem in his numbers and the cost of no change.","1":"A tour of the screens suits users, not the person deciding whether to move budget.","3":"Logos build trust, but they're secondary proof at best. Without his problem and its cost, he has no reason to listen."},"2":{"0":"The settings screen takes her away from her problem. She wants to see the outcome, not the architecture.","1":"A climax at the end sounds logical, but by minute 18 half the room is on their phones. Start with the result she wants.","2":"A free mouse from minute one takes her away from her problem. Show the result first, then the short path to it."},"3":{"0":"Putting the competitor down hurts your credibility and doesn't uncover what's behind the objection.","1":"Promising next quarter closes the topic fast, but it's a commitment with no discovery and no approval.","3":"A table of the competitor's weaknesses doesn't answer which problem it solves for them, and talks about the rival instead of the need."},"4":{"1":"A full list is too long to glance at mid-call. A usable battlecard is short enough to skim and gives ready wording.","2":"Hiding the competitor's strengths blows up in front of the customer, and sellers stop trusting the card.","3":"A battlecard has to stay updated. One that never changes goes stale, which is why it carries a verified date."},"5":{"0":"The loss reason field tends to say price: buyers soften criticism and sellers don't grade themselves. Diagnose first.","2":"CRM data is a starting point, not garbage. You don't ignore it; you check it against buyer interviews.","3":"A targeted discount sounds cautious, but it fixes a problem that hasn't been diagnosed yet."},"6":{"1":"Decision criteria cover how a vendor is chosen. A security questionnaire and data addendum come after the decision, on the way to signing.","2":"Metrics define the business outcome the customer wants. They don't map procurement, legal and security.","3":"The decision process is also about process, but it ends at the decision. Here the delay came after the yes, on the way to signing."},"7":{"0":"A demo and an upgrade offer turn the forum into a sale. A CAB exists to learn from customers, with them talking most of the time.","1":"Collecting wish lists sounds like listening, but promising dates in the room turns the forum into a marketplace.","2":"A funding deck turns the meeting into selling the vendor, and gives customers no chance to challenge the plan."},"8":{"0":"A feeling doesn't replace evidence. An EB nobody has met is an assumption, so the deal can't stay in Commit.","2":"A numeric fix to the forecast doesn't replace evidence. The review checks each deal by the evidence behind its color.","3":"A champion who will present to leadership with no EB access is exactly what MEDDPICC exists to expose."},"9":{"0":"Price and discounts are commercial roles. The PM adds value on letters where product knowledge matters: pain, metrics, criteria, competition.","1":"The forecast and what goes into Commit belong to the commercial side, not the PM.","2":"The paper process looks like orderly work to take on, but it belongs to the AE and the commercial roles."},"10":{"0":"A bigger environment doesn't solve it: a POC without criteria is a sandbox that never ends in a decision.","2":"A discount doesn't define what success means. Without criteria, an owner and a date, the POC won't lead to a decision.","3":"A longer schedule sounds generous, but it only stretches an evaluation with no target."},"11":{"0":"Showing it as existing helps close, but the customer will find out, and the whole demo's credibility will suffer.","1":"Dedicated code for one demo is maintenance with no value, and the stage rules out special in-product code for a demo.","3":"There's no need to hide every direction: a future screen is fine when marked as a direction, PM-approved and undated."},"12":{"0":"One customer's logo color is a single request. It gets a workaround or a reasoned no, not discovery.","2":"A capability that already exists needs better demoing and maybe UX work, not discovery of something new.","3":"An integration for one customer who plans to replace the system is a single request, not a pattern: workaround or reasoned no."},"13":{"0":"AE approval and a customer-picked date don't check cost, capacity or product fit. Those are exactly what's missing.","1":"VP Sales approval sounds senior but doesn't replace checking cost, capacity and fit. A competitor's date isn't an estimate.","2":"A verbal estimate and a champion's word aren't discovery, a qualified deal or product leadership approval."},"14":{"1":"The reason wasn't a blanket policy: six developer-weeks in a deal with gaps on the EB and metric was a gamble.","2":"Laura stayed the champion and proved it through action. The meeting was to learn whether the sync mattered to the EB.","3":"The EB doesn't approve every technical decision. The meeting closed a gap in the map: nobody knew if the sync mattered to him."}},"33":{"0":{"0":"Lead source matters only once you know the leads are good. An MQL is someone who showed interest, not a buyer.","2":"Cost per lead matters only once you know the leads are good. A cheap lead from the wrong segment is worth nothing to the business.","3":"Opens and clicks show interest in content, not whether leads became opportunities or came from the right audience."},"1":{"0":"The opposite: share of voice above market share tends to come with growth over time, so it isn't necessarily waste.","1":"Binet and Field found a link: on average about 0.5% a year of share gain for every 10 points of excess voice.","3":"The research points the other way: a brand more visible than its share tends to grow, not shrink."},"2":{"0":"Views show exposure, not understanding. They don't say whether buyers got the message.","1":"Salespeople's satisfaction matters, but it isn't what the customer understood.","2":"Coverage on news sites is exposure, not understanding. The strong signal is buyers repeating the message on their own."},"3":{"0":"A trend isn't a category. Creating one is costly and slow; Redgate, for example, joined a trend without inventing a category.","1":"When everyone says AI, the word itself doesn't differentiate. What does is what the capability does that alternatives don't, with proof.","3":"Ignoring the trend misses an important answer to why now."},"4":{"1":"Dunford warns that category creators often lose, in the long run, to those who enter after them.","2":"The main risk isn't trademark registration but teaching the market a new problem and frame, which takes years and budget.","3":"Teaching a market a new category takes years and budget, not just a year of analyst recognition."},"5":{"0":"The most advanced and smartest is a general claim without proof: puffery that won't convince a skeptical buyer.","1":"Early warning is a pillar, a theme that supports the promise. Proof is something checkable, like a figure with a source and size.","2":"See what's stuck is the roof, the value proposition. It holds no figure, quote or case you can check."},"6":{"0":"The PMM leads messaging, but the PM owns the product truth and can, and should, stop an untrue claim.","1":"The PM doesn't write the slide. The PM's role is to bring the data and limits; messaging stays with the PMM.","2":"What persuades doesn't make a claim true. Predicts every delay is a claim the product can't back up."},"7":{"0":"The tier sets marketing resources, not who gets the feature. A T1 launch can, and usually should, roll out gradually.","2":"Gradual rollout isn't reserved for T3. Tier and rollout are two separate axes.","3":"The tier is set by market and business impact, not by how many accounts receive the feature."},"8":{"0":"Release notes are a factual record of what changed, not marketing. The narrative tells why it matters to the customer.","1":"Every launch needs release notes, and mainly T1 and T2 launches also need a narrative. That's the reverse of this.","2":"Release notes record what changed; the narrative explains what changed for the customer. Neither is a price list or bug list."},"9":{"1":"Every new name is one more word to learn. Brand names are kept for what's sold separately or changes the story.","2":"A feature inside the product gets a name that says what it does. A witty name doesn't tell the customer that.","3":"An English-only name ignores the Hebrew UI and glossary."},"10":{"1":"Descriptive names aren't banned in marketing. The issue is that they're weak trademarks, registrable only under certain conditions.","2":"Length isn't the point. The feature vs product difference is protection: a descriptive name is clear but hard to protect.","3":"Translation isn't the issue. A descriptive name is a weak trademark, so it suits a product built for years less well."},"11":{"0":"A literal translation can sound stiff or read differently, as happened to Velox in the case. And English testing isn't Hebrew testing.","2":"A Hebrew page needs a message that works in Hebrew. A message tested in English hasn't been tested with Israeli buyers.","3":"Machine translation plus a spelling check stays literal: it doesn't check feeling or goal, and isn't tested with buyers."},"12":{"0":"At about 60 conversions a month an A/B test won't reach significance in reasonable time; a one-week result is mostly noise.","1":"Colleagues already know what you meant, so they can't reveal whether an outside buyer understands the message.","3":"Search volume for a keyword doesn't test whether buyers understand the message, or why."},"13":{"0":"The Consumer Protection Law does apply mainly to consumers, but B2B has other tools: the Commercial Torts Law and contract law.","2":"The Commercial Torts Law prohibits a false description of your own product too, not just a competitor's.","3":"A claim in a sales deck can be enough: the Contracts Law allows cancelling a contract entered because of misleading."},"14":{"0":"A fixed if-then rule isn't AI. An AI label on a capability with no model is AI washing, and it misleads customers.","1":"The problem isn't design but misleading. A customer who finds the AI is a fixed rule stops believing the real capabilities too.","3":"The FTC made clear there's no AI exemption from the law, including for business customers, not just regulated finance."},"15":{"0":"Without knowing who you sell to and what you're compared with, any headline is a guess. The landing page comes from the ICP and positioning.","2":"Yaara found customers actually compare with IT team scripts, so an early card against Zapier would target the wrong alternative.","3":"A video that grabs attention still needs a message. Without an ICP and positioning, its content is a guess."},"16":{"1":"A customer name isn't the issue. The text describes a person, not an organization, so it's a persona, not an ICP.","2":"An ICP isn't one word: it describes industry, size, structure, trigger and when not to sell.","3":"ICP and persona are different documents: organization vs person in the deal. Mixed, sales reaches the right people in the wrong organizations."},"17":{"0":"A T1 launch needs pages, sales enablement and customer stories a PM alone can't produce without giving up discovery.","1":"Cancelling a ready product's launch gives up customer value. You can ask for resources or lower the tier openly.","3":"T1 in the deck, T3 in practice is the worst of all: leadership expects results nobody planned to deliver."},"18":{"0":"Buyers soften criticism in front of the person who sold to them, so someone outside the deal should ask.","1":"A yearly survey comes too late and mostly hears from the happy ones.","2":"Wins show what really tipped the decision. Without them you don't know what to keep in the message and product."},"19":{"0":"More capabilities lengthen a list nobody reads. Buyers need to see at a glance whether the plan fits and how it differs.","2":"Platinum still doesn't say who the plan is for or what you get. Same problem, different name.","3":"Hiding the price sometimes suits large deals, but it doesn't solve a problem of understanding the plans."},"20":{"0":"Spare budget isn't a reason to hire. The real sign is a workload that crowds out discovery.","1":"What a competitor does isn't a reason. Hiring a PMM comes from your own workload and real need.","3":"A website redesign is a job for a designer or an agency, not necessarily a PMM."},"21":{"0":"An employee survey on colors doesn't reveal why customers leave. The cause is usually in the experience, not the logo.","2":"Logo cost and timing jump to execution before checking the cause. A rebrand doesn't fix a broken promise.","3":"What competitors did doesn't explain your churn. And a rebrand can reset distinctive assets already built."},"22":{"0":"A rise in conversion alone doesn't show what happens next: cancellations, complaints and trust.","1":"A blanket rejection gives up the learning. Guardrails and an on-brand version test whether you can get both.","3":"Growth vs brand tension is expected, and the PM decides it with data and the promise. It isn't to be handed off."},"23":{"1":"Immediate absorption gives up brand equity you paid for, when the audience and promise differ.","2":"Full independence forever is expensive and transfers no trust from Velox.","3":"A brand-new name erases what customers already know, and transfers no trust from Velox."},"24":{"0":"Followers are short-term exposure. Brand equity is the difference the name makes to the decision and the price.","1":"Clicks in the week after a conference show brief exposure, not what the market remembers.","2":"A feature count measures output, not the name's effect on buyers' decisions."},"25":{"1":"Binet showed a link in three consumer categories in 2020, not in every category. In B2B, with few searches, it's noisy.","2":"Binet did find a link between share of search and share of market. In B2B it's noisy, so use it as a trend rather than reject it.","3":"A one-off comparison with an average from a post ignores the method. Compare yourselves with yourselves over time."}},"34":{"0":{"1":"What competitors do is a consideration, not the test. A good unit aligns with value, is predictable and grows with the customer.","2":"Billing convenience is a consideration, not the test. Project fails here because customers will merge projects to save.","3":"Immediate revenue is a consideration, not the test. A unit that doesn't reflect value backfires as customers adapt to it."},"1":{"0":"Unlimited exposes gross margin to the heavy tail: the cost rises with every action, and the price doesn't.","1":"Usage-only pricing creates an unpredictable bill, exactly what the buyers don't want.","2":"Outcome pricing needs an agreed definition of done. When the agent decides that itself, it invites arguments."},"2":{"1":"Inside Pro it would raise costs and lower the package's value for most customers, who don't want it.","2":"Dropping it misses the 15% willing to pay a lot. You drop only when both interest and willingness to pay are low.","3":"In Free the expensive capability only makes non-paying users costlier."},"3":{"0":"The PM knows the product but can't see the whole picture alone: finance brings floors and margins, sales the field. A big change is the CEO's call.","2":"Finance brings floors and margins but not the unit, packaging and product usage. No one alone sees it all.","3":"Sales brings the field, but the unit and packaging are what product answers for. Building the product around a price is backwards."},"4":{"0":"Transparency isn't the point: consumers must see the total price only, in Israeli currency with VAT, and dollars don't meet that.","2":"A footnote isn't enough: Section 17D prohibits showing a consumer any price other than the total price only.","3":"The law doesn't require a free trial. The problem is how the price is shown: total, in shekels, with VAT."},"5":{"0":"More people say yes at $20, but the product is 12, below the 12.5 at $25.","1":"The highest price doesn't maximize revenue: $30 times 35% is 10.5, the lowest of the three.","2":"The four questions belong to Van Westendorp, a different method for a price range. Here you just multiply price by buyer share."},"6":{"0":"Cutting price without a diagnosis gives up revenue. Interviews often reveal another reason, like value that wasn't proven.","1":"A survey of existing customers doesn't ask the people who didn't buy, so it can't show why you lost.","3":"Routing approvals to the CEO doesn't answer why we lost."},"7":{"0":"A reverse trial has no prepayment. Users start with paid capabilities for a limited time, then drop to a free plan.","1":"It's the other way round: users start with the paid capabilities, and drop to the free plan at the end.","3":"There's a real difference: a regular trial ends access, while a reverse trial keeps users on a free plan."},"8":{"1":"When a free user is expensive, generous freemium funds users who may never pay.","2":"Ads don't solve the cost problem, and an unlimited free plan only makes it bigger.","3":"Full price with no trial adds a barrier, just when value is felt only after a few days of setup."},"9":{"0":"85% ignores the credits. Inference cost incurred on every use is part of COGS, up to another $1.20.","1":"Inference cost incurred on every use is COGS, not a development expense, so it enters gross margin.","3":"Credit packs are sold separately and bring their own revenue, so they don't lower the seat's gross margin."},"10":{"0":"Cutting the price so nobody notices gives up revenue and doesn't help customers understand what burns credits.","1":"An unlimited that isn't unlimited damages trust. Credits usually fail on the explanation, not the numbers.","3":"A quiet change for some customers damages trust. At Cursor, surprise charges led to an apology and refunds."},"11":{"0":"Automatic approval teaches the market that the price list isn't real.","1":"A blanket no misses good deals. A deep discount goes up to a level that sees the whole picture, with a give-get.","2":"Promising a feature in return for a discount is exactly a roadmap written by deals."},"12":{"0":"$12.75 sees only the invoice and misses the leak: you're paid for only 9 of 12 months.","2":"Free months are revenue that never arrives. In the price waterfall they stack on top of the invoice discount.","3":"$11.25 counts only the free months and forgets the 15% discount. The two stack."},"13":{"0":"At 10% churn 90% of seats remain: 90% of $50 is $45, above $40. Revenue still rises.","2":"At 25% churn 75% remain: 75% of $50 is $37.50, already below $40. Breakeven is lower.","3":"At 50% half the seats remain: $25 instead of $40. Revenue starts falling long before that."},"14":{"1":"The law didn't forbid an increase. The split protected margin fast and gave long-time customers time to adjust to the price.","2":"The research didn't set $8 as a ceiling. Delaying the price was to reduce churn, not to keep it forever.","3":"Guy actually wanted to raise prices for everyone at once. The reason was separating margin protection from protecting veterans."}},"35":{"0":{"0":"Competitors matter, but without the buyer map you'll build on old habits. At a bank, signer, user and blocker are different people.","1":"Monthly retention is a consumer-world habit. At a bank you first need to know who signs, who uses it and who can stop it.","3":"At a bank compliance, procurement and legal can stop the deal. Talk-free onboarding ignores who actually decides."},"1":{"1":"Israel's Defense Export Control Law also covers the marketing that precedes an export, not just the shipment.","2":"The funding source doesn't exempt you. An exemption is confirmed with DECA, not assumed.","3":"An NDA doesn't exempt you from registration or a marketing license. Marketing without one is a real legal risk."},"2":{"0":"A deck is a promise. In defense tech, proven in the field beats any slide deck.","2":"Civilian downloads measure a different market, with other users and needs. The defense customer wants to see it work in real conditions.","3":"A happy consumer customer belongs to another market. A defense buyer looks for a field trial and procurement-ready TRL."},"3":{"0":"An MVP doesn't exempt you from a license. In fintech, what's allowed is a product question asked before the spec.","2":"A closed beta doesn't exempt you from a license. Holding others' money can make the company regulated from day one.","3":"After launch is too late: if a license is needed, the service is already running without one. Ask before the spec."},"4":{"0":"Encryption alone doesn't take you out of scope: whoever stores card data is still subject to PCI DSS.","1":"Email is exactly what the standard is meant to prevent. And the card data still reaches you.","3":"A public cloud doesn't take the responsibility off you. Anyone storing card data is subject to the standard."},"5":{"0":"MITRE publishes no ranking. The evaluations show how products detect attack steps, so number 1 isn't a valid claim.","1":"The evaluations do carry weight: they show how products detect attack steps. The limit is they aren't a ranking and coverage can hide noise.","2":"Full coverage can come with many false alerts. A CISO will want to see the noise level and a POC in their own environment."},"6":{"0":"In Gartner's survey most consolidate to improve risk posture, only a minority to save money. A discount misses the need.","1":"Ten weak modules don't make a platform. A point product survives by closing a painful gap and fitting what's already bought.","2":"Waiting leaves the product out while the CISO is already consolidating. What survives fits into the existing platforms."},"7":{"0":"An algorithm that predicts deterioration and steers a clinical decision is almost surely SaMD. Calling it wellness is a trap.","2":"An A/B test on patients is exactly one of the traps. Medical software builds evidence through structured clinical validation.","3":"Releasing to doctors before checking the regulatory path runs medical software with no clearance or clinical validation."},"8":{"0":"FDA clearance isn't automatically valid: you still need an Israeli AMAR registration, with declarations and documents.","1":"Israel actually relies on approvals from recognized countries like the US, so no new clinical study from scratch is needed.","2":"AMAR relies on approvals from recognized countries, the US included, not only on CE marking."},"9":{"1":"Opening to everyone creates an empty store. A marketplace without liquidity disappoints both sides, and no network effect starts.","2":"Most marketplaces Lenny Rachitsky studied handled supply first. Buyers who arrive at an empty store are disappointed.","3":"A take rate that's high too early invites leakage: the sides meet in the store and deal outside it."},"10":{"0":"Blocking contact details annoys both sides and gives them no reason to stay.","1":"Raising the take rate only increases leakage, because it raises the incentive to work outside the store.","3":"Ignoring it loses fees and data. Leakage signals the platform gives too little value after the introduction."},"11":{"0":"Whoever waits for the tender finds the threshold conditions too late, and misses the chance to shape practical requirements.","2":"Trying to get around the tender committee puts the bid at risk.","3":"The requirements aren't set yet. An RFI is the chance to help shape them and learn the threshold conditions, not to guess and build."},"12":{"0":"A checkout failure on the busiest day costs more than any conversion gain. That's why a change freeze precedes peak season.","1":"An A/B test at peak load still puts half the customers at risk of a checkout failure.","2":"Asking the customer to cancel the season simply isn't realistic. You postpone the change, not the season."},"13":{"1":"A roadmap in week one, before you understand the language and the money, is exactly what Watkins advises against.","2":"Copying from your last company ignores the regulation, risk and value specific to payments.","3":"Changes before you understand why things are done this way are exactly what Watkins advises against."},"14":{"0":"Agreeing on the spot would have taken on a second product line's fixed cost for one customer, when the real need was different.","1":"Refusing would have lost a deal that could be met: every real need was solved without a local install.","3":"A discount doesn't answer what the bank really needed: data in Israel, keys under its control and audit logs."}},"36":{"0":{"0":"Gathering all the events sounds thorough, but that's exactly what produces 40 charts with no purpose.","2":"A new tool doesn't say which decision the dashboard serves. Without one, there's just room for more unopened charts.","3":"A full dashboard from every table is the same wall of charts, only faster. First ask which decision is waiting."},"1":{"0":"The convention is an object then a past-tense action. A button name or number ties the event to the UI, not the action.","1":"An event per template scatters one action across dozens of names, and no funnel can be built on it. Details go in a property.","2":"A date in the name is a similar mistake: time is already recorded with the event, and a changing name splits one action."},"2":{"0":"Email is tempting because it identifies for certain, but it changes and it's personal data. Without workspace_id the event isn't counted.","1":"A session links one visit. A workspace-level metric is calculated from the workspace ID, not the visit.","3":"The anonymous ID is for the time before sign-up. Without the workspace ID, activation per workspace can't be calculated."},"3":{"0":"From the browser the event can be lost to ad blockers, and sent even when the invitation failed.","2":"Sending from both sides under the same name counts every invitation twice.","3":"A retry doesn't solve browser blocking, and the event still fires even when the invitation failed."},"4":{"0":"A merge rule in the analytics tool fixes one place and leaves the warehouse broken, as in the case.","1":"Renaming is allowed; it's just a new version of a contract: update, map, notify and test.","3":"Waiting for something to break pushes the problem onto consumers. Map both names and notify before the release."},"5":{"0":"Code that compiles and passes unit tests doesn't mean the event arrives with the right name, properties and IDs.","1":"A month after launch is too late: wrong data has been collected. Test before, and compare with the product DB a day after.","2":"Confirming the connection works doesn't check that each event arrives once, correctly named, and only on success."},"6":{"1":"Under the GDPR, pseudonymized data that can be linked back to a person still counts as data about an identifiable person.","2":"The issue isn't performance. It's personal data that can be linked back, and the metric doesn't need it anyway.","3":"The vendor's location doesn't make a hashed email anonymous. And the metric only needs how many invitations were sent and how."},"7":{"1":"The analytics tool is fast but sees only what was sent to it: not the support tickets and not billing.","2":"The dashboard shows activation by month and doesn't join support tickets and billing.","3":"A CRM report shows paying customers, not product events or tickets. The question crosses three sources."},"8":{"0":"COUNT(*) counts rows, that is events, not users. 12,400 are tasks, and 1,850 are the people.","1":"The query is on Task Created, not views. 12,400 are creation events, and 1,850 are distinct users.","3":"The gap looks like duplicates, but an active user simply appears in many rows, and that's perfectly fine."},"9":{"1":"A time zone shifts a few hours at the month's edges; it doesn't triple any count.","2":"Bots don't explain a count that grows by member count. A one-to-many join duplicates each workspace by its members.","3":"The duplication comes from the join in the query, not the load. Use COUNT(DISTINCT workspace_id) or aggregate members first."},"10":{"0":"Israel is two hours ahead of UTC in winter and three in summer, not a full day.","2":"Rounding doesn't explain it. The gap comes from month boundaries shifting a few hours between the two time zones.","3":"It sounds logical, but the month's edges move: workspaces from its first hours count in the previous month in UTC."},"11":{"1":"Known-bot lists don't know your QA automation. Tests have to be flagged by you.","2":"Employees test, demo and experiment, which skews onboarding data. Flag internal accounts and filter in the model.","3":"Deleting at collection sounds thorough, but what's deleted never comes back, even if the rule was wrong."},"12":{"0":"More charts enlarge the wall nobody opens. A dashboard gets opened when it's clear what to do with it.","1":"An email with 40 charts moves the same wall into the inbox. The problem is purpose, not channel.","2":"Replacing the tool is tempting when the interface seems to blame, but a new tool doesn't solve a problem of purpose."},"13":{"0":"A freshness problem would hit only the latest months, not create a constant gap across all of them.","1":"A time zone affects only the period's edges and doesn't explain a jump in the middle.","2":"There's no such rule. A constant gap suggests a definition or filter difference; a jump from a date suggests a change that day."},"14":{"0":"Asking the assistant to explain sounds responsible, but a model's confidence isn't evidence. Check the SQL and a known number.","2":"The same answer twice can be the same mistake twice. The case's duplication was caught only against a known total.","3":"A neat, full table can still count duplicated rows. Check unit, population and clock in the SQL."}},"37":{"0":{"0":"A demo looks like progress, but it shows the cases that suit the vendor. Without a decision and a metric there's nothing to compare against.","1":"Website coverage is measured on the vendor's population. Without the decision, you don't know which fields or what quality you need.","2":"A contract with no defined need doesn't know what to protect. First define the decision, the metric and the value per point."},"1":{"1":"80% really was measured, but a match rate doesn't say whether the answer is correct. Effective coverage is match times accuracy.","2":"An average of match rate and accuracy is meaningless. You multiply them: 0.80 times 0.84.","3":"The 95% was measured on a different population. What counts is what was measured on Velox's records."},"2":{"0":"A profile for a closed company is an error, not an advantage. The vendor is inventing values or not updating.","2":"50 trap records are enough to expose the problem. Six full profiles for companies that don't exist are already a finding.","3":"Trap records don't test speed. They test whether the vendor returns data on companies that no longer exist."},"3":{"0":"An average can hide a segment that collapsed. That's why the per-segment threshold was set as a gate: whoever fails is out.","1":"A required form question brings back exactly the signup friction you wanted to avoid.","2":"A discount lowers the price but doesn't fix coverage. Israel still fails the gate."},"4":{"0":"Comparing found with not found is biased: large, well-known companies get found more often, and they differ anyway.","1":"Comparing with last year mixes in seasonality and everything that changed since. Only a random split neutralizes it.","3":"Salespeople's feeling isn't a measurement, and it doesn't isolate the effect of enrichment."},"5":{"0":"A volume discount also makes wrong records cheaper. A wrong record costs twice: when bought and when decided on.","1":"Prepaying only increases your dependency on the vendor before any value has been proven.","3":"A higher minimum only increases dependency before value is proven, and doesn't tie payment to correct records."},"6":{"1":"Break-even is 54 divided by 20, that is 2.7 points, not 9. Even the interval's lower bound, 3, is above it.","2":"An interval that excludes zero signals a significant result, not doubt. And its conservative end is already above break-even.","3":"Calculating on gross profit is the more conservative choice, and it still pays back. Revenue isn't needed to reach Go."},"7":{"0":"The license decides what's allowed, not our interpretation. An internal model is still training, and the license forbids training.","1":"Trial data is usually limited to testing only, so training on it isn't a way out.","2":"Hiding the source only makes the breach worse. The right way is a written add-on, including what happens to the model at the end."},"8":{"0":"This is the sunk-cost trap: the earlier investment won't come back whether you continue or not.","1":"For now sounds cautious, but it easily turns into a year of payments to a vendor that already failed.","3":"A discount doesn't fix missing coverage. The vendor still failed the blind sample."},"9":{"0":"Privacy authorities stress that personal data is protected even when public. Visible online isn't a license for any purpose.","1":"Buy and delete means you already processed personal data you didn't need.","2":"The vendor's location doesn't exempt you from the privacy duties that come with personal data."},"10":{"1":"The vendor's customer count isn't the check. What must be asked is whether its database is registered, as required for passing data to others.","2":"Data age isn't the legal question here. After Amendment 13, a database selling data on over 10,000 people must be registered.","3":"The storage question sounds serious, but it's a different, security check. The first must-ask is whether the database is registered."},"11":{"0":"The cloud brand tempts you to think someone checked, but each product carries the vendor's agreement, and quality is yours to check.","2":"Every marketplace product comes with the vendor's agreement, on AWS the Data Subscription Agreement, which sets usage rights.","3":"No one checked for you. You check quality, source and usage rights just as in a direct purchase."},"12":{"0":"The renewal review does check everything, but it comes months too late.","1":"Salespeople don't see coverage, so a drop like this won't be caught quickly through them.","3":"A cost review doesn't measure quality, and quarterly is too slow anyway."},"13":{"1":"A long contract is tempting because of the discount, but it deepens the dependency instead of reducing it.","2":"The vendor's identifier as primary key ties every system to it, and switching becomes heavy work everywhere.","3":"Keeping data after the contract ends usually breaches the license. You need a deletion plan, not a copy."},"14":{"0":"Open doesn't mean unconditional: ODbL requires attribution and share-alike, and legal should check.","2":"ODbL actually allows commercial use, on condition of attribution and share-alike.","3":"ODbL's conditions are attribution and share-alike, not royalties per user."}},"38":{"0":{"0":"AI hasn't made engineering unnecessary: production code, architecture and security stay with engineering, even for small features.","1":"A beta label doesn't make unreviewed code safe. It still has no permissions, tests or owner.","3":"Giving up prototypes means giving up the fastest way to learn. A PM builds to learn, not to ship."},"1":{"0":"A beta label doesn't change that the data is exposed to unreviewed code with no permissions or monitoring.","2":"A time limit doesn't help: even for a week, customer data is exposed to code with no permissions, monitoring, tests or owner.","3":"A legal waiver doesn't change the risk: the data is still exposed to code nobody has reviewed."},"2":{"0":"Production data adds risk without teaching more. A usability question tests fine on fake data.","2":"A deck with explanations doesn't test independent understanding: Michal explains, and the manager does no task himself.","3":"Polish pulls the discussion toward colors instead of whether a manager figures out the plan alone."},"3":{"1":"Payments are building to earn: a mistake there is a financial incident, so it belongs to engineering even when small.","2":"Changing production data is building to earn. A mistake hurts real customers, so it belongs to engineering.","3":"A permissions change is building to earn: a mistake there is a security incident, so it belongs to engineering."},"4":{"0":"Works in the demo doesn't mean it has permissions, tests or an owner. The usability tests proved the idea, not the code.","2":"Developing alone moves production code to the PM. If some code is reused, that's the Tech Lead's call.","3":"The recordings are exactly what you keep: the code is thrown away, the learning and the prototype as a living spec stay."},"5":{"1":"In slide decks it's hard to find both the decision and its reasoning. A decision record keeps the why.","2":"A chat channel records the what with no context or trade-offs, and its reasoning is hard to find six months later.","3":"In a full transcript it's hard to find the decision and its reasoning. A one or two page record keeps the why."},"6":{"0":"Summaries lose the evidence along the way, and don't let you count how many interviews support each finding.","1":"A deck of ten insights loses the evidence and the other findings, and can't be searched or linked to future studies.","3":"Full recordings without tags are nearly impossible to search, and don't let you count support for a finding."},"7":{"1":"More information isn't a better answer: context is limited and noise lowers quality. And the model still won't know what was canceled.","2":"More documents without status and date don't tell the model which version is current or which decision was canceled.","3":"Access to every folder adds noise and bypasses permissions, which must be enforced before retrieval."},"8":{"0":"Chapters of one PRD depend on each other. Parallel agents that can't see each other make hidden decisions that don't fit.","1":"When each screen depends on the last, parallel agents make hidden decisions that don't fit together.","2":"For a short question an agent team only adds cost: such a system can use 15 times the tokens."},"9":{"0":"Checking only at the end misses errors that piled up along the way, when they're hard to trace.","2":"Agents don't replace the operator's checkpoints: the plan, a sample from each agent and what goes out.","3":"Checking every step cancels out the advantage of agents. Stopping at the points where errors cost most is enough."},"10":{"0":"A general request doesn't define success: no goal, no decision context and no definition of done.","1":"An impressive role doesn't define success. The agent needs a goal, context, constraints, an example and a definition of done.","3":"A long list of prohibitions without a goal confuses the agent instead of guiding it."},"11":{"0":"A model confirming itself doesn't prove accuracy. AI synthesis can invent quotes.","1":"A well-written report can still hold invented quotes. The important check is against the source, not the wording.","2":"A match with expectations doesn't prove accuracy. It's actually a reason to check twice."},"12":{"0":"This is the jagged frontier: similar-looking tasks can fall on opposite sides of the model's capability. One success doesn't generalize.","1":"Giving up entirely loses what AI does well, such as summarizing that you delegate and verify.","3":"Prioritizing is a decision the PM makes and signs. Checking only after a complaint hands the decision to the agent."},"13":{"0":"Deleting the history doesn't change that an unapproved tool gives no control over retention, training or data location.","1":"The problem isn't quality but customers' personal and commercial data going into an unapproved tool.","2":"A request in the chat doesn't change the tool's terms. There's still no control over retention, training or location."},"14":{"0":"A feeling isn't a measurement: in METR's trial experienced developers were 19% slower with AI, yet estimated they'd been 20% faster.","1":"Prompt count measures activity, not value. A target on it only grows the activity.","2":"One study of a specific group doesn't prove the tools slow everyone. Compare outcome metrics with a baseline."}},"39":{"0":{"0":"A profile looks like a first step, but without evidence there's nothing to amplify. A personal brand is reputation resting on work.","1":"Followers are at most an amplifier. Without work you can point to, there's nothing to talk to them about.","2":"Virality is at most an amplifier, and it brings a random audience. Reputation is built on evidence, not one post."},"1":{"0":"An opinion on everything looks like engagement, but it dilutes what people identify you with.","2":"Posts outside don't replace work managers see up close: a lead area, fast answers and closed loops.","3":"A name in an update doesn't build a recognized strength. An internal brand comes from leading one area and daily reliability."},"2":{"0":"A nameless we sounds modest, but it erases Tal's contribution.","1":"Claiming the work loses the team. Good credit is precise: what she did and what others did, by name.","3":"Disappearing erases Michal's part: the framing and the decisions. Precise credit includes both of them."},"3":{"0":"The sentence is accurate and sounds professional, but it's a topic, not a claim. There's nothing to dispute in it.","1":"It's a hot topic is a line nobody will argue with, so nobody will remember it. It has no claim, reason or conclusion.","3":"Worth learning is a line no one will dispute. A point of view is an arguable claim, with a reason and a practical conclusion."},"4":{"0":"LinkedIn publishes no formula. It describes categories of signals, so anyone selling an exact formula is guessing.","1":"LinkedIn describes identity, content and activity signals, dwell time included, not ranking by follower count.","2":"LinkedIn itself advises avoiding engagement bait like comment YES, so assuming it always works runs against its own advice."},"5":{"0":"A burst of ten posts then a break builds no repetition. Reputation comes from the same topic, same level, over time.","1":"Jumping between topics erases recognition with the niche, even if it sounds like a product experiment.","2":"Virality brings a random audience, and long silence erases the link between the name and the niche."},"6":{"0":"You write in the language of the audience you want. American security leaders won't read a post in Hebrew.","2":"A bilingual post looks like both, but it's long and tires out both audiences.","3":"Ease of writing matters less than who will read. The audience he wants reads English."},"7":{"0":"A general overview doesn't stand out and has no first-hand experience. Organizers want what the audience can apply.","1":"Presenting the product is a pitch. Reversim Summit's call for papers, for example, says marketing and sales talks aren't wanted.","2":"A general intro brings no first-hand experience, so it won't stand out against abstracts with a real case."},"8":{"1":"Likes are easy to compare, but Michal's popular post brought zero requests, and the technical one with few likes brought four.","2":"Followers are at most an amplifier. A personal brand exists to create opportunities and trust, so you measure requests.","3":"One post's impressions don't show whether opportunities came. Michal's popular post brought zero requests."},"9":{"0":"Writing from scratch every week wastes the good document that already exists. One idea can reach several audiences.","1":"The quiet month rarely comes, and a small steady rhythm beats a one-off effort.","3":"Polished design for one post a month slows the rhythm. Repetition builds reputation, not graphics."},"10":{"0":"The method is the value for the audience, and it doesn't expose a customer or its data.","2":"The lesson is exactly the value for the audience, and it exposes no customer or protected detail.","3":"A customer story the company already published is a safe source to link to."},"11":{"0":"A negative claim about another company without backing is a defamation risk, and sharpness drags the discussion off topic.","1":"Deleting legitimate criticism looks like hiding something. One factual reply signals confidence.","3":"Deleting the whole post looks like hiding, and gives up the content that built your reputation. One factual reply is better."},"12":{"1":"Your personal voice is the brand. When AI writes everything, the story, opinion and details are no longer hers.","2":"Uploading the full internal document promises accuracy, but it's a leak of internal information.","3":"LinkedIn doesn't ban AI use: it labels content with Content Credentials and limits automated comments."},"13":{"0":"Close friends are available and loyal, but they see the same world you do.","2":"Complete strangers are too far away. The study found the most moves came from moderately weak ties.","3":"The study looked at tie strength, not recruiters as a group. Most moves came from moderately weak ties."},"14":{"1":"A mentor isn't necessarily the direct manager. What counts is what she did: she used her influence to advance Michal.","2":"A sponsor needn't sit outside the company. Sharon, from inside, put Michal forward for a role: exactly sponsorship.","3":"Sponsorship is defined by what is done, using influence for someone, not by rank. Sharon did exactly that."}},"40":{"0":{"0":"Sample size doesn't turn an estimate about the future into evidence. The survey measures employers' expectations about 2030.","2":"The survey really was conducted and has data, so it isn't opinion. But it measures expectations about the future: a forecast.","3":"The publisher's prestige doesn't turn an estimate about the future into evidence. It gets checked against reality in 2030."},"1":{"0":"In this stage, as of means the data date, not the publication date. Four months is long in a fast-moving field.","2":"When someone checked the website says nothing about when the figure was measured. Readers need the collection date.","3":"The next survey's date doesn't say when today's numbers were measured. A figure carries its collection date."},"2":{"0":"Which tools were deployed is a later question. First you need to know what share of all layoffs this even is.","1":"The industry split doesn't say how big the number is. The base-rate question comes first: out of how many layoffs in total.","3":"Tool cost doesn't say whether a record is a large share. Per Challenger, only about 4.5% of 2025 layoffs were attributed to AI."},"3":{"0":"High exposure means some tasks can be sped up, not that the job disappears. A job is a bundle of tasks.","1":"Exposure is relevant: some tasks really will move to tools. The next step is to break the role down and see which.","2":"Exposure doesn't mean the role will vanish, so there's no reason to flee. Break it into tasks and build what grows."},"4":{"0":"The finding concerns young workers only: among experienced workers in the same occupations there's no gap.","2":"Experienced workers in those occupations show no gap, and the decline runs mainly through fewer young hires.","3":"The decline runs mainly through fewer hires, and the researchers call it description, not cause. By choice is an explanation the data doesn't show."},"5":{"1":"There's no basis for that split. The explanation is simpler: a posting keeps running even when nobody is hired for it.","2":"Postings and hires are two different measures and needn't move together. A posting can stay open for months.","3":"The number of postings actually rose, so companies do post. They just hire less than they post."},"6":{"0":"Klarna didn't drop the bot; it added people. The lesson is to measure quality alongside cost, not to rule out agents.","1":"Waiting for competitors teaches nothing. The lesson is to measure satisfaction, repeat contacts and escalations from day one.","2":"The CEO didn't say he miscalculated; he said cost was too dominant a factor, and the result was lower quality."},"7":{"1":"The Hype Cycle describes expectations of a technology, not blaming a cut on AI when it has other causes.","2":"Base rate is the proportion question, out of how many. It doesn't describe an explanation that hides falling revenue.","3":"Augmentation describes AI helping workers, not an excuse for a cut driven by falling revenue."},"8":{"1":"Writing test cases is a routine task moving to tools, so it's likely to shrink, not grow.","2":"Running regression is a routine task moving to tools, so it's likely to shrink.","3":"Script maintenance is a routine task moving to tools. What grows is testing systems whose answers vary."},"9":{"0":"An AI governance lead writes policy and assesses risk. The problem here is that nobody builds for the customer before signing.","1":"Agent Ops runs agents in production. Velox's problem is custom integrations that must be built for customers.","2":"Velox isn't missing specs; it's missing a builder. Another PM writes more documents, and the integration still isn't built."},"10":{"0":"The difference isn't the manager to worker ratio but layer width: an hourglass has a wide base and a thin middle.","1":"The models aren't about remote work but org shape: how many early-career, middle and senior people there are.","3":"Both models have leadership and new hires. The difference is width: a diamond has a narrow base, fewer juniors, not none."},"11":{"0":"The competitor's PM headcount doesn't say where your work is waiting.","2":"There's no ratio to align with: Andrew Ng called two PMs per engineer very extreme. It isn't a norm.","3":"Cost doesn't reveal the bottleneck. If work waits on development or review, more PMs only lengthen the queue."},"12":{"0":"Most companies still cite other reasons for layoffs. About 6.9% is a small minority, not the main reason.","1":"The direction is clear, and stronger in hiring: companies cutting hiring because of AI rose from about 3% to about 10%.","2":"What rose is the share of companies citing AI, not the number of layoffs. The layoff rate in H1 2026 was 2.8%."},"13":{"1":"The reason wasn't price: freezing juniors saves a little now but leaves the company without tomorrow's senior people.","2":"Michal herself changed the plan and slowed other hiring. She kept juniors because it's a one-way door.","3":"The reasoning wasn't technical. Michal separated reversible decisions from hard-to-reverse ones, and a junior freeze is the latter."},"14":{"0":"A feeling isn't proof. The radar works only when based on outputs, like a test set you built or a tested prototype.","1":"Finished courses aren't proof of ability, and weekly is too frequent to see change.","3":"The radar is for correcting course along the way. Updating only when job hunting is too late to build what's missing."}},"100":{"0":{"0":"Per LeMay, a good PM is not defined by a CS degree. CORE is four daily verbs: communicate, organize, research, execute, not code.","2":"These sound like PM traits, but they are not the letters of CORE. LeMay names four daily actions, not estimation or risk.","3":"These are product goals and metrics, not skills. CORE describes what the PM does every day, not what the product measures."},"1":{"0":"That is exactly what LeMay warns against: taking a framework and bending reality to fit it. A framework serves understanding.","2":"The principle starts by understanding the challenge deeply. Per LeMay, the most common PM problem is a lack of understanding of the real problem.","3":"The principle is about understanding the challenge before choosing a tool, not about task size or postponing big problems."},"2":{"0":"The PM has near-zero formal authority and leads through trust and influence. Connective tissue links teams; it does not decide for them.","2":"Per LeMay, if every decision routes through you, you have not organized, you have built a bottleneck. That is the opposite of connecting.","3":"The metaphor is about people and teams, not technical systems. The translation is between business, engineering and user languages."},"3":{"0":"LeMay pushes the opposite: write short. Communication matters because the other skills rest on it, not because of document volume.","2":"The stage gives a different reason: communication is the foundation the other three are built on, not a skill that is hard to measure.","3":"Research and execution are themselves CORE skills of the PM. Per LeMay, no task that moves the product forward is beneath you."},"4":{"0":"LeMay prefers facilitation over presentation: ask and listen, not lecture over slides. A deck does not justify a meeting.","2":"Per LeMay, a status update is not a goal. If there is no decision to make, send a message instead of holding a meeting.","3":"The test is a goal and a decision, not attendance. Even with everyone present, a meeting without a purpose wastes time."},"5":{"0":"Whoever waits for full certainty never moves. By the time all the data arrives, the opportunity is gone.","2":"Passing every decision up gives up the job. Living in the gray means deciding with what you have, not escaping the risk.","3":"Per LeMay, the gray is not a malfunction of the role but the role itself. Avoiding uncertainty means avoiding most product work."},"6":{"0":"LeMay advises against changing the organization by force: learn to work within it and build alliances with key people.","2":"Politics is how organizations make decisions; ignoring it means giving up influence. And data advises, it does not decide.","3":"A meek yes to every order from above is abandoning the job. LeMay suggests asking which goal it serves and proposing a cheap test."},"7":{"0":"HiPPO stands for Highest Paid Person's Opinion. It is about decisions made by rank, not about hidden goals.","2":"The name is right but the problem is reversed: the risk is deciding by rank instead of evidence, not ignoring senior views.","3":"HiPPO stands for Highest Paid Person's Opinion, not a prioritization method. It describes decisions made by rank."},"8":{"0":"LeMay wrote the opposite of a framework catalog. He warns that a framework serves understanding and never replaces it.","2":"Per the stage, for long-term strategy you look elsewhere, like Inspired. LeMay's strength is the day-to-day.","3":"The stage lists metrics and advanced data analysis as a topic to find elsewhere (Lean Analytics), not as LeMay's focus."},"9":{"0":"Waiting and hoping is exactly what makes a deadline problem blow up twice as big. Clarity over comfort means saying it early.","2":"Hiding the risk from Sales avoids the awkward talk but leaves them no time to adjust. The surprise only grows.","3":"Softening the news to stay comfortable is exactly the trap. The principle asks for saying it clearly, not as a minor risk."},"10":{"0":"Research is about users, data and the market. Shortening an email is about what the reader needs to know, which is communication.","1":"Execution is about leading delivery and removing blockers. Cutting an email to a paragraph is a choice about the message and its reader.","3":"Organization deals with prioritization and processes. Here the change is in how the message is written for its reader."},"11":{"0":"LeMay warns against starting with frameworks. Proven elsewhere does not mean fit here: an imported best practice needs investigation.","1":"Start with the work means starting from the problem, not the tools. Rolling out frameworks before understanding is the reverse.","3":"The issue is not the pace. Even one tool per quarter still starts from the framework instead of the concrete problem."},"12":{"0":"Per LeMay, ceremonies are a means; once they become the goal you have lost the way. Following them blindly is the trap.","2":"The point is not attendance. A ceremony that serves no goal gets changed or canceled, not just re-explained.","3":"LeMay warns against imported best practices: what worked elsewhere worked in a specific context, and blind copying disappoints."},"13":{"0":"Living in the gray means acting inside uncertainty, not waiting it out. Not deciding is also a decision, usually the worst one.","2":"Sharing the data is not the issue. Waiting for 100% certainty is itself the mistake: the information will never be perfect.","3":"LeMay sets no certainty threshold by risk size. In big decisions too, you define what would change your mind and move."},"14":{"0":"Per the metaphor, a PM's value is in connecting, not expert knowledge. Coordination between teams is the work itself, not a waste.","1":"Connective tissue works day to day in the space between teams. A PM detached from execution cannot sync or remove blockers.","3":"Routing every decision through the PM creates a bottleneck. Connecting means the team functions even while you are on vacation."}},"101":{"0":{"0":"Selling to customers is not the reason. The stage ties the book to influencing teams and stakeholders without command authority.","2":"A PM has no command authority over developers, designers or stakeholders. That is exactly why influence tools matter.","3":"Per Carnegie, we deal with creatures of emotion, not logic. Persuading with logic alone loses before the discussion begins."},"1":{"0":"Smiling, remembering names and genuine interest belong to the six ways to make people like you, part 2 of the book.","2":"Avoiding arguments, admitting mistakes and letting the idea be theirs are persuasion rules from part 3, not the fundamentals.","3":"Praise first, indirect mistakes and saving face come from part 4, changing people without resentment."},"2":{"0":"The difference is not the setting. Honest praise rests on a specific fact, and Carnegie even recommends giving credit in public.","2":"Size is not the test. Carnegie says to praise every improvement, even the smallest, as long as it is specific and sincere.","3":"Carnegie does not pair praise with a point to improve. He even warns that a but after praise erases it."},"3":{"0":"A man convinced against his will is of the same opinion still. Winning on logic makes the other side feel inferior, not cooperative.","2":"Per Carnegie, the only way to win an argument is to avoid it. A heated argument hardens positions; it builds no respect.","3":"The losing side feels inferior and pays you back at the next opportunity. What you gain is resentment, not authority."},"4":{"0":"A bonus is an outside reward. The principle is to connect the task to what the developer already wants, like learning a new technology.","2":"Data-driven persuasion comes from your angle, not theirs. Carnegie baits the hook with what the fish wants, not what he likes.","3":"Your excitement is the strawberries and cream: what you like. The fish bites on a worm, what it wants."},"5":{"0":"The rule is to admit fast and emphatically, even before anyone else says it. Hiding it to protect your standing misses the point.","2":"Waiting for all the facts delays the admission. One real-time I was wrong is worth ten speeches about credibility.","3":"Explaining the circumstances turns the admission into an excuse. The strength is in admitting fast and emphatically, without dodging."},"6":{"0":"Full data convinces with your argument, and the idea stays yours. The principle plants the direction with questions so they reach it.","2":"Even when the idea is theirs, the PM steers with questions toward the goal. Stepping back with no guidance gives up the lead.","3":"Picking alone and announcing makes it your decision. The principle is that the other person feels they reached the idea."},"7":{"0":"Opening straight with the problem triggers defensiveness. Carnegie begins with honest praise, like lather before the razor.","2":"The but erases the praise before it. Carnegie suggests and instead, and pointing at the mistake indirectly with questions.","3":"Criticism only in a 1:1, never in front of the team. And proposing the fix yourself takes away ownership; ask instead."},"8":{"0":"The book deals with human relations in general, not closing deals. Its principles apply to anyone you depend on, stakeholders included.","2":"Carnegie rejects winning debates: winning the argument means losing the relationship. Rebuttals only harden positions.","3":"Carnegie's influence rests on trust, listening and the other side's motivation, not on hierarchy, escalation and sign-offs."},"9":{"0":"Appreciation matters a lot to Carnegie, but the sweetest sound he names is the person's own name.","2":"Praise is a separate principle. The sweetest sound in Carnegie's line is a person's own name.","3":"Reflecting back is part of active listening, a different principle. The sweetest sound is the person's own name."},"10":{"0":"Escalating and documenting is criticism in another form. It puts the developer on the defensive and does not change the attitude.","1":"Rebutting every objection is winning the argument and losing the relationship. The resistance only hardens.","3":"Raising it before the whole team exposes the developer publicly, and a majority vote ignores what actually bothers him."},"11":{"0":"Presenting it as already approved replaces persuasion with hierarchy. The team will at most comply, with no ownership.","2":"A case that leaves no room to argue convinces against their will, and per Carnegie such people keep their opinion.","3":"Waiting passively may never produce a similar idea. Carnegie plants the direction actively, with questions."},"12":{"0":"Per Carnegie, the principles work only when they come from the heart. Fake interest is spotted instantly, so it does not still work.","2":"The danger Carnegie raises is about trust, not softness. Insincerity gets exposed and erodes the trust everything rests on.","3":"The issue is not praise wearing out. Insincere praise is flattery, and people smell it a mile away."},"13":{"0":"A data email after the meeting is the same argument in writing. Every such written win loses a little more goodwill.","1":"Avoiding the argument does not mean dropping your direction. With Carnegie you steer with questions toward the goal, not surrender.","3":"Escalation turns the disagreement into a contest of hierarchy. Carnegie's influence works through questions and their point of view."},"14":{"0":"Steering is step 4. It comes only after preparing, opening with recognition and a real dialogue.","2":"Executive backing is not one of the five steps. The model rests on the stakeholder's point of view, not on pressure from above.","3":"A finished PRD is not in the model. Opening with your plan skips understanding the other side's pressures and goals."}},"102":{"0":{"0":"A sprint is five full days, one per stage. Squeezing it shorter leaves no time for individual thinking and user recruiting.","2":"A sprint writes no code and launches nothing: Thursday builds a codeless facade, and Friday tests it with five users.","3":"Ten days of design, development, QA and launch is a full delivery cycle. A sprint is five days of discovery ending in a test, not a launch."},"1":{"0":"That describes the Facilitator, who owns the clock and must stay neutral, which is why they should not also be the Decider.","2":"A sprint replaces vague consensus with one Decider. Their Supervote decides what gets built, whatever the straw poll said.","3":"Friday's interviews are run by the Interviewer. The Decider rules on the target on Monday and casts the Supervote on Wednesday."},"2":{"0":"The aim is not saving time. Individual work exists so the loudest voice does not win and everyone brings a complete solution.","2":"Everyone sketches on Tuesday, not only designers. In Michal's sprint the winning sketch came from the support rep.","3":"The sketches are anonymous, so they are judged on content, not on their author. Owning the idea is not the aim."},"3":{"0":"Too polished means you wasted the day. The prototype has to be believable to users, not a demo for management.","2":"Too sloppy, and users will not take it seriously. Goldilocks must look real enough to trigger honest reactions.","3":"The prototype is a codeless facade, built to be thrown away. Production code is the opposite of the idea."},"4":{"0":"The argument is not statistical significance. Per Nielsen's research, 5 users already uncover about 85% of the problems.","2":"Even with more time, more users add little: after the fifth the patterns repeat and the ROI collapses.","3":"A finding is what 3 of 5 did the same way, a behavior pattern. It is not a vote on what they said they liked."},"5":{"0":"A design sprint is not UI work: it takes a problem to a solution tested with users, which is discovery.","2":"The durations are swapped: a design sprint lasts five days, and a Scrum Sprint lasts two weeks.","3":"The roles are reversed: a design sprint uses a faked prototype, not a coded MVP, and Scrum is for delivery, not research."},"6":{"0":"There are no presentations in the Art Museum: sketches are anonymous and hang in silence. Discussion comes later, 3 minutes each.","2":"Inspiring products from other industries are Lightning Demos on Tuesday, not the Art Museum on Wednesday.","3":"The storyboard is drawn at the end of Wednesday, after the decision. The Art Museum displays the solution sketches."},"7":{"0":"That is exactly when a sprint pays off: a big, fuzzy problem, or a team stuck on it for months.","2":"Knapp lists both as reasons to run a sprint: a team arguing about direction, and a deadline with no room for a long process.","3":"High stakes and a hard-to-reverse decision are top reasons to run a sprint, and so is the moment before committing a quarter."},"8":{"0":"Inspiration from other products is Lightning Demos. Crazy 8s is 8 variations of your own idea in 8 minutes.","2":"Dots on the wall are Wednesday's Heat Map. Crazy 8s is a Tuesday sketching exercise.","3":"Mapping the journey and risks is Monday's map. Crazy 8s is 8 sketch variations, one minute each."},"9":{"0":"A sprint ships no version: it builds a codeless facade to test. It insures development; it does not replace it.","2":"A sprint replaces consensus with one Decider. Its value is evidence from real users, not team agreement.","3":"The prototype is a disposable Goldilocks facade, built to learn from users, not polished for investors."},"10":{"0":"An A/B test after launch means you already built it. The sprint exists to learn before months of development, not after.","2":"An over-perfect prototype is itself a common mistake. Without Friday, even a polished prototype tests nothing.","3":"Who decides is not the issue. Skipping the test is the mistake, no matter who approves it."},"11":{"0":"That is a flawed success: the direction works and needs polish. An efficient failure means the direction itself is wrong.","2":"A prototype crash is a technical glitch, not a test outcome. An efficient failure is about a direction proving wrong.","3":"Per Knapp, compressing into two days is a common mistake: without time to think alone and recruit users, it is just a workshop."},"12":{"1":"The storyboard is not a summary for management. It is the frame-by-frame script the team builds from on Thursday.","2":"The journey map of up to 15 steps is Monday's work, for choosing the target. The storyboard comes Wednesday, after the decision.","3":"A sprint writes no spec. The storyboard guides a codeless prototype built the next day, not developers later on."},"13":{"0":"Knapp rejects compressing: without time to think alone and recruit users, you are left with just another nice workshop.","2":"The purposes are swapped: Lightning Demos are for inspiration, Crazy 8s for quick variations, and user interviews for validation.","3":"The stage lists tools to take on their own, like Note-and-Vote, Crazy 8s and the five-user test. Each works without a full sprint."},"14":{"0":"Wall space is not the reason. Knapp limits the team because above seven the pace collapses and discussions balloon.","2":"The recommended team includes a marketing or sales expert and a support rep. Their voice is part of the method.","3":"Budget is not the reason. Above seven people, every decision becomes a committee and the pace collapses."}},"103":{"0":{"0":"Feasibility belongs, but scalability and time to market are not Cagan risks. Usability and business viability are missing.","2":"These are business metrics, not risks. Cagan's risks are questions you answer before building, like whether anyone will want it.","3":"Usability is one of the four, but market size, competition and pricing are not. Cagan even sees obsessing over competitors as a bad-team trait."},"1":{"0":"Gathering requirements from sales and customers is what Cagan calls a bad team. Customers cannot simply tell us what to build.","2":"Discovery is not a design phase. The trio tests all four risks together, including feasibility and viability, not just UX.","3":"Per Cagan, an MVP is an experiment to run, not a stripped-down version developed for months. Discovery builds no product."},"2":{"0":"Seniority is not the difference. An empowered team gets a problem and a success metric instead of a feature list.","2":"Leadership still brings the problems and metrics, and the team returns validated solutions. Goals are not set without leadership.","3":"A feature list with dates is exactly a Feature Team. An empowered team gets a problem to solve and a success metric."},"3":{"0":"The four questions do not look at competitors. They focus on our business objective, the success metric and the customer's problem.","2":"The questions are not a financial calculation. They start from the business objective and the customer problem, before any solution.","3":"Naming a feature, timeline and launch date jumps to the solution. The assessment comes before touching the solution."},"4":{"0":"Dual-Track is not bugs versus features. One track discovers what to build, the other builds what was already validated.","2":"The tracks run in parallel, all the time. A research week at the start of a quarter is not continuous discovery per Cagan.","3":"It is one team, not two: the trio of PM, designer and tech lead tests the next opportunity while the team builds what was validated."},"5":{"0":"The timespan is not Cagan's issue. The problem is that features get locked in as solutions before they are validated.","2":"Cagan's alternative is an outcome roadmap, with even fewer features. And when a date is truly needed, there is a High-Integrity Commitment.","3":"The reverse: a feature roadmap gives the team no freedom; it dictates the solution from above."},"6":{"0":"The Scrum Master is not part of the trio, and usability belongs to the Product Designer, not the PM.","2":"The trio has no data analyst, and feasibility is missing here. The tech lead owns feasibility, the designer owns usability.","3":"The trio is the team's own PM, designer and tech lead, doing discovery together daily. A CPO and VPs are leadership, not the team."},"7":{"0":"A research week at the start of the quarter is exactly the mistake Cagan names: discovery as a one-time ritual.","2":"Customers cannot simply tell you what to build. Collecting requests is what Cagan describes as a bad team.","3":"Monitoring after launch learns at the most expensive moment. Discovery tests ideas before they are built."},"8":{"0":"Feasibility is easy and comfortable to test. Cagan calls value the critical, neglected risk: if nobody needs it, nothing matters.","2":"Viability matters, but a product nobody wants fails anyway. Value comes first.","3":"Usability only matters once someone wants the product. Without value, ease of use saves nothing."},"9":{"0":"Cagan reverses this: you can ship a lot and change nothing. Teams are measured on outcome, not on what shipped.","2":"Cagan does not weigh them equally. Output without outcome is hard work on the wrong thing.","3":"Velocity and story points measure work volume, which is output. Outcome is a change in reality, like retention."},"10":{"0":"Per Cagan, a dated feature list encodes failure: half of it will not work, and all of it has already become a commitment.","2":"Choosing the order still builds features nobody validated. The team should get a problem and a metric, not a list.","3":"Scope is not the issue. Even 6 features with fixed dates are solutions locked in before discovery."},"11":{"0":"A concierge does the work manually, so it does not test whether it can be built. That is what a feasibility prototype is for.","1":"A manual service has no interface to learn yet. Usability is tested with a user prototype.","3":"The concierge first tests whether customers want the value at all, before asking whether it pays off for the business."},"12":{"0":"The vision is the multi-year picture and must stay stable. An outcome roadmap does not replace it; it sets results for a period.","2":"An outcome roadmap does not drop every date. When one is required, the team makes a High-Integrity Commitment after checking.","3":"It is not the same content: one dictates solutions, the other sets a result and lets the team discover the way."},"13":{"0":"Cagan calls a PM who only relays requirements a pipe, not a product manager. You define and design together, not in a relay.","2":"Validating alone still skips the designer and tech lead. Usability and feasibility belong inside discovery, from the start.","3":"Cagan sets no exception for experience. The trio is a principle: together from minute one, each owning a different risk."},"14":{"0":"The order is reversed: Empowered focuses on the organization, leadership and culture; Inspired on the tools of the PM and trio.","2":"The stage presents Empowered (2020) as a separate book on the organization, not as an updated edition of Inspired.","3":"The split is not company size. Inspired is about the team and the product; Empowered about the organization and its product leaders."}},"104":{"0":{"0":"That is the loop itself: cue, craving, response, reward. The laws are what you do to each stage, like making the cue obvious.","2":"Trigger, action, variable reward and investment is Nir Eyal's Hooked model, not Clear's four laws.","3":"These are not Clear's laws. Starting small is a tool inside Law 3; the laws map to cue, craving, response and reward."},"1":{"0":"The line is reversed. Per Clear, goals are not enough; it is your systems that set your real level.","2":"Clear does not rely on motivation: it wobbles, while environment and systems do not.","3":"Clear's message is the reverse: ambitions alone lift no one; you fall to the level of your systems."},"2":{"0":"Starting several habits at once is a common mistake per Clear: five new habits on Sunday equal zero by Thursday.","2":"Pairing a must-do with something enjoyable is Temptation Bundling, a Law 2 tool, not Habit Stacking.","3":"Setting time and place in advance is an Implementation Intention. Habit Stacking anchors to a habit that already exists."},"3":{"0":"Clear's rule is not about small tasks. It shrinks a new habit, like one page instead of reading every evening.","2":"Two minutes is a ceiling for the starting version, not a daily minimum. First show up, then improve.","3":"The rule is about how a habit starts, not a review. Tracking progress belongs to Law 4, like the paper clips."},"4":{"1":"Identity is not image. It is who you believe you are, and every action is a vote for it, whatever others see.","2":"Clear does not talk about innate traits. Identity is built from actions, each one a vote for who you become.","3":"Process is a separate layer. Clear names three layers: outcomes, processes and identity, and identity is the deepest."},"5":{"0":"The plateau is not final: like the ice cube, one more degree and it melts. Most people quit right before the breakthrough.","2":"That describes an automatic habit. The plateau is the long stretch of investing while seeing no results.","3":"The plateau is the opposite of fast growth: you invest and see nothing, the valley of disappointment."},"6":{"0":"More settings and paths add friction. Law 3 makes the right action the easiest one, with smart defaults.","2":"Notifications and reminders make the cue obvious: that is Law 1, not Law 3.","3":"Celebrations and instant feedback make it satisfying: that is Law 4."},"7":{"0":"Clear talks about a fast return, not doubling up. Perfection is not required, and the weekly total is not the point.","2":"Resetting to zero turns one miss into failure. Per Clear, one miss is an accident; only two start a new habit.","3":"A second miss in a row is exactly what the rule warns against: it starts a new habit, of not doing it."},"8":{"0":"Goals on the wall still rely on willpower. Clear designs the environment so the right action is the easiest one.","2":"Fixing a time and place is an Implementation Intention. Environment design works on the space, like the cafeteria water bottles.","3":"Comfort is not the point. The environment should make the desired action easy and the temptation invisible."},"9":{"0":"Two stages are swapped: gamification like streaks makes it attractive (craving), and one-click makes it easy (response).","2":"These are acquisition tactics. The four laws build a repeated usage loop, not a one-time sign-up.","3":"The stage applies the laws directly to products: notifications, streaks, fewer clicks, celebrations. And Clear rejects relying on willpower."},"10":{"1":"More features than competitors is not what makes it attractive. Law 2 is about anticipating a reward, like a progress meter.","2":"A long tutorial adds friction instead of removing it. Law 3 cuts steps, like creating a task straight from the email.","3":"A discount for every return is not the reward Clear means. And before any reward, the user has to see a cue at all."},"11":{"0":"A sharper goal is still a goal. Per Clear, winners and losers hold the same goals; the system makes the difference.","2":"Announcing is exactly what failed for Michal: the goal was announced in every meeting and did not move. Only a system moves it.","3":"Raising the target does not help. Without a system, a bigger goal is just a bigger slogan."},"12":{"0":"Linking to an existing daily action is Habit Stacking, a Law 1 tool. Temptation Bundling ties need-to with want-to.","2":"Removing steps reduces friction, a Law 3 tool. Temptation Bundling makes the action attractive, Law 2.","3":"A discounted feature bundle is pricing, not a habit technique. Temptation Bundling ties a must-do to a want-to."},"13":{"0":"Clear does see a difference: outcome-based habits are weak, identity-based ones strong, because habits flow from identity.","2":"Clear says the reverse: behavior without identity needs constant reminding, while identity makes it flow naturally.","3":"Enforcement through reviews is not the reason. Identity works because habits flow from it on their own, not from pressure."},"14":{"0":"365% assumes simple addition. Daily gains compound: 1.01 to the power of 365 is about 37.8 times.","2":"37% is off by a factor of a hundred: 1.01 to the power of 365 is about 37.8 times, not 1.37.","3":"1% a day compounds to about 37.8 times, far above 10%. And Clear argues small consistent changes beat dramatic ones."}},"105":{"0":{"0":"It is not about employment type but attitude: a mercenary asks what ships tomorrow, a missionary asks if it solves the customer's problem.","2":"Cagan does not want both: we need teams of missionaries, not teams of mercenaries.","3":"Per Cagan, the difference is the environment, not character: the same people become missionaries when given true ownership of a problem."},"1":{"0":"Hiring is part of staffing, but firing and budgeting are not among the three. Strategic context and coaching are missing.","2":"Planning, executing and measuring is a generic work cycle. Cagan's leadership provides context, staffs teams and coaches people.","3":"Vision is part of strategic context, but a roadmap is not a leadership responsibility, and staffing and coaching are missing."},"2":{"0":"Telling the PM what to do is dictating, which is micromanagement. Coaching asks, for example: how did you reach this decision?","2":"Per Cagan, a course or workshop is no substitute for ongoing guidance. Coaching is a recurring 1:1.","3":"Cagan's 1:1 is about thinking and decisions, not status reporting. A task review is not coaching."},"3":{"0":"A roadmap of features and dates is what a feature team receives. Strategic context gives the why, not a plan to execute.","2":"Ownership, dependencies and platform teams belong to team topology. Strategic context is vision, strategy and objectives.","3":"A personal skills plan comes from the gap assessment, a coaching tool. Strategic context is what the whole team receives."},"4":{"0":"Cagan locates the cause in the environment: the same people become missionaries when given true ownership.","2":"The book's title says the opposite: ordinary people create extraordinary products. You do not need only stars.","3":"Cagan points to leadership and culture, not technology. Tools and infrastructure are not among the reasons he lists."},"5":{"1":"The terms are reversed: output is what we shipped, and outcome is what changed for the customer and the business.","2":"Both what we planned and what we shipped are output. Outcome is the impact, like an improvement in retention.","3":"The split is not short versus long term. Output is what we made, and outcome is the effect it had."},"6":{"0":"Cagan warns against deriving the topology from the org chart, and requires every part to belong to exactly one team.","2":"Experience teams are built around users and journeys, not tech layers. Layers scatter ownership and add dependencies.","3":"Rotating teams and a shared backlog blur ownership. Cagan wants every part owned by exactly one team over time."},"7":{"0":"Collecting and ranking requirements is the feature-team approach. In Empowered, stakeholders give constraints, not solutions.","2":"A stakeholder surprised at launch becomes a permanent opponent. Share discovery findings before you need their support.","3":"Per Cagan, a feature for every party is a roadmap of industrial peace, not of strategy."},"8":{"0":"The reverse: Inspired holds the discovery techniques, and Empowered deals with leadership, culture and coaching.","2":"The split is team versus organization, not startup versus enterprise. Empowered is about building the organization.","3":"Empowered does not replace discovery; it builds the organization that enables it."},"9":{"0":"Detailed upfront planning is the opposite of experimenting. Teams afraid to fail stop trying new things.","2":"Hunting for culprits turns failure into punishment. Cagan wants failure to be learning.","3":"Ignoring failures loses what they teach. Cagan's culture learns from failure; it does not skip over it."},"10":{"0":"Direction comes from strategic context and a problem with metrics. A weekly feature list dictates solutions.","2":"The responsibility lies with leadership, not the team. Per Cagan, the fix starts with leadership, not the team.","3":"A quarterly feature roadmap still dictates solutions. An empowered team gets problems and metrics, not features."},"11":{"0":"The transformation starts with commitment from the top; the pilot comes after it, not instead of it.","2":"In Cagan's sequence, coaching comes before autonomy. Without coaching, go be autonomous is abandonment.","3":"Per Cagan, missionaries are made by the environment, not found. A pilot proves results; it does not sort people."},"12":{"0":"A backlog manager does not run discovery; a product person does. Here the roles are swapped.","2":"The difference is not seniority but the way of working: discovering with users, or relaying requirements.","3":"Writing full specs up front is not discovery either. A product person tests ideas with users rather than documenting them."},"13":{"0":"Context does not exempt teams from results: team objectives are measurable results each team takes on.","2":"Context does not replace coaching. Both are separate responsibilities of product leadership.","3":"In Empowered, stakeholders provide constraints and context, and the team is the one that finds the solution."},"14":{"0":"Inspecting every step is micromanagement too. The coach does not check each step but develops judgment.","2":"Saying exactly what to change is still an instruction. Coaching asks questions so the PM finds the answer.","3":"Cagan's coaching tool is a private recurring 1:1, not an open review that exposes the PM's mistakes."}},"106":{"0":{"0":"These are not Ries's steps. His loop builds, measures and learns, and it is planned backward, from what you want to learn.","2":"Design, develop, deploy describes delivery that ends at launch. Ries's loop ends in learning: was the hypothesis validated?","3":"Ries's loop has an explicit measure step: real users' behavior, with metrics that drive decisions."},"1":{"0":"Per Ries, an MVP is not a shrunken version 1. A version with all the basic features is a product, not an experiment.","2":"An MVP is not built for a full launch. Its success is measured by how much it teaches, not how ready it is.","3":"The whole point of an MVP is the behavior of real users. An internal prototype produces no validated learning."},"2":{"0":"The difference is not the audience. A vanity metric misleads the team too; the test is whether it drives a decision.","2":"Precision is not the point: total downloads can be exact and still vanity. It always rises and changes no decision.","3":"Usage can be actionable too, like weekly active users or cohort retention. The test is whether it drives a decision."},"3":{"0":"A survey where people say they would buy appears in the stage as an illusion of learning. Validated learning rests on actual behavior.","2":"An expert calling it a great idea is an illusion of learning per the stage. Only real users in a real experiment validate.","3":"The competitors already do it is an illusion of learning. It says nothing about how our own users behave."},"4":{"0":"The baseline is just the starting point, and even grim numbers are an asset. First you tune the engine with experiments.","2":"A competitor's move is not the trigger. A pivot rests on our own data showing the direction is stuck despite experiments.","3":"The meeting is scheduled so the decision is not postponed, not to pivot by calendar. If the needles move, you persevere."},"5":{"0":"Ries warns that without good facilitation the five whys become five blames. The goal is fixing the system, not finding a culprit.","2":"5 Whys analyzes a problem that already happened. It is not a way to justify a feature idea.","3":"Fixing symptoms is exactly what the method prevents. It digs down to the root, which is often a process."},"6":{"0":"Market size and pricing are not the two leap-of-faith assumptions. Ries names the value and growth hypotheses.","2":"These are not Ries's two. Problem and solution leave out the growth question: how new customers will find the product.","3":"Technology and funding are not Ries's hypotheses. Both of his concern the customer: value and growth."},"7":{"0":"Ries does not sort pivots by size. His catalog names what changes: the feature, the segment, the revenue model or the technology.","2":"Landing page, video, concierge and the rest of that list are MVP types, not pivot types.","3":"Sticky, viral and paid are the three engines of growth, not types of pivot."},"8":{"0":"Innovation accounting is not a budget. It measures progress from a baseline through experiments, before ROI is meaningful.","2":"Cumulative downloads and sign-ups are exactly the vanity metrics Ries warns against: success theater.","3":"The number of experiments is not the measure. What counts is whether the metrics move from the baseline toward the target."},"9":{"0":"Per the analysis the stage cites, the money did not run out first: the most common cause, about 42%, was no real market need.","2":"Ries answers the reputation worry: if we do not know who the customer is yet, how do we know what they consider quality?","3":"In most failures the technology did not collapse. The common cause was building something nobody needed."},"10":{"0":"Without one clear hypothesis there is no MVP. Fifteen features over three months is a product, not an experiment.","2":"Trimming to core features still builds a product. The measure is the smallest experiment for one hypothesis, not a feature count.","3":"Polish first, release later is exactly the waste the book fights. The problem is too much building, not too little."},"11":{"0":"Download growth percentages are still vanity: 200% growth appears in the stage as a misleading metric.","2":"The same downloads as a climbing chart are the same vanity metric. The format does not make it actionable.","3":"Accuracy is not the issue: even an audited total always rises and says nothing about real usage."},"12":{"0":"Slack did not sell the game to a new audience. One internal tool became a product of its own.","1":"The product itself changed, not just its technology. A technology pivot keeps the exact same solution on a new technology.","3":"Zoom-out turns a product into a feature. Slack went the other way: one feature became the whole product."},"13":{"0":"Ries's reason is learning speed, not cost or headcount. A shorter loop finds problems after a week, not a month.","2":"Release frequency does not raise retention by itself. Small batches shorten the learning loop and cut risk.","3":"Feature count and velocity are output. Ries measures progress in validated learning, not in features delivered."},"14":{"0":"Per Ries, a pivot is data-driven by definition, built on validated learning. The criticism is the reverse: pivoting without learning.","2":"By Ries's definition, a pivot keeps what was already learned. Starting from scratch is not what makes it real.","3":"Ries recommends scheduling the Pivot-or-Persevere meeting in advance, so the decision is not postponed forever."}},"107":{"0":{"0":"The order is broken: every use starts with a trigger, and investment comes only after the reward.","2":"These are not the Hook's steps. Eyal's loop has a variable reward and ends with investment, not habit formation.","3":"The Hook's reward is variable, not fixed: a fixed reward wears thin. And the loop ends with investment."},"1":{"0":"An internal trigger is not a reminder the user sets. It is an emotion, like boredom or FOMO, welded to the product.","2":"Inside or outside the app does not matter: an in-app banner is still an external cue. Internal means an emotion.","3":"Triggers start usage; rewards come after the action. An internal trigger is an emotion like boredom or anxiety."},"2":{"0":"Paid, earned and owned are types of external triggers, not rewards.","2":"Identify, codify and modify are the steps of habit testing, not reward types.","3":"Boredom, FOMO and loneliness are internal triggers: emotions that start usage, not rewards that follow it."},"3":{"0":"The formula has no reward. In Fogg's model the third component is the trigger: motivation, ability and trigger.","2":"Frequency and perceived utility are the axes of the Habit Zone, not Fogg's formula.","3":"The letters are right but the meaning is not: A is ability and T is trigger, not attention and free time."},"4":{"0":"Motivation is the most expensive and least effective lever. Removing friction is almost always cheaper and more effective.","2":"Asking for data before the action adds friction. And investment is requested only after a reward.","3":"A reward after the action does not help when users do not complete it. Low conversion points to friction, so check ability."},"5":{"0":"Frequency and perceived utility define the Habit Zone. The Manipulation Matrix is an ethical compass.","2":"That is the Entertainer: uses it, but it does not improve lives. A Facilitator needs both.","3":"That is the Peddler: believes it helps but does not use it. A Facilitator needs both."},"6":{"0":"Stored value is value for the user, not the company's revenue. CLTV is a different metric.","2":"Eyal separates exit penalties from stored value: history that improves the service is real value; a penalty is not.","3":"Variable rewards are step 3. Stored value is what the user puts in at step 4, investment."},"7":{"0":"Size is not what works. Per Skinner, the uncertain anticipation, not the prize itself, ignites the urge.","2":"Value that grows with investment describes stored value, step 4. The variable reward works through uncertainty.","3":"Cost is not the reason. Unpredictability is what reinforces behavior most powerfully."},"8":{"0":"Mastery and achievement, like an empty inbox, are rewards of the Self, not the Hunt. Hunt is about resources and information.","2":"The two are swapped: Tribe is social recognition, and Hunt is the search for information and resources.","3":"Tribe is not brand loyalty and Hunt is not competition. Tribe is social recognition; Hunt is information and resources."},"9":{"0":"Session length is not Eyal's test. He asks how soon the user would notice the product is gone: hours means a Hook.","2":"Opening after a notification shows a response to an external trigger. A habit means returning without one.","3":"Time to the Aha moment measures onboarding. Eyal's habit test is how fast users would notice the product is gone."},"10":{"0":"8% means most users do not return daily. The Habit Zone requires high frequency, and 8% is low.","2":"The data says the opposite: at 8% DAU/MAU most users do not return daily. Frequency is the problem.","3":"New users do not create a habit. The ratio shows existing users do not return; more acquisition fills a leaky bucket."},"11":{"0":"Flooding users with notifications is a common mistake: they switch everything off. More of the same welds no emotion.","2":"Push notifications are owned triggers, not paid ones. And any external trigger helps only when welded to an emotion.","3":"Per Eyal, you do not start from motivation. The missing link is an internal trigger, an emotion, not a campaign."},"12":{"0":"Improving lives without using it is the Peddler, not the Entertainer. The Entertainer uses it, but it does not improve lives.","2":"A Dealer neither uses it nor improves lives. Improving lives without using it describes the Peddler.","3":"Using it and improving lives is the Facilitator, not the Entertainer. And the Dealer does not use the product at all."},"13":{"0":"Motivation and trigger are separate parts of B=MAT, and frequency and utility belong to the Habit Zone. None are ability factors.","2":"These are UI quality principles. Eyal's six factors measure what an action costs the user: time, money, effort and more.","3":"These are emotions and desires, not the cost of an action. Simplicity factors measure time, money, effort, social acceptability, routine."},"14":{"1":"Friction only matters once a trigger is there. Eyal starts by making sure a clear trigger is present.","2":"Motivation is the most expensive and least effective lever, so it comes last, not first.","3":"Reward and investment come after the action, inside the loop. Without a trigger nothing starts at all."}},"108":{"0":{"0":"Mom is not the toughest customer but the kindest: she loves you, so she encourages you. That is exactly the problem.","2":"Family and friends give the least honest feedback: they love you and want to be supportive. That is the book's starting point.","3":"The name is not about simplicity. It is about questions so factual that even someone who loves you cannot tell you pleasant lies."},"1":{"0":"Pitching the idea breaks rule 1, asking about the future breaks rule 2, and compliments and feature requests are bad data.","2":"The first rule is right, but asking what they would want in the future is fluff, and feature requests are clues, not specs.","3":"Fitzpatrick wants depth: dig under every generic answer. And write down exact quotes, not a summary in your own words."},"2":{"0":"That is one of the book's good questions: it anchors the talk to a real past event. It focuses; it does not scatter.","2":"Asking what they pay today is recommended in the book: it reveals willingness to pay through the present, not promises.","3":"How do you solve this today is a good question: it is about their life, and not mentioning your idea is exactly the point."},"3":{"0":"Specific stories and exact quotes from the past are good data. Surveys and ratings capture opinions, not behavior.","2":"Compliments are the classic bad data, and the unpleasant data point is often the most important. The test is facts, not tone.","3":"Purchase promises and future intentions are fluff, which is bad data. Good data is past behavior and real money."},"4":{"0":"An enthusiastic I will definitely use it is still words: fluff about the future. Commitment costs time, reputation or money.","2":"Feedback on missing features is a feature request, another type of bad data. Commitment means giving something up.","3":"Compliments do not add up to commitment. It is a different currency: time, reputation or money, not more praise."},"5":{"0":"Presenting the product turns the talk into a pitch, and the learning stops. Advancement is a step the customer takes.","2":"Advancement is about the customer conversation, not onboarding inside the product. It is the next concrete step.","3":"A pleasant end with praise and no next step is a pleasant failure: zombie leads, friendly and never buying."},"6":{"0":"Saturation is across interviews, not within one: you stop when answers repeat across people, usually 5 to 15.","2":"Agreement with your hypothesis is not the test. Saturation means the same pains repeat, whatever you hoped to hear.","3":"Saturation here is about the interviews, not competitors. It signals that enough conversations have been held."},"7":{"0":"Casual does not mean unprepared: the book asks for three big questions in advance and exact quotes during the talk.","2":"Pitching the idea breaks the first rule. A casual talk lowers defenses; it is not a stage for the idea.","3":"The goal is learning, not selling. Once you start selling, the learning stops, even if the meeting went well."},"8":{"0":"How much would you pay is a hypothetical, fluff. A request is a clue to pain; dig for the motivation behind it.","2":"It is not the customer's job to know what to build. A feature request is a clue to pain, not a spec.","3":"Showing the roadmap turns the interview into a pitch and accepts the request as is. First dig for the pain behind it."},"9":{"0":"Unexpected problems are a good sign: if nothing surprised you, you did not learn anything.","2":"Per the 20% rule, the customer should talk most of the time. Talking more than a fifth of the time is selling, not learning.","3":"Discomfort is expected: a good interview often feels a little uncomfortable, because you hear what you did not want to hear."},"10":{"0":"78% saying they want it measures optimism. Building on it skips checking whether the pain is real.","2":"How much would you pay is exactly the bad question the book flags. Ask what the problem costs them today.","3":"A reaction to a mockup is still an opinion and invites compliments. Validation comes from past behavior."},"11":{"0":"A stated intention about the future is fluff, the second type of bad data. People imagine an idealized version of themselves.","2":"Enthusiasm without commitment is noise, not signal. Signal is a pain that repeats, or a willingness to advance.","3":"It is not neutral; it is bad data. A survey would measure the same optimism. Only past behavior or commitment validates."},"12":{"0":"Would you use it and how much would you pay are hypothetical future questions that invite optimism.","2":"Asking their opinion of the idea puts your idea on the table, and from there everyone turns polite.","3":"Which feature are you missing invites a request, not pain. And future questions about wanting and paying are fluff."},"13":{"0":"Covering every pain ignores the signal: a different pain from each person points to a segment that is too broad, not broad demand.","2":"More interviews from the same broad audience will keep scattering. The book's fix is slicing the segment, not sampling more.","3":"Emotional intensity is not the test. Signal is a pain that repeats across different people in a focused segment."},"14":{"0":"A compliment is bad data, fool's gold. It is not validation, however strong it sounds.","2":"A rating turns a compliment into a number, but it stays an opinion. It only invites more praise.","3":"Pitching pricing and features turns the interview into a sale and invites more compliments. The learning stops."}},"109":{"0":{"0":"That is the direction most organizations use, from the outside in, and the Why gets lost on the way.","2":"How is the middle circle, so it comes before What. Inspiring organizations go Why, then How, then What.","3":"Sinek sets a fixed direction for inspiring organizations: always from the core outward, with the Why first."},"1":{"0":"The brain regions are swapped: the neocortex handles language and analysis and matches What; the limbic brain matches Why.","2":"The limbic brain has no capacity for language at all. The Why speaks to the part that decides, not the part that explains.","3":"Logic and analysis belong to the neocortex, the What circle. The Why speaks to emotion and trust in the limbic brain."},"2":{"0":"Manipulation for Sinek is not lying: discounts and promotions work and are honest. They buy a transaction, not loyalty.","2":"Fear and aspirations are on Sinek's list of manipulations, but inspiration is not price and specs. It is a belief people join.","3":"The department is not the distinction. A discount is manipulation whoever runs it, and inspiration is a belief, not a budget."},"3":{"0":"Taking everyone's advice is exactly the party trap: you buy it all, waste money, and nothing fits together.","2":"Matching competitors is the opposite of filtering by belief. The test is consistency with our Why, not with the market.","3":"The Celery Test filters by the Why, not by payback. Velox declined a lucrative contract that contradicted its Why."},"4":{"0":"Sinek says the reverse: early adopters buy the Why and forgive rough edges. It is the majority that needs proof.","2":"The early majority needs proof and recommendations. The ones who buy the Why and spread it are the early adopters.","3":"Early adopters buy vision and gut feeling, not features. TiVo marketed the What and struggled despite superior technology."},"5":{"0":"Sinek sees no rivalry: behind almost every inspiring organization stands a pair, and neither is enough alone.","2":"Neither replaces the other: the visionary sees what does not exist yet and the operator builds the structure, together.","3":"Sinek describes two people, like Walt and Roy Disney or Jobs and Wozniak, not one leader doing both."},"6":{"0":"Every maker had access to the same engineers. Per Sinek, Apple sells a belief; specs alone do not explain the loyalty.","2":"Beautiful, simple design is Apple's How. Per Sinek, the loyalty comes from the Why: challenging the status quo.","3":"Peer pressure is one of the six manipulations Sinek lists. It buys transactions, not the loyalty he credits Apple with."},"7":{"0":"The reverse: what is lost is direction, the Why. The How person stays and keeps executing, but without a compass.","2":"Sinek's warning is about the compass, not customer relationships. Decisions slide from Why to What.","3":"Sinek's risk is not caution but losing direction: decisions become What-driven and stop being true to the Why."},"8":{"0":"Opening with specs is What-first: the discussion slides straight into colors, costs and timelines instead of value.","2":"Data and ROI address the neocortex, which understands but does not decide. People argue with a What and join a Why.","3":"Hiding the Why from stakeholders gives up its main power: people argue with a What, but they join a Why."},"9":{"0":"A Why that actions do not back up breaks trust. VW's Phaeton is the book's example of a What contradicting the Why.","2":"A slogan alone is the Why as a campaign: if product and pricing decisions differ, customers will smell the gap.","3":"A Why that shifts by segment is not a belief. A What consistent with one Why is one of Sinek's three conditions."},"10":{"0":"The Why is found by looking backward, not in forecasts. Forecasts help decide the What, not reveal what you believe.","2":"Competitors' positioning tells you about them, not what you believe. Sinek says to look backward, at your own story.","3":"Usage data helps decide the What. It does not reveal the belief, which lies in the past, in the founding story."},"11":{"0":"Slowness is not the reason. The decision is made in the limbic brain, which has no capacity for language at all.","2":"Sinek does not talk about stress. The limbic brain decides in every case, and it simply cannot speak.","3":"The reverse: people decide by feeling first, and only afterward recruit data to justify it."},"12":{"0":"A satisfaction score measures liking, not sharpness. The test is whether the team can quote the line from memory.","2":"Unique wording does not make a Why sharp. The test is whether someone in the hallway can quote it.","3":"Listing features is the What. A Why line is a belief, like teams should spend time on the work, not managing it."},"13":{"0":"A generic Why like making the world better filters no decision. A good Why is sharp enough to say no with.","2":"A metric and a deadline make it a target, which is exactly the problem. Profit and market lead are results, not a Why.","3":"A Why hidden from customers loses its power: people buy why you do it, not what you do."},"14":{"0":"Age is not the critique, and the book includes Apple entering phones in 2007. The weakness is which cases were chosen.","2":"The reverse: the book leans on hand-picked winners, and the companies that collapsed never enter the picture.","3":"The critique is not too many numbers; the book is built on stories. The weakness is that only winners' stories are told."}},"110":{"0":{"0":"Karp argues the opposite: neutrality is an illusion, and refusing to choose is itself a choice.","2":"Karp does not call on governments to build alone. He calls for an alliance between the best engineers and national missions.","3":"Karp says the reverse: the AI era is like the atomic age, and whoever leads writes the rules. He does not call for a pause."},"1":{"0":"Selling to any lawful buyer is exactly the neutrality Karp rejects. Choosing whom to sell to is a value choice.","2":"Palantir built its business on working with Western governments and security agencies, not on avoiding them.","3":"Palantir works with US allies too, like the UK and Israel. The line is democracies versus adversaries, not one country."},"2":{"0":"Those are the Privacy by Design principles from the Velox chapter. Of them, only Data Minimization is in the stack.","2":"Those describe Palantir's engineering culture, the second tool, not the accountability stack.","3":"Those are the four ethical questions for any product, the third tool, not the accountability stack."},"3":{"0":"Karp calls this view technological agnosticism: building powerful tools while refusing to ask what they are for.","2":"Per Karp, values live in the system itself: who is a suspect, what gets shown, who gets a loan. Clean data does not erase that.","3":"Leaving the values to the customer is the neutrality Karp rejects. Choosing whom to serve is the vendor's own value choice."},"4":{"0":"Human-in-the-Loop applies to critical decisions about life, liberty and rights, not to routine scheduling and reports.","2":"That is reversed: in critical decisions the AI only proposes. Reviewing logs afterward keeps no human in the decision.","3":"The human in the loop is the decision-maker who approves the recommendation, not the affected person giving consent."},"5":{"0":"The stage speaks of ethical responsibility, not legal liability for defects. It comes from deciding what to build and for whom.","2":"Supplying whatever the customer asks is hiding behind I just build tools. The gatekeeper defines which deals and uses to refuse.","3":"The PM's power comes from defining what gets built, not from owning a company ethics policy or approving the team's values."},"6":{"0":"Adding privacy after launch is exactly the mistake: ethics and privacy bolted on afterward instead of designed in.","2":"Collecting everything contradicts data minimization: collect only what the mission requires, not everything just in case.","3":"Privacy by Design is the vendor's duty within the design itself: minimal collection, a defined purpose and access control."},"7":{"0":"The test is not about marketing buzz. It asks whether we would be proud: an ethical check, not a PR one.","2":"Clarity of documentation is not the question. The test asks whether the system's story would make us proud.","3":"The test is not about leaks. It imagines the system's story in print and asks whether we would be proud of it."},"8":{"0":"Leaving the lines to regulators is hiding behind I just build. The PM is the ethical gatekeeper of what gets built.","2":"The stage says cyber startups face the same dilemma: whom to sell to and whom to refuse, even at three times the price.","3":"Per the stage, these dilemmas are more alive in Israel than almost anywhere, and the tech units still grow PMs today."},"9":{"0":"A sign-off just before launch is still late. By design means ethical questions in the PRD, design review and sprint planning.","2":"The difference is timing, not layer: from the start of development versus only after a problem, in UI and code alike.","3":"A published code of ethics is not design. By design means ethical questions inside the development process itself."},"10":{"0":"An FDE does not scout markets or demo for sales. They sit with the customer, see the problem up close and fix it on the spot.","2":"An FDE is not an incident-response team. They work at the customer to adapt the product to real needs.","3":"An FDE works at the customer site, not at headquarters. Their value is seeing the problem up close in the field."},"11":{"0":"Speed and scale are How questions. The lesson's first question comes before them: who the end user is, under what oversight.","2":"Price and number of buyers come later. The first ethical question is whom the system serves and how it affects citizens.","3":"What competitors do settles nothing ethical. The first question is whom the system serves, not how it stands out."},"12":{"0":"Data minimization is about collecting less, not compressing what is stored. The goal is privacy, not storage costs.","2":"Collecting broadly is exactly the problem. The principle limits what is collected, not just what is displayed.","3":"Using data only for its purpose is purpose limitation, a separate principle. Minimization limits what is collected at all."},"13":{"0":"Karp's concern is not revenue. It is that democracies will be left with inferior technology while rivals advance.","2":"Karp argues the opposite: if the best Western engineers refuse, China and Russia fill the vacuum.","3":"Karp's argument is about the balance of power, not public opinion or regulation."},"14":{"0":"The book says the reverse: talent aimed at ads and shopping is the problem, not proof of an edge.","2":"The fallacy is about the West's superiority, not first-mover advantage. And Karp ties power to responsibility.","3":"Dismissing employee dissent as indulgence is a criticism aimed at Karp himself by critics, not the winner's fallacy."}},"111":{"0":{"0":"Sinek's circle is not an HR policy. It is built by the leader's behavior, day after day.","2":"External threats are constant and out of your control. The circle protects from internal dangers so energy can turn outward.","3":"A circle that covers only a chosen few creates a court, not a team. Sinek stretches it around everyone."},"1":{"0":"Two pairs are swapped: endorphins mask pain and dopamine rewards achievement; serotonin is pride and oxytocin is trust.","2":"Every role is misplaced: endorphins mask pain, dopamine rewards achievement, serotonin is pride, oxytocin is trust.","3":"Endorphins mask pain; they are not a stress alarm, which is cortisol. And oxytocin builds trust; it does not mask pain."},"2":{"0":"The grouping is wrong: endorphins and dopamine are the selfish pair, serotonin and oxytocin the social one.","2":"Per Sinek, the selfish chemicals are essential. The problem is a culture that rewards only them, not the chemicals themselves.","3":"The split is not body versus mood. The selfish ones drive individual performance; the social ones bind people into a team."},"3":{"0":"Slow trust-building is oxytocin, not dopamine. Dopamine's danger is addiction to individual targets.","2":"Recognition and status are serotonin. Dopamine's risk is a race for individual numbers that runs over colleagues.","3":"Sinek does not tie dopamine to fear of failure; fear and stress are cortisol. Dopamine's risk is every man for himself."},"4":{"0":"Destructive Abundance is not about a backlog. It is when metrics become more real than people and values erode with success.","2":"Generosity is not the problem: at Barry-Wehmiller shared sacrifice actually produced generosity. The danger is values eroding.","3":"Per Sinek, values erode precisely when things go well. Destructive Abundance begins with success, not scarcity."},"5":{"0":"Relaying demands as-is makes the PM a pipe that passes panic along. A leader absorbs pressure and translates it.","2":"Keeping the credit reverses the rule: push credit out to the team and pull responsibility in.","3":"A Circle of Safety without high standards is a social club. And hiding uncertainty is cortisol; share the context."},"6":{"0":"Project Aristotle found the opposite: psychological safety was the strongest predictor of team success.","2":"Google found safety to be stronger than the individual talent of team members, not weaker.","3":"Edmondson's term is a shared team belief that it is safe to take risks, not a personal trait. Sinek's circle is the same idea."},"7":{"0":"Public recognition builds pride and status, which is serotonin. Oxytocin comes from personal contact and trust.","2":"Goals, milestones and bonuses are dopamine, a sense of achievement. Oxytocin is built through personal connection.","3":"Per Sinek, you cannot build oxytocin over email. It takes presence and real human contact, not more updates."},"8":{"0":"A satisfaction survey is not the test. Per the stage, the bug moment measures leadership better than any survey.","2":"Everyone saying all is well can signal fear, like Michal's status meetings with only wins. Safety means problems surface.","3":"Rushing to find who is responsible is blame hunting. In a Circle of Safety the question is what we learned."},"9":{"0":"The title is not about skipping meals. It comes from Marine chow halls, where senior officers eat after their people.","2":"No regulation requires it; it is culture. And the message is care for people, not hierarchy.","3":"The title comes from Marine chow halls, not meetings. Its message is taking care of your people first."},"10":{"0":"Safety does not trickle down on its own. Sinek says to stretch the circle around everyone, not only the senior layer.","2":"Protecting only top performers leaves the rest spending energy on self-protection. A circle for the few is a court.","3":"A circle only for those who agree is a court. The stage's PM even protects the minority opinion in a debate."},"11":{"0":"The finding is the opposite: those carrying the heaviest responsibility are not the first to wear down.","2":"The study found workload is not the poison. Helplessness, a low sense of control, is.","3":"The study followed British civil servants by grade and sense of control. The finding is about control, not competitive roles."},"12":{"0":"Leading on borrowed authority, the CEO said so, burns trust, the stage warns.","2":"Controlling information breaks safety: secrets are cortisol, and the team fills the gaps with imagination, always for the worse.","3":"For Sinek, leadership is a choice, not a rank. A formal reporting line does not make people choose to follow you."},"13":{"0":"Open discussion and a public consequence teach everyone to hide the next bug. Ask what we learned, not who is to blame.","2":"Hiding the bug blocks learning and breaks transparency. Protecting means taking responsibility outward, not concealing.","3":"Reporting the developer's name exposes them instead of protecting them. Next time, problems will simply be hidden."},"14":{"0":"Abstraction for Sinek is about distance from people, not vague goals. The danger is people turning into numbers.","2":"Beyond 150 people, Sinek pushes authority down to local leaders; he does not centralize it.","3":"Sinek says the reverse: distance makes harm easy. Leaders should meet the people their decisions land on."}},"112":{"0":{"0":"This flips the formula: the Objective is the qualitative direction, and the Key Results are the numbers that measure it.","2":"Completed tasks are activity, not results. Key Results measure an outcome, so a list of deliverables does not fit the formula.","3":"That describes MBO, which Grove upgraded: OKRs are quarterly and public, not an annual goal agreed in private."},"1":{"0":"The roles are swapped: the KPI is the stable health metric, and the OKR is the quarterly campaign meant to change something.","2":"OKRs run on a quarterly cycle, not once every few years, and a KPI stays stable over time instead of changing each sprint.","3":"An OKR does include numbers: its Key Results are quantitative. A KPI is a separate ongoing metric, not proof the OKR was met."},"2":{"0":"The labels are reversed: Committed is the promise where 1.0 is expected, and 0.7 counts as success on the Aspirational moonshot.","2":"Aspirational OKRs are scored too: an average of 0.7 counts as success, so they are tracked through the whole quarter.","3":"The split is promise versus ambition, not time horizon. On an Aspirational OKR, reaching 100% means the team aimed too low."},"3":{"0":"CFR stands for Conversations, Feedback, Recognition, and it is continuous. Weekly check-ins stay part of the OKR cycle.","2":"Doerr offers CFRs as the replacement for the annual review, and he recommends keeping OKR scores apart from bonuses.","3":"CFR is not about collecting customer feedback: it is Conversations, Feedback, Recognition between managers and employees."},"4":{"0":"Several KRs per Objective is the normal structure: each Objective gets 2 to 5 Key Results.","2":"Numbers are exactly what a Key Result needs. As the stage quotes Marissa Mayer: it's not a key result unless it has a number.","3":"Stretch targets are encouraged: when everything lands at 100%, it is a sign nobody was stretched."},"5":{"0":"The problem the stage describes is behavioral, not budgetary: reward tied to OKRs pushes people toward safe targets.","2":"The effect runs the other way: with a bonus on the line, people protect themselves with low targets they are sure to hit.","3":"HR approval is not the issue. The risk is fear of failure, which leads to conservative targets and no innovation."},"6":{"0":"The directions are reversed: in Cascading goals flow down from management, while in Aligned the teams set their own OKRs.","2":"The difference is how goals are set, not company size: Cascading flows down and is slow, Aligned comes from the teams.","3":"OKRs are public in both approaches. The difference is who sets the goals: management or the teams themselves."},"7":{"0":"Green starts at 0.7, not only at 1.0. On an aspirational goal, a score of 0.7 already counts as full success.","2":"Each KR is graded on a 0.0 to 1.0 scale, not with stars, and green starts at 0.7.","3":"The scale is 0.0 to 1.0, and green starts at 0.7, not 80%. Red is 0.0 to 0.3, not everything under half."},"8":{"0":"The order is reversed: Grove built the system at Intel, and Doerr, who joined Intel in 1974, learned it from him.","2":"Drucker coined MBO in the 1950s. The one who upgraded it at Intel into the system Doerr brought to Google was Grove.","3":"Page and Brin adopted the method when Doerr presented it at Google in 1999. It came from Grove at Intel."},"9":{"0":"The point of transparency is alignment, not rivalry: you see overlaps and how each team's work connects.","2":"Fear of failure is exactly what the system tries to avoid: it pushes people toward safe targets instead of ambitious ones.","3":"Investor reporting is not the reason. Visibility lets every employee see how their work contributes to the larger goal."},"10":{"0":"This swaps them: the Objective is the qualitative direction, and the Key Result is the number that shows you arrived.","2":"Tasks and features are output. A Key Result measures an outcome, such as Activation rising from 45% to 60%.","3":"A KR does not break the Objective into smaller deadlines: it is the quantitative metric that checks whether it was reached."},"11":{"0":"OKRs are a living system: check-ins exist so a KR that no longer matters can be updated or stopped.","2":"Quietly lowering the bar destroys the honesty the system relies on, and the green score no longer reflects reality.","3":"A red KR calls for a debrief, not a blame hunt, and this one should be stopped openly rather than kept as a failure."},"12":{"0":"Focus is one of the system's four superpowers: only 3 to 5 objectives and a polite no to everything else.","2":"Surfacing duplicate work is part of the Align superpower: goals visible to all reveal overlaps early.","3":"This is something OKRs do provide: good Key Results measure outcomes, not the number of features shipped."},"13":{"0":"More features is still output. Ten launches show no better than five whether the experience improved.","2":"The Objective is meant to be qualitative. The number belongs in the Key Results, and here the weak part is the KR.","3":"A percentage of planned features is still output: you can launch 80% of the plan and the experience stays the same."},"14":{"0":"A Stretch Goal demands creative thinking, not just hard work. Overtime is more of the same, not a new approach.","2":"The stretch is in the target's ambition, not the budget: you aim high enough to force fresh thinking.","3":"A Stretch Goal is set ambitious from the start, so 60-70% is the ideal score. It is not a target widened midway."}},"113":{"0":{"0":"Flow and Transactive Memory are tools the book builds on top of the accelerators, not accelerators. Resonance and Safe Place are missing.","2":"None of these is a Click accelerator: the accelerators are conditions that create connection, like proximity, not team outcomes.","3":"Charisma and humor are personal traits. The book says chemistry is not something you have or lack, but conditions anyone can create."},"1":{"0":"Vulnerability works through trust, not pity: revealing a weakness signals trust and invites the other side to open up too.","2":"The mechanism is trust and reciprocity, not humility: the other side opens up in return, it does not just stop competing.","3":"Lowering expectations is not the point. Vulnerability signals trust, and leaders who admit mistakes are seen as more credible."},"2":{"0":"A shared hometown is surface similarity. Quick-Set Intimacy needs early disclosure, a safe place and an intense shared experience.","2":"Mirroring is not in the recipe. Fast closeness comes from mutual, escalating disclosure in a safe and intense setting.","3":"One disclosure regardless of setting skips the safe place, and the shortcut the stage describes is mutual, escalating disclosure."},"3":{"0":"Nobody needs to learn every area: the efficiency comes from knowing who knows what, so knowledge is not duplicated.","2":"Transactive Memory is distributed, not centralized: the knowledge stays with each expert, and everyone knows whom to ask.","3":"The concept is about knowledge, not shared emotions: each member knows what the others are expert in."},"4":{"0":"Reports and boards share information but create no closeness. Proximity comes from seeing faces and informal talk.","2":"Once a year is not proximity: closeness comes from regular contact, like a daily stand-up and open channels.","3":"Less contact means less proximity. In a remote team closeness has to be created deliberately, not reduced."},"5":{"0":"Flow is about deep engagement, not speed: time disappears because both sides truly listen, not because nobody pauses.","2":"Holding back your views makes it one-sided. Flow needs mutual engagement, with both sides building on each other's words.","3":"Avoiding disagreement is not flow. Flow is full concentration and mutual engagement, not keeping the mood light."},"6":{"0":"A safe place does not replace the other accelerators: it is what enables vulnerability, and they work together as a system.","2":"The order is reversed: the safe place comes first and enables vulnerability, not the other way around.","3":"A safe place means it is okay to err, ask and be real, not that disagreement disappears. Its role is to enable vulnerability."},"7":{"0":"Vulnerability here means admitting uncertainty about the solution, not an unrelated personal story told to win sympathy.","2":"The goal is not gentler ratings. The PM opens up to hear real pains, not polite answers.","3":"A polished vision to confirm invites polite agreement. Admitting uncertainty is what gets the user to open up."},"8":{"0":"Same schools and cities are surface similarity. The book points to deep similarity: shared values and formative experiences.","2":"Similar skills is not the similarity the book means. The PM highlights values the team already shares, not hires look-alikes.","3":"Highlighting shared values is not Groupthink: per the stage it is a shared foundation for working together that strengthens chemistry."},"9":{"0":"The book argues the opposite: chemistry is not something you either have or lack, it is something you build.","2":"The accelerators need personal involvement: admitting, showing up, listening. Roles and rituals alone do not create a click.","3":"Waiting for a first-conversation click misses the point: a PM creates the conditions for connection instead of waiting for it."},"10":{"0":"Level 3 is where connection begins, not where talk gets stuck, and there is no need to jump straight to the peak.","2":"Feelings at level 4 deepen the bond. Going back to neutral facts returns the talk to levels 1-2, where no bond forms.","3":"More background facts stay at level 2. A real bond starts only when someone shares an opinion or feeling, level 3 and up."},"11":{"0":"The book actually recommends creating the conditions on purpose. The danger is staged vulnerability, not planning itself.","2":"The stage says the opposite: leaders who admit mistakes are perceived as more credible, not less.","3":"The accelerators work as a system: one gives an effect, several together give a click. Sticking to one weakens the result."},"12":{"0":"Brafman puts relationships first: Proximity and Transactive Memory take time, and a quick win does not build them.","2":"Fixing processes right away skips getting to know the people. A safe place is built through relationships, not a process map.","3":"Dashboards do not show who knows what. Michal learned it in a get-to-know-you conversation with each team member."},"13":{"0":"Answering every story with your own shifts the focus to you. Resonance is full listening, not taking turns at disclosure.","2":"Resonance is presence, not agreement: you can truly listen without validating every opinion you hear.","3":"Meeting cadence is not resonance, and nothing replaces deep listening: resonance is full presence in the moment."},"14":{"0":"No special training is needed: it starts with admitting a mistake or asking for help. The barrier is courage, not knowledge.","2":"Vulnerability is a shortcut, not a long process: one admitted mistake from Michal broke the silence in the retro.","3":"The stage does not limit it to any culture. The difficulty is the cultural risk in the belief that leaders don't admit weakness."}},"114":{"0":{"0":"RICE is not what this stage centers on. Torres's tree compares paths to an outcome instead of scoring features one by one.","2":"A Gantt chart schedules tasks already decided. Torres's central tool asks which opportunities and solutions lead to an outcome.","3":"Torres uses an experience map to align the trio before interviews, but it is a supporting tool, not the book's central one."},"1":{"0":"The trio is not senior executives: it is the product team itself, the three who run discovery and interviews together.","2":"The trio is the team doing discovery, not the customers being interviewed. All three join the same interviews.","3":"The trio is people, not research methods: product, design and engineering sitting in the same interview."},"2":{"0":"A research round once a quarter is the project approach Torres moves away from. She asks for weekly contact.","2":"Interviewing only when a feature is planned just validates a chosen solution. Torres asks for a fixed weekly habit.","3":"Once a month is too rare: Torres asks for at least one customer touchpoint every week, as a fixed habit."},"3":{"0":"These are the four levels of the OST, not assumption types. Assumptions are about whether a given solution will work.","2":"Scalability and Urgency are not on Torres's list. Beyond the familiar four, she adds Ethical: could it cause harm?","3":"These are factors for choosing an opportunity, like how often it comes up and how painful it is, not assumptions about a solution."},"4":{"0":"A Snapshot is a written one-page summary, not a frame from the recording: a quote, an experience map sketch, opportunities, surprises.","2":"The Snapshot is written after the interview, not sent before it. It turns each conversation into a page in a living archive.","3":"A full transcript is the opposite of a Snapshot: the point is one page the team actually uses, not raw text."},"5":{"0":"The stage does not justify comparison by statistical significance. It is there to curb bias and surface real preferences.","2":"Comparison does not replace assumption mapping: both are links in one chain, and you map assumptions before you test.","3":"Build speed is not the point. Comparison turns yes or no into which is most promising, so evidence picks the winner."},"6":{"0":"Torres puts discovery in the trio's hands: product, design and engineering interview together, not a separate research team.","2":"Validating a chosen solution is what Torres avoids: she starts from customer needs and compares several solutions.","3":"A quarterly sprint is still a project with a start and an end. Torres asks for a weekly habit that runs alongside delivery."},"7":{"0":"Torres expects several solutions per opportunity and compares them. She does not assume there is one right answer.","2":"A requested feature is often a solution in disguise: I need a dashboard is a solution, and the opportunity is the need behind it.","3":"Solutions still need assumption tests: choosing the opportunity first does not prove that any solution will work."},"8":{"0":"An executive's pet feature still goes on the tree: Michal tested one with five customers instead of building it outright.","2":"Torres's answer to scarce resources is a small, cheap habit, under three hours a week, not skipping discovery.","3":"A launched product still shapes user behavior, and anything that touches the user experience calls for Discovery."},"9":{"0":"The engineer is part of discovery from the start and joins interviews, not just estimating effort after the PM decides.","2":"Usability is not the engineer's lens: the engineer brings the technical view and checks whether the solution can be built.","3":"Viability, whether it works for the business, is not the engineer's lens. The engineer checks whether it can be built."},"10":{"0":"That describes a roadmap of features and dates. The OST starts from a desired outcome and maps needs before solutions.","2":"Starting from customer requests skips the opportunity level: a request is often a solution in disguise.","3":"The direction is reversed: the tree starts at the outcome and goes down to solutions, not up from a chosen solution."},"11":{"0":"Churn is a lagging business outcome shaped by many factors outside the trio's reach, so it makes a poor team goal.","2":"A traction metric like clicks on one button is too narrow to be a team goal and leaves no room for creativity.","3":"Shipping features is output, not an outcome: the trio can reach the finish line while churn does not move."},"12":{"0":"Outsourcing the interviews breaks the habit: the trio itself should hold weekly, direct contact with customers.","2":"Batching interviews once a quarter turns discovery back into a project. The point is weekly, ongoing contact.","3":"Power Users flatter. Churned users, hesitant users and a competitor's customers teach the most, so variety matters."},"13":{"0":"Asking what to build collects opinions. People predict their own behavior poorly, so the answers mislead.","2":"Would you use it is speculation about the future. Because of the Say-Do Gap, such answers sound good and mislead.","3":"A 1 to 10 rating is still an opinion. It reveals no context, emotion or workarounds, which is where opportunities come from."},"14":{"0":"One solution per opportunity skips what Torres stresses: pick one target opportunity, then compare several solutions for it.","2":"Building a solution for all 8 opportunities spreads the team thin and gives up comparing solutions for the same opportunity.","3":"Voting on features leans on requests, often solutions in disguise. The trio picks the opportunity from interview evidence."}},"115":{"0":{"0":"The book's complaint is the opposite: too much feature detail with dates, which hardens into commitments, not too little.","2":"More features would deepen the problem: a feature list focuses on output and loses the why behind the work.","3":"The book actually wants a steady update rhythm: weekly, monthly, quarterly. A roadmap left alone becomes a museum piece."},"1":{"0":"Grouping by product area is still a feature list. A theme names a goal or problem, such as improving team collaboration.","2":"Key customers' requests are still features, and a roadmap run by requests is the Sales-Driven anti-pattern.","3":"Bundling features into launches is about release timing. A theme replaces specific features with a strategic goal."},"2":{"0":"The horizons are deliberately not tied to specific quarters: they show how certain the team is, not calendar dates.","2":"The horizons describe certainty, not teams. The same team works on Now and plans Next and Later.","3":"The horizons are about time and certainty, not customer types. Each one is tagged with High, Medium or Low confidence."},"3":{"0":"Customers should get general themes plus a disclaimer, not promises, and even the sales view shows no dates.","2":"Tailoring is not about secrecy: customers see what is on the way, framed as problems being solved, not only what shipped.","3":"The versions differ in what each audience cares about, not in review cadence: strategy, detail or general direction."},"4":{"0":"Outcomes are not only financial: they are changes for the customer, such as raising the Activation rate.","2":"An outcome-driven roadmap sets goals up front. It does not wait for proven results before it is updated.","3":"It is built ahead as a plan of desired outcomes, not written in hindsight from what was already built."},"5":{"0":"Working with Sales is fine: sales and CS insights are a valid input. The anti-pattern is letting every request in unfiltered.","2":"Showing sales targets is not the issue. The anti-pattern is every deal or loud request becoming a roadmap item.","3":"This describes the opposite. A Sales-Driven Roadmap has too many customer requests in it, not too few."},"6":{"0":"Once a year is too rare: a roadmap that is not updated becomes a museum piece within weeks.","2":"Waiting for a dramatic change leaves the roadmap stale. The book wants a steady rhythm, not updates only in a crisis.","3":"Daily updates are not the rhythm the book gives: a short weekly pass over Now, and less often for Next and Later."},"7":{"0":"A confidence level says how certain an item is, not how much budget it gets.","2":"Confidence levels describe certainty, not ownership. They tell the audience how likely an item is to change.","3":"Confidence tags are for managing expectations: they mark what may change. They are not a ranking that sets build order."},"8":{"0":"Exact release dates belong in the release plan, not the roadmap. The roadmap answers where we are going and why.","2":"This swaps them: the backlog holds tasks for the coming sprints, and the roadmap is the long-term direction.","3":"Reversed again: the backlog is the detailed work list, not a management summary, and the roadmap is the big picture."},"9":{"0":"An optimistic date that slips becomes a broken promise. Michal's customer waved a slide as if it were a signed contract.","2":"Refusing leaves the customer with nothing. Now/Next/Later with a confidence level gives an honest answer without a date.","3":"Sending the customer to developers dodges the question. The PM can answer honestly with Now/Next/Later and a confidence level."},"10":{"0":"Unveiling a roadmap for the first time in a full forum is the classic mistake: every objection surfaces there, live.","2":"Top-down sign-off skips the stakeholders. Shuttle diplomacy builds support with each of them before the meeting.","3":"Building around the most requested items lets requests, not strategy, decide the roadmap."},"11":{"0":"The book's audiences are those who act on the roadmap day to day. The press and partners are not among its four.","2":"This turns the roadmap into a budget and deadline document. Exact dates belong in the release plan, not the roadmap.","3":"These are all roles inside the product team. The book's four audiences also include sales and CS, and customers."},"12":{"0":"Splitting it in two keeps the same false precision. The issue is detail a year out, not the number of items per document.","2":"Adding committed dates makes it worse: exact dates are out of the roadmap and harden into rigid commitments.","3":"Ranking does not fix it: a ranked list of 30 features is still a feature list that will break as things change."},"13":{"0":"Exact release dates are explicitly in the out group: they belong in the release plan, not the roadmap.","2":"The full backlog is out as well: it belongs in the dev tools, not on the roadmap.","3":"Task-level detail is out: tasks belong in the release plan and dev tools, not in the roadmap's core."},"14":{"0":"Dual Track is not bugs versus features: it separates exploring what to build from building it.","2":"Dual Track is not internal versus external. Audience views come from one source of truth, not a secret roadmap.","3":"The split is not by platform: Dual Track runs discovery and delivery in parallel, each feeding the other."}},"116":{"0":{"0":"Mixing technical and business items is not Patton's complaint: each item can make sense, but the list shows no journey.","2":"Better sorting would still leave a one-dimensional list. What is missing is context: where each story sits in the journey.","3":"More detailed criteria per story would not show the journey. The problem is the missing second dimension, not detail."},"1":{"0":"The Backbone is about user activities, not bugs: the big steps the user takes, such as search, view and pay.","2":"The vertical axis is depth: details, variations and edge cases under each activity. The Backbone is the top row.","3":"Stakeholders are not on the map. The Backbone is the row of the user's big activities, in the order they happen."},"2":{"0":"A Walking Skeleton actually works: the user completes the whole journey, even crudely. A design-only prototype does not walk.","2":"It is a working slice of the product, not a requirements document: the thinnest cut that crosses the entire Backbone.","3":"That describes a persona. A Walking Skeleton is a minimal working version that lets the user finish the journey end to end."},"3":{"0":"These are planning constraints, not Patton's Cs. His three Cs are about how a story is told, discussed and confirmed.","2":"This is strategic analysis, not about user stories. The Cs Patton adopts come from the XP world and describe a story's life.","3":"These are workflow steps for an item. Patton's Cs stress that the card only invites a conversation, which is the main thing."},"4":{"0":"The card is not worthless: it records the story and invites the conversation. The point is that it cannot replace talking.","2":"Space on the card is not the issue: even a flawless document nobody discussed creates only the illusion of understanding.","3":"The conversation is not about dates or commitments. It is about everyone understanding the same thing about the story."},"5":{"0":"Slicing by teams builds separate parts that join only at the end. A release strip delivers a complete journey right away.","2":"Picking top-scoring stories one by one builds towers, not strips: you might get a rich cart and no way to pay.","3":"Slicing by estimated time ignores the journey. Each release has to cross every activity in the Backbone."},"6":{"0":"Dividing the work misses the point: the value is in building together, so everyone understands the whole journey.","2":"Impressing management is not the goal. The real output is shared understanding, which improves everyday decisions.","3":"Understanding only your own part is exactly what gets lost: when everyone knows the whole journey, decisions improve."},"7":{"0":"Neither map is for investors or a database: Big Picture serves strategic and release planning, Detailed serves sprints.","2":"They are two zoom levels: first zoom out to the whole product, then zoom in on the flow you are building now.","3":"Both maps contain stories. The Big Picture shows the big activities; the Detailed adds edge cases and acceptance criteria."},"8":{"0":"The map does not replace the ritual: in Sprint Planning the team still picks stories, now with their context.","2":"The map gives context and the next strip; it does not fix in advance how many sprints the releases will take.","3":"The map assigns no one: it shows where each story sits in the journey, and the team still decides who does what."},"9":{"0":"The map is supposed to change: a map that never moves is scenery. The updates are not why it is a secondary output.","2":"A living map does guide work, in planning, refinement and review. The point is that building it together matters more.","3":"Patton does not rank the backlog above the map. He says the conversation that builds the map matters more than the artifact."},"10":{"0":"This is a generic delivery process with gates, not mapping steps. Patton's steps begin by framing the problem.","2":"These are sprint-management steps. Mapping comes before that: frame the problem first, then lay out the big picture.","3":"This is a design-process model, not Patton's mapping steps, which start with framing the problem and the big picture."},"11":{"0":"The map raises questions as it is built, such as what if an employee forgets to log a whole week. It is not a question list.","2":"Mapping does not replace research: one of its steps is validating the map by telling the story to real users.","3":"Story mapping is not an analytics tool. The discovery comes from the team conversation, which exposes gaps and assumptions."},"12":{"0":"Patton allows both: a physical wall or a shared digital board. The tool matters less than building the map together.","2":"The stage sets no story limit. The warning is against detailing everything up front: depth only where you build now.","3":"Bringing QA and design into the room is recommended, not a mistake: every discipline should be there."},"13":{"0":"Everything requested is the opposite of minimal. Patton asks what the user needs to achieve, not how many features fit.","2":"One feature in full depth is a tower, not a strip: an interrupted journey is no journey, however polished one part is.","3":"Copying a competitor is output thinking. An MVP is the smallest slice that delivers complete value across the Backbone."},"14":{"0":"Stories are not all detailed in advance: depth is added only where you build now, and refinement is where it happens.","2":"The vertical axis shows depth, not priority. Details and variations sit lower, not less important stories.","3":"Refinement stays: it is the session where the team breaks down cards, and with a map it updates the map too."}},"117":{"0":{"0":"Cost is not the distinction. Output is what was shipped, and an Outcome is the behavior change it causes.","2":"Outcomes are measurable: 70% of signups completing first setup is a number. Being measurable is a mark of a good Outcome.","3":"Both matter to the whole team. The difference is between what was built and the change in people's behavior."},"1":{"0":"Cost and ROI ask about the investment, not about people. The magic question starts with which behavior will change.","2":"Copying competitors is the Feature Parity trap. The question is which behavior it would change for us.","3":"Meeting the schedule is the Roadmap Trap: celebrating dates instead of behavior targets."},"2":{"0":"Both have a role: lagging ones like revenue sum up the result. The advantage of leading ones is that they leave time to act.","2":"The difference is when the signal moves, not the horizon: a leading indicator moves early, a lagging one after the fact.","3":"Both kinds are numbers: weekly usage is a leading indicator and revenue a lagging one, and both are quantitative."},"3":{"0":"Novelty, looks and cost say nothing about behavior. A good Outcome describes a measurable change in what people do.","2":"Formal approval does not make an Outcome good. It must be measurable, influenceable, behavioral and tied to business value.","3":"The stage sets no one-sprint limit: Outcomes are set per quarter, such as Activation from 30% to 50%."},"4":{"0":"The trap is not slowness: velocity is high and the charts are green, but nobody checks whether anyone uses the features.","2":"Changing direction is not the trap. The Velocity Trap is celebrating points closed instead of change in the user.","3":"Meetings are not the issue. The trap is measuring production speed instead of the change created for users."},"5":{"0":"Technology layers tie a team to a part of the stack, not to a result. Seiden gives each team a behavioral target.","2":"Projects with fixed dates commit to a solution. Seiden commits teams to a result and lets them choose the path.","3":"Departments describe hierarchy, not results. Seiden names teams by outcome, such as the activation team."},"6":{"0":"Output is the means, not the job: shipping everything while no behavior moves is exactly the story that opens the book.","2":"A CEO request is not the criterion. Output matters in itself for technical debt, regulation and enabling infrastructure.","3":"Seiden does not say never: technical debt, regulation and infrastructure for future Outcomes are recognized exceptions."},"7":{"0":"They are separate rungs: an Outcome is behavior the team moves directly, Impact a broad result it only contributes to.","2":"Impact is not a technical metric. It is the broad business result: revenue, profitability and market share.","3":"Output is the bottom rung of the ladder and Impact the top: what was shipped versus the broad business result."},"8":{"0":"The difference is what it lists, not how far ahead: Outcomes per quarter instead of a feature list with dates.","2":"It still has a time frame: Outcomes per quarter. The team commits to a result rather than to a specific solution.","3":"It guides the teams themselves: each team gets a behavioral target and picks its own way to reach it."},"9":{"0":"The Build Trap is not about QA. It is measuring success by the amount delivered instead of the value created.","2":"Too many parallel MVPs is not the definition. The trap is a feature race in which nobody asks what changed.","3":"Slow development is not the trap. Its sign is shipping a lot while nobody can say which business result moved."},"10":{"0":"Feature Parity is about competitors, not internal coordination: the competitor has it, so we must too.","2":"The trap is not features looking alike. It is copying a competitor without asking which behavior it would change.","3":"A big customer's request is a different pressure. Feature Parity copies competitors without checking the effect on an Outcome."},"11":{"0":"They fit together: Key Results are behavioral Outcomes. Seiden only warns against KRs that are a task list.","2":"OKRs do not replace Outcomes, they carry them. The method works only when the Key Results are behavior changes.","3":"Outputs belong in the initiatives, not the Key Results. KRs that are deliverables turn OKRs into a task list."},"12":{"0":"Budget, competitors and headcount skip the core: which behavior will change. Without that, it is too early to build.","2":"These are planning documents, not questions about behavior. The five questions start from the human change.","3":"Starting with which feature to build is Output thinking. The first question is which behavior will change."},"13":{"0":"Saving money is not the reason. An Outcome team picks its own path, an email or a template, to move a behavior.","2":"Clear boundaries are not the point. The benefit is freedom to try different solutions toward one behavior target.","3":"Reports for leadership are not the reason. A team tied to a result, not a feature, can experiment until behavior moves."},"14":{"0":"Language has nothing to do with it. A bad Outcome is Output in disguise, not measurable, or an Impact out of reach.","2":"Ambition is not the test. Grow revenue by 30% is ambitious but bad: it is Impact, outside the team's control.","3":"A good Outcome is measured in numbers, such as task completion from 60% to 80%. Being measurable is a core criterion."}},"118":{"0":{"0":"These are generic management stages. The pyramid starts with the market, who the customer is and which needs go unmet, then the product.","2":"This is a growth funnel, not the pyramid. Olsen's layers start from the target customer and needs, not from acquisition.","3":"These are steps of a learning loop, not the pyramid's layers, which rise from the target customer up to the UX."},"1":{"0":"The framework is about customer needs, not the team or the code: how important a need is and how satisfied customers are.","2":"It is not a pricing tool. It maps each need by importance and satisfaction to find needs that go underserved.","3":"It measures customers' needs, not employee satisfaction: high importance with low satisfaction marks the opportunity."},"2":{"0":"Paying users are not the measure. The question is how users would feel if they could no longer use the product.","2":"NPS is a separate loyalty metric. The stage presents it as a complement to the Must-Have Score, not a substitute.","3":"Shipping roadmap items on time says nothing about market fit. The score comes from users' answers, not delivery data."},"3":{"0":"Kano classifies by customer reaction, not build effort: whether a feature is expected, linear, or a surprise.","2":"Input, process and output describe a system, not customer value. Kano sorts features by how customers react to them.","3":"Kano is not about time horizons. It sorts features by the kind of satisfaction they create for customers."},"4":{"0":"This is a generic delivery sequence. Olsen's process starts in the problem space: target customer first, then underserved needs.","2":"It starts with research and ideas instead of the customer and needs, and reaches full build before testing with customers.","3":"These are generic phases. Olsen's steps climb the pyramid, from the target customer up to a prototype tested with customers."},"5":{"0":"One sprint is not a signal. Olsen looks for evidence that builds up over several rounds, like a Must-Have Score that stays low.","2":"Velocity measures the team's pace, not market fit. Pivot signals come from users: retention and the Must-Have Score.","3":"A competitor's entry is not on Olsen's list. The signals are about your own users: a low Must-Have Score and falling retention."},"6":{"0":"A polished UI built at great cost is the opposite: Wizard of Oz tests whether the solution works without building the tech.","2":"There is no real algorithm behind it: a person does the work behind the curtain while the customer thinks it is automatic.","3":"No-code is a way to build. Wizard of Oz is about hiding manual work behind an interface that looks automated."},"7":{"0":"Cost is not the reason. The bottom layers are the market: if the customer or need is wrong, excellent upper layers won't help.","2":"The order is not about easy feedback. The upper layers rest on the lower ones, and a weak base brings down everything above.","3":"Investors are not the reason. Without the right customer and need, no feature set or UX will create fit."},"8":{"0":"A curve that keeps rising is not the pattern: retention naturally drops at first, and fit shows when it levels off.","2":"A curve that keeps sliding without leveling off is a pivot signal, not fit, even if churn slows a little.","3":"Ups and downs driven by campaigns show no stable core. Fit looks like a curve that levels off at a positive level."},"9":{"0":"They do not contradict: the stage presents strong PMF with early adopters as a precondition for crossing the chasm.","2":"Neither replaces the other: Olsen deals with reaching fit, and crossing the chasm comes after it.","3":"They are directly linked: PMF with early adopters is the starting point for moving into the mainstream."},"10":{"0":"It is not a split between departments. The problem space is customer needs; the solution space is everything you build.","2":"It is not about timing: the warning is against jumping to solutions before understanding the problem, at any stage.","3":"The problem space is not academic: it is what the customer is trying to accomplish, with not a word about technology."},"11":{"0":"QA is not the reason. A narrowly defined customer lets you understand specific needs and build a precise value proposition.","2":"Investors are not the reason. Aim at everyone and you hit no one: a broad customer blurs the value proposition.","3":"The aim is not reaching more people cheaply. It is a sharp value proposition: start with a niche and serve it precisely."},"12":{"0":"A Delighter is defined by the customer's reaction, not by cost: an unexpected feature that thrills when it is there.","2":"Kano is about the customer, not the internal team: a Delighter surprises and pleases users.","3":"Delighters are worth building, in moderation: one great one creates love. Over time they become Must-Haves, not useless."},"13":{"0":"The Opportunity Score uses no revenue or probability: it combines how important a need is and how satisfied customers are.","2":"Market size is a different question. The score ranks customer needs by importance and current satisfaction.","3":"The score is not an average of satisfaction metrics. Importance counts twice, minus satisfaction, to find underserved needs."},"14":{"0":"The reason is not cost or team size: each round produces learning that may send you back to an earlier layer.","2":"Customers preferring gradual releases is not the reason. Iteration exists because tests change your assumptions.","3":"Olsen is not just following Agile. The loop exists because each test reveals information that changes earlier layers."}},"119":{"0":{"0":"The chasm is about buyers, not architecture: the gap between visionaries and pragmatists in the adoption cycle.","2":"B2B versus B2C is not the gap. The chasm lies between two buyer groups: Early Adopters and the Early Majority.","3":"The chasm opens once a product already has first customers: it lies between visionary buyers and pragmatic ones."},"1":{"0":"The difference is motivation, not intelligence: a visionary wants a competitive leap, a pragmatist a safe, proven improvement.","2":"Price is not the dividing line. Visionaries forgive bugs and skip references; pragmatists demand reliability and references.","3":"The split is by attitude, not company size: a visionary seeks a leap and accepts risk, a pragmatist wants proof from peers."},"2":{"0":"The Whole Product is not finishing the original roadmap: it adds support, documentation and integrations around the core.","2":"More backlog features is not the point. Pragmatists need the full solution around the core: support, integrations, training.","3":"Passing QA is part of a working core. The Whole Product also includes support, integrations and an ecosystem."},"3":{"0":"Going after the largest market spreads your force: better a big fish in a small pond than a tiny fish in a huge one.","2":"A beachhead focuses on one niche with one Whole Product, not a separate product for every segment.","3":"Enterprise is not the rule: the beachhead is a narrow niche with an urgent reason to buy, chosen for the leverage it gives."},"4":{"0":"It is not one shot and a hope: each niche won deliberately knocks down the adjacent one through references.","2":"The lane runs through adjacent niches, not only enterprises: the beachhead in the example is R&D teams at SaaS companies.","3":"The product is not replaced for each niche: references and the Whole Product carry over to similar niches."},"5":{"0":"A big TAM is not the test: the niche must be small enough to win, with an urgent reason to buy.","2":"More customers is not the measure. The niche must be big enough to matter yet small enough to win.","3":"Existing customers help, but the criteria are broader: an urgent reason to buy, a feasible Whole Product, leverage to the next niche."},"6":{"0":"The model does not blame the hardware: visionaries bought it, but pragmatists had no reason to buy and no Whole Product.","2":"Price is not the model's explanation. Pragmatists had no compelling reason to buy and no Whole Product for daily life.","3":"Awareness was not the gap. The model's diagnosis: no compelling everyday reason to buy and no Whole Product."},"7":{"0":"For Moore, positioning is the ticket into the pragmatist market, not a slogan: for whom, which problem, which category, how it differs.","2":"Positioning is not a technical document: it states in one sentence who the product is for and how it differs from the alternative.","3":"A price list compares packages. Positioning places the product in a category and against an alternative."},"8":{"0":"Without product-market fit yet, the stage points to books like The Lean Product Playbook. The chasm comes later.","2":"Once in mass growth, the challenges are different. The book fits the stretch between first customers and the mainstream.","3":"Shutting a product down is not the chasm. The book deals with growing from early adopters into the broad market."},"9":{"0":"Payment is not the issue. Early Adopters are a small slice of the market, and pragmatists do not accept them as a reference.","2":"Fickleness is not the reason given. The problem is that visionaries' love does not persuade pragmatists, who want peer references.","3":"Contacts are not the problem: pragmatists talk mostly to each other and do not accept visionaries as a reference."},"10":{"0":"These numbers are off: Innovators are about 2.5% and Early Adopters 13.5%, not 10% and 20%.","2":"Close but off: Innovators are about 2.5%, Early Adopters 13.5%, and each majority is 34%.","3":"Each majority is 34%, not 40%, and Early Adopters are 13.5%, not 9%."},"11":{"0":"The analogy does not come from the book's title: it illustrates concentrating force on one beach before advancing.","2":"Budget is not the point. The lesson is focus: one landing beach, not five markets in parallel.","3":"The analogy is about focus, not courage: win one beach completely, then advance from it."},"12":{"0":"Here it is about sales and marketing, not developers: doubling the sales team before a repeatable win in one niche.","2":"Raising prices is not the issue. The danger is a big sales team wasting time on pragmatists who won't buy without references.","3":"Geographic expansion is not the definition. Premature scaling is growing sales before a stable beachhead exists."},"13":{"0":"The PM does not write all the code. The PM maps the gaps and prioritizes integrations and support.","2":"The PM works with others: drafting positioning with marketing and testing it on real customers, based on field interviews.","3":"More features is the wrong move: during the crossing, integrations and support come before shiny features."},"14":{"0":"Wealth is not the trait. Conservatives buy only once it is standard, and they are sold through standardization and a low price.","2":"The Late Majority are not more technical. They are conservatives who buy only once a technology has become standard.","3":"The groups are defined by their attitude to new technology, not by B2B or B2C."}},"120":{"0":{"0":"Focusing only on Discovery is what other books do. This one covers the whole field, from defining the role to career.","2":"The book is not only for senior leaders: it also covers breaking into the role and growing in it, as a textbook for the field.","3":"It is not a technical manual: the PM owns the why and the what, and engineers decide the how."},"1":{"0":"Those are not the four Ds. The loop starts with Discovery, deciding what to build, not with data collection or debugging.","2":"The loop starts with Discovery, finding the right problem, and has no Delight stage: Delivery leads back to Discovery.","3":"There is no Done in this loop: the book says there is no done, only learned, and Delivery returns to Discovery."},"2":{"0":"The book wants a reasonable level in all six families, not one strength: the PM's job is to connect the specialists.","2":"A PM need not be an engineer: the aim is a good-enough level in each family, and the how belongs to the engineers.","3":"Dropping the technical family breaks the aim of a reasonable level in every family, and it is Michal's lowest score."},"3":{"0":"HEART measures experience quality, but it does not split the journey into stages, so it won't show where users drop off.","2":"OKRs align goals but do not show where the journey breaks. A revenue target for sales skips the funnel stages.","3":"A North Star focuses teams on one metric, but a single number cannot pinpoint which funnel stage is breaking."},"4":{"0":"Sign-ups measure attraction, not the value customers get: people can sign up and never reach value.","2":"MRR is a lagging financial result, not the value customers get. A North Star reflects the core value the product delivers.","3":"Features launched are output, not outcome. They show what the teams produced, not what value customers got."},"5":{"0":"Reach alone decides nothing: RICE weighs all four parts, and A's Effort of 4 divides its score down to 400.","2":"Confidence under 100% only scales the score: A's 80% is multiplied in. The score still works and turns argument into discussion.","3":"This multiplies by Effort instead of dividing. The right math for idea A is 2,000×1×0.8÷4 = 400."},"6":{"0":"The PM does not assign tasks, approve code or grade the team: the PM brings the why and what, the team owns the how.","2":"The PM attends all four ceremonies, including Standup to remove blockers and Retro to improve the process.","3":"Handing over documents without taking part is what the book warns against: specs without conversation leave the team without the why."},"7":{"0":"The phrase is not about pay or perks. It describes owning the product's success without formal authority.","2":"A PM has no power to fire anyone: that is exactly the formal authority missing. Influence comes from data and trust.","3":"A PM does not control the company budget. The point is responsibility for the result without formal authority over others."},"8":{"0":"The order is scrambled: PMF is the Introduction goal, scaling comes in Growth, and reinvention is one Decline option.","2":"The PM keeps leading at every stage. What changes is the focus: from PMF to scaling, to optimization, to the product's future.","3":"The job changes shape with the stage: from finding PMF, through scaling and optimization, to deciding the product's future."},"9":{"0":"Writing code is not on the book's list of mistakes. The list opens with trying to please everyone and fearing no.","2":"Remote work is not on the book's list. The leading mistakes are pleasing everyone and avoiding no, which blur focus.","3":"The book warns of the opposite: ignoring the data and deciding by gut feeling alone is one of the common mistakes."}},"121":{"0":{"0":"These are product life stages, not CIRCLES. The method opens with Comprehend: understanding the situation through clarifying questions.","2":"These are not the seven steps. CIRCLES moves from understanding the situation and the customer to a summarized recommendation.","3":"This list jumps to roadmap and code. CIRCLES ships nothing: it is an answer structure that ends in a recommendation."},"1":{"0":"Lin argues the opposite: candidates fail not for lack of knowledge but for lack of structure.","2":"Lin does not blame missing experience. A candidate with a framework sounds like someone who already thinks like a PM.","3":"Clarifying questions are recommended: Lin asks for at least 2-3 before solving. Skipping them reads as impulsive."},"2":{"0":"Listing solutions first is the Solution-First mistake: ideas not rooted in a customer come out generic.","2":"Evaluate comes late and weighs trade-offs between solutions, not what the interviewer wants to hear.","3":"Summarize is the last step and closes with a recommendation. The first step is understanding the situation."},"3":{"0":"Marketing is not part of AARM, and the order is off: the funnel runs from Acquisition through Retention to Monetization.","2":"AARM tracks the user lifecycle, from first exposure to money. Awareness, Reach and Metrics are not its stages.","3":"Aim, Act, Refine is a generic loop. AARM is a funnel: Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Monetization."},"4":{"0":"That describes the Primary metric: what the feature is meant to move. A guardrail watches what must not drop.","2":"That describes the North Star, the one metric that aligns all teams. A guardrail protects the rest of the product.","3":"That describes secondary metrics, which complete the picture. A guardrail checks that nothing else gets hurt."},"5":{"0":"This sounds like a generic project report. DIGS opens with Dramatize, a vivid opening, not a plain description.","2":"DIGS does not open by defining the situation but with Dramatize, and its I stands for Indicate alternatives.","3":"The D is right, but I stands for Indicate alternatives and S for Summarize, not Implement and Sell."},"6":{"0":"DIGS opens with Dramatize, not Define, and what it adds is alternatives that show judgment, not goals.","2":"DIGS does not open with the result: results come last, in Summarize. Go through stresses I, not team effort.","3":"DIGS opens with Dramatize, not Details, and adds Indicate alternatives, which STAR is missing."},"7":{"0":"Segment size is not her reason: Dana chose by engagement potential, a local uses it weekly and a tourist once a year.","2":"Ad revenue is not the reason. The choice rests on recurring engagement: weekly use versus once a year.","3":"The interviewer did not narrow it: picking a persona is the candidate's job, and Dana justified the choice herself."},"8":{"0":"25 minutes on solutions leaves no time for the customer and trade-offs. Lin gives List about 5 minutes.","2":"Skipping Comprehend reads as impulsive. It takes only 2-3 minutes and narrows the problem.","3":"Summarize takes about 3 minutes. Twenty minutes on it starves the steps that build the answer."},"9":{"0":"Lin holds that a product for everyone is a product for no one: the solution comes out generic and solves no real problem.","2":"A numeric target would not fix this. The problem is the missing persona, not a missing quantity.","3":"Picking one persona happens in Identify, before Cut. Cut prioritizes one need of that persona."},"10":{"0":"That is a different fatal mistake, Boil the Ocean: solving everything for everyone at once.","2":"A missing metric is a separate weakness. Solution-First means proposing a solution before understanding the problem.","3":"That is inverted time management, a different mistake. Solution-First is jumping to a solution too early."},"11":{"0":"This recaps the steps instead of recommending. Lin wants a clear recommendation with a measure of success.","2":"There is a recommendation and a reason, but no primary KPI, guardrail or risk to judge success by.","3":"Leaving the call to the team ends with no recommendation. Lin asks you to close with I recommend X because, plus a success measure."},"12":{"0":"Pausing to think is fine, but doing it in silence hides the reasoning. The interviewer wants to see how you think.","2":"Thinking before answering is not Solution-First, which is jumping to a solution. The problem here is the silence.","3":"Silent Thinking is one of Lin's five fatal mistakes, not a minor issue: the thought process is part of what is judged."},"13":{"0":"Swapping one vague verb for another fixes nothing: there are still no numbers and no timeframe.","2":"Growing revenue is still vague without numbers. Lin's fix is a measurable target, like Activation from 30% to 45%.","3":"Users will love it is even vaguer. Lin asks for numbers: specific, measurable and with a timeframe."},"14":{"0":"Innovation is not the mantra: for Lin, a mediocre idea with clear structure beats a brilliant idea without it.","2":"Speed is not the message. Lin even warns against jumping to a solution in the tenth second.","3":"Consensus is not the mantra. Lin asks you to surface trade-offs and finish with a clear recommendation."}},"122":{"0":{"0":"The book's subtitle counts 167 actual questions and answers, not 100.","2":"250 overstates it: the book's full title promises 167 actual interview questions and answers.","3":"500 is far off: the book collects 167 real PM interview questions, with sample answers."},"1":{"0":"These are engineering areas, not PM interview categories. A PM is not measured on writing code.","2":"These are company departments, not question types. The book sorts by what a question tests, like estimation or metrics.","3":"These are business functions. The book's categories are question types, such as strategy, estimation and behavioral."},"2":{"0":"3C is not a calculation method: it is Ohmae's framework that looks at the company, the customers and the competition.","2":"Cost, Capacity, Capability are not the 3C. Ohmae's 3C looks at our capability, the customer's need and the competitor.","3":"Close, but cash and channel are not in it: 3C is Company, Customers and Competition."},"3":{"1":"This swaps them: Top-Down starts from a macro figure like Israel's population, Bottom-Up from a small unit like one table.","2":"Neither gives an exact number: estimation aims for the right order of magnitude, and both methods are legitimate.","3":"The difference is the starting point, not how you segment: a macro figure going down, or a small unit going up."},"4":{"1":"Precision is not the goal: 1,432,500 sounds less credible than about 1.5M. Fake precision is on the list of mistakes.","2":"Declaring assumptions is a separate golden rule. The Sanity Check comes at the end and tests whether the result makes sense.","3":"Rounding is a rule of its own. The Sanity Check asks whether the final number is reasonable, such as 15% of the population."},"5":{"0":"Revenue and satisfaction are not the required set, and a guardrail is missing: what must not get hurt.","1":"Leading and lagging describe timing, not the four required types. A North Star and a Primary metric are missing.","3":"Input and output are not part of the set, and there is no North Star, Primary metric or guardrail here."},"6":{"1":"Yoav's strategy deliberately avoided a UX fight and leaned on Microsoft's Enterprise relationships instead.","2":"Selling Teams as a separate product is the opposite of the bundle that Yoav's strategy relied on.","3":"Price was not the lever in the case: the edge came from the Office 365 bundle for Enterprise customers."},"7":{"1":"Clarifying questions are not the mistake. The iron rule is never to fake knowledge.","2":"Guessing out loud from the term's name, while admitting you don't know, is exactly what the book recommends. Faking is the mistake.","3":"The book wants concept-level answers: a PM is measured on an informed talk with engineers, not on writing code."},"8":{"0":"Five Forces analyzes a whole industry, not a target company. To assess a company or an acquisition, the stage points to SWOT.","2":"The forces are not internal capabilities: they are external, like new entrants, suppliers, customers and substitutes.","3":"A funnel model is something else, like AARRR. Porter's forces measure how attractive a market is."},"9":{"0":"These are the book's general question categories, not behavioral ones. A behavioral question is about your own experience.","2":"These are the qualities the categories test, not the categories: conflict tests collaboration, failure tests humility.","3":"A success or a promotion is not on the list. The categories probe hard moments, like conflict, failure and time pressure."},"10":{"0":"Awareness and Reach are not in AARRR. The framework is Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue.","2":"Activity and Reach are not in it, and the order breaks the lifecycle that starts with Acquisition.","3":"Attention and Reliability are not in AARRR. It is a user-lifecycle framework, not a product health check."},"11":{"0":"That measures the feature's own success, a primary or secondary metric. A guardrail watches what could break elsewhere.","2":"NPS of feature users measures the feature itself. The guardrail here protects performance: load time and CPU.","3":"Bandwidth and server cost are not the proposed guardrail. The example names meeting load time and CPU usage."},"12":{"0":"A direct yes shows the depth was missed: there is no single right answer, so the recommendation should be conditional.","2":"On one hand, on the other, with no recommendation is a dodge. Nuance is welcome; dodging is not.","3":"Use one framework and name it, not three. Piling up frameworks does not replace a conditional recommendation."},"13":{"0":"A real, painful failure is the right choice, as long as you show what you learned and fixed afterward.","2":"A failure with a lesson is exactly what works: Michal's story about skipping the beta is built that way.","3":"The failure's age is not the problem the book names. The top mistake is picking a trivial failure out of fear of the question."},"14":{"0":"Rolling back is a conclusion before diagnosis. First slice the drop and separate internal causes from external ones.","1":"A win-back campaign before the cause is known treats a symptom. Diagnosis comes first: segments, then causes.","3":"Seasonality is one external cause to check, not a reason to wait. The book asks you to diagnose before concluding."}},"123":{"0":{"0":"Lin and Lu wrote The Product Manager Interview. The stage contrasts this book with Lin's framework books.","2":"Anon and González de Villaumbrosia wrote The Product Book, a different title on the shelf.","3":"These are not the authors. The stage credits a duo: a former Google engineer who wrote Cracking the Coding Interview, and a former PM."},"1":{"0":"Amazon has more than 10: there were 14 principles for years, and two more were added in July 2021.","1":"12 is too few: the list stood at 14 for years before two principles were added in 2021.","2":"14 was the count for years, and it is the list shown in the chapter. In July 2021 Amazon added two, making 16."},"2":{"1":"Build, Use, Sustain is not BUS. Bavaro's three steps are a business goal, the users and solutions.","2":"Brainstorming first jumps to ideas. BUS starts with the business goal, moves to users, and only then to solutions.","3":"BUS is not about buying and selling: it is a three-step frame for answering product design questions."},"3":{"0":"3 stages is far too few: McDowell's funnel runs from resume screen to offer in 8 stages.","1":"5 stages misses several filters, such as the take-home assignment and the hiring committee.","3":"12 overstates it: the standard funnel has 8 stages, from resume screen to offer and negotiation."},"4":{"0":"Half would be generous: the resume screen is the tightest gate, and only about 10% pass a half-minute scan.","1":"30% is too high for this stage. Only about 10% pass, which is why measurable results on one page are critical.","3":"About 80% is the rough pass rate at the hiring committee, not the resume screen, where only about 10% pass."},"5":{"0":"Customer Obsession and Frugality belong to Amazon's principles, not to Meta's profile.","2":"Long-term vision and enterprise worlds describe Microsoft in the book's map of companies.","3":"Structured, data-driven thinking and a high technical bar describe Google, not Meta."},"6":{"0":"It is reversed: CIRCLES has 7 steps and BUS has 3. And the book sees both as valid, not one as better.","2":"Both are for product design questions. Behavioral questions get their own method, such as DIGS stories.","3":"Neither is tied to a company: both are general frameworks for product design questions, and both are valid."},"7":{"0":"Memorizing definitions is not enough: Amazon expects you to prove the principles through personal stories.","2":"Interviewers probe at least 4-5 principles, but you can't know which, so Shira prepared a story for every one.","3":"Generic product design drills miss Amazon's values-based interview, which is built around behavioral stories."},"8":{"0":"That describes Google's hiring committee, which reads written feedback. The Bar Raiser is an interviewer with a veto.","2":"The Bar Raiser comes from outside the team, not the hiring manager, and the job is to raise the bar, not fill a seat.","3":"The bar is the hiring standard, not salary. The Bar Raiser is an interviewer from outside the team who holds a veto."},"9":{"0":"Duties are exactly what the book says to replace: achievements, not duties. A company name shows no result.","1":"Claiming a methodology with no story is a known mistake: theory without an example. Numbers show results.","3":"Unit names and acronyms mean little outside the military: translate them into people, budget and a result anyone understands."},"10":{"0":"Technical depth is Google's emphasis in the company map. Apple looks for passion for product, quality and discretion.","2":"Openness and public betas are the opposite of Apple's discretion and uncompromising quality.","3":"Metrics and growth are not what sets Apple apart: the book stresses passion for product and attention to detail."},"11":{"0":"30 hours is exactly what the book warns against: cap it at 8-10 hours. They judge the thinking, not a perfect deck.","2":"Speed is not what is tested: the cap is 8-10 hours, and the company looks at the quality of the thought process.","3":"Past 10 hours output declines. The book sets 8-10 hours as the maximum, not the minimum."},"12":{"0":"Picking the interviewing company's own product looks like flattery. A well-known product from elsewhere is better.","2":"A product nobody knows is hard for the interviewer to follow. The book recommends a well-known product, analyzed.","3":"The product type is not the point: any well-known product works if you analyze the target user, pain and solution."},"13":{"0":"A recap of strengths is not the close the book recommends: a candidate who asks no questions signals no interest.","2":"Salary is handled at the offer stage, when the recruiter becomes your agent. Closing questions should show real thinking.","3":"The decision comes later, from a committee or a debrief. The close is for smart questions about team, product and culture."},"14":{"0":"Frameworks matter, but the top mistake is generic preparation: each company measures against a different yardstick.","2":"Coding is not the issue in the book: its first reason for rejection is zero research on the company.","3":"Asking questions is encouraged: a candidate who asks none signals no interest. The top mistake is generic preparation."}},"124":{"0":{"0":"Fisher, with Ury, stands behind the classic Win-Win approach of Getting to Yes, which Voss argues against in this book.","2":"Kahneman is the source of the System 1 / System 2 idea Voss leans on; the book cites him, he did not write it.","3":"The book was written by a former FBI kidnapping negotiator together with Tahl Raz; Cialdini is not its author."},"1":{"0":"Voss says outright that empathy is not agreement: you show you understood without conceding your position.","2":"That is sympathy, which Voss rules out: the aim is to understand their feelings and show it, not to feel them yourself.","3":"Tactical Empathy is about the other side's emotions, not yours; they open up because they feel understood, not in return."},"2":{"0":"A Mirror is only the last 1-3 words in a questioning tone; a full summary is a different tool, aimed at That's right.","2":"Isopraxism is the instinct behind it, but the Mirror works gently through words; openly copying body and pace is not it.","3":"Naming the emotion with It sounds like is Labeling, a separate tool; a Mirror hands their own last words back."},"3":{"0":"Voss says Yes is often said out of politeness or to make you go away, with no real commitment behind it.","2":"Voss warns that You're right is the polite way of saying stop talking, not a sign of real agreement.","3":"Voss values No as a starting point that makes the other side feel safe, but the goal he sets is That's right."},"4":{"0":"Voss avoids Why: it sounds like an accusation and puts the other side on the defensive.","2":"A yes/no request that chases Yes; Voss replaces such a request with Would it be ridiculous to ask for your help? to invite a No.","3":"This just asks for their demand again; the magic question hands your constraint back so they explain how or ease off."},"5":{"0":"How is one of the two words Voss builds Calibrated questions on: open-ended and not accusatory.","1":"What is the other base word of Calibrated questions, as in What about this is important to you?","3":"When accuses no one; its limit is that it draws out only a quick fact, not a detailed answer."},"6":{"0":"The audit comes first, before they speak; refuting their claims afterward is the very argument it is meant to prevent.","2":"In the audit you say the harsh things yourself, in your own voice; asking them to list objections just gives them a stage.","3":"The audit lists what they might say about you, not their weak spots; it defuses objections rather than building leverage."},"7":{"1":"The shares are swapped: words are the smallest channel at 7%, and body language is the largest at 55%.","2":"The 7 is not seconds: the rule splits a message into three channels, words 7%, tone 38%, body language 55%.","3":"Voss does not debunk the rule, he cites it; the caveat is that it covers feelings when words and tone conflict."},"8":{"0":"A playful, positive tone is Voss's default for most conversations, not the DJ voice kept for tense moments.","2":"The assertive tone is the one Voss saves for rare red lines, and it risks escalation; the DJ voice calms things down.","3":"The DJ voice is slow and low with a falling end; fast, excited energy is the opposite of the calm it spreads."},"9":{"0":"Ackerman opens higher, at 65% of the target; 50% is not one of the steps in the model.","2":"85% is the second step of the model; you only climb to it after opening at 65% of the target.","3":"100% is the final offer, given as a non-round number with a small non-monetary extra."},"10":{"0":"That is Taleb's original meaning; Voss applies the term to hidden information you can uncover by listening and asking.","2":"Holding back a concession is a bargaining move; a Black Swan is information about the other side you don't know yet.","3":"An opening offer is something you put on the table; a Black Swan is something hidden that you have to discover."},"11":{"0":"Soothing is the instinct Voss rejects: there's no reason to be angry dismisses the emotion instead of naming it.","2":"This is a Why question, which Voss says sounds accusatory; a Label describes the emotion and then goes silent.","3":"A Label only describes what is in the air, without agreeing, just as Tactical Empathy is understanding, not agreement."},"12":{"0":"The block was not team load: the old Site Generator's codebase was the problem, and a separate project solved it.","2":"Nothing here points to padded estimates: what surfaced was technical, Legacy Code in the old Site Generator.","3":"No beta or delay was involved: the discovery cut the timeline itself, from 6 months down to 2.5."},"13":{"0":"Voss sees No as the beginning, not the end: whoever says it feels protected and is now free to listen.","2":"Voss does not chase Yes; he even asks questions that invite a No, because Yes is often just politeness.","3":"A quick concession is splitting the difference, which Voss warns against; after a No you keep talking, not giving."},"14":{"0":"Win-Win, logic and meeting in the middle are what Voss argues against, and he says round numbers sound like guesses.","2":"Voss saves the assertive tone for rare red lines because it risks escalation; threats and ultimatums are not his tools.","3":"Taking the first offer skips the whole process; in the salary scenario Shira climbs from 35K to 43,892 with Voss's tools."}},"125":{"0":{"0":"Voss wrote Never Split the Difference, a separate book in the course; this salary book is by career coach Jack Chapman.","2":"Fisher is linked to Getting to Yes and the Win-Win approach; this salary negotiation field kit comes from Jack Chapman.","3":"Lewis Lin is not tied to this book: it was written by Jack Chapman, a career consultant and coach with 40+ years of experience."},"1":{"0":"The title is about your own gain: the raise divided by the minutes you spent negotiating, not a coach's fee.","2":"That is true and in the book, but it is the compounding argument for negotiating, not the math behind the title.","3":"Chapman says the opposite: never close on the spot, and ask for 24-48 hours to think even when the offer is good."},"2":{"0":"A personal range is still a number that limits her, and any early number becomes a ceiling; her leverage grows later.","2":"Any number before an offer becomes her ceiling; current salary plus 10% locks her low before they even want her.","3":"Even at the first interview Chapman says to defer and understand the role first; money is discussed only after an offer."},"3":{"0":"The rule is about the salary number itself, the anchor; it does not ask you to hold back on every other topic.","2":"Chapman holds the opposite: whoever names the first number loses, because your own number becomes your ceiling.","3":"The rule is about who names the first number, not who concedes first; their number is the base you climb from."},"4":{"0":"Chapman stresses it is no theatrical performance: a felt pause of surprise, not anger and getting up from the chair.","2":"The counter-offer comes later, ideally after 24-48 hours; the Flinch is the surprised reaction that comes before it.","3":"A poker face hides your reaction, while the Flinch shows it on purpose to signal the number has not closed the story."},"5":{"0":"31K is below the Walking Number she set in advance, the floor she won't sign under; being near her target doesn't matter.","2":"The Walking Number is the floor, not the maximum; the most you can ask for is the Dream Number.","3":"Signing below her floor breaks the rule; a raise needs a case built over time, and the book puts internal raises at 3-7%."},"6":{"0":"Asking for 50-100% more would sound out of touch with reality, the exact risk Chapman warns about with high asks.","1":"20-30% overshoots: Chapman warns that asking too high sounds out of touch; on 35K, 10-15% is about 38.5-40K.","3":"2-5% leaves most of the held-back margin on the table; the first offer is never final, so there is room to ask more."},"7":{"0":"Chapman says never close on the spot, even on an excellent offer; the pause is what sets up the counter-offer.","2":"Signing the moment it arrives skips both the thinking pause and the counter-offer that Chapman says always has room.","3":"Chapman says to use an outside offer only if you are truly ready to leave, never as a bluff; a made-up offer is a bluff."},"8":{"0":"Chapman marks needs like a mortgage or a family as the bad approach: the other side wants to know what it gets.","2":"Years and loyalty say nothing about results; Chapman's case is built on evidence of value: results, KPIs and market data.","3":"Chapman calls an outside offer a dangerous tactic that can crack trust; use it only if you are truly ready to leave."},"9":{"0":"The numbers are reversed: per Chapman, an internal raise brings 3-7%, and changing jobs brings 10-30%.","2":"Chapman says to treat a raise like a project: build a case and ask in a dedicated meeting, not wait to be noticed.","3":"Raises at your current place, review included, are the 3-7% path in the book; the big jump comes from changing jobs."},"10":{"0":"Your current salary is still a personal number, and any number you name becomes your anchor and your ceiling.","2":"A precise number is still a personal number; even under pressure, Chapman's answer names only a market range.","3":"The Walking Number is your floor; saying it out loud hands them the lowest figure you would accept."},"11":{"0":"An annual bonus is a percentage of the base, so a locked base keeps it stuck too; Chapman rates it medium to get.","2":"Chapman rates faster Vesting as hard to get, while a one-time sign-on bonus is the easiest win on his list.","3":"Chapman rates RSUs hard but possible, and they release only over years; a one-time payment at entry is far easier to approve."},"12":{"0":"With a Cliff nothing vests month by month from day one: until month 12, not a single unit releases.","2":"The yearly 25% comes only for a completed year; leaving at month 10 is before the Cliff, so nothing vests.","3":"The Cliff has nothing to do with the share price: it is the opening period of Vesting, when nothing releases."},"13":{"0":"~30K is only the one-time sign-on bonus; base, bonus and RSU added more, about 120K in total.","2":"~5K is below even a single component of the deal; the base alone rose by about 60K a year.","3":"Daniel's multi-dimensional negotiation added base, sign-on, bonus, RSU and 5 vacation days in 30 minutes of talk."},"14":{"0":"Chapman says a No is not final; accepting it quietly gives up the chance to turn it into when, and on what terms.","2":"Chapman suggests asking your manager to recommend you to whoever decides, not going over their head.","3":"Chapman calls the exit threat dangerous: managers may read it as disloyalty and quietly start looking for a replacement."}},"126":{"0":{"0":"That trade-off is the very belief the book tests and finds backwards: the fastest teams also failed less and recovered sooner.","2":"The book finds a link, not independence: in the strong groups both speed and stability were better.","3":"The book says slow is not safe: stability is built from small batches and frequent deploys, not long freezes."},"1":{"0":"Dropping tests could happen as a side effect, but the direct risk is that the number rises while reality does not.","1":"Hiring SREs is not what this target sets off; a metric turned into a goal invites gaming the number, and that is the risk.","2":"Nothing says the metric is already high in every team; the real danger is the opposite, a number pumped up artificially."},"2":{"1":"More manual testing keeps the same big batch and delays it further; a small slice is easier to test and to undo.","2":"A night release keeps the same big batch and adds a tired team; it does not shrink the risk of the change itself.","3":"Per the research, an outside approval board slows delivery and was not found to reduce failures."},"3":{"0":"The research found otherwise: outside approval meant slower delivery and was not found to cut failures; more members add delay.","1":"The research says nothing about meeting length; what it links to slower delivery is outside approval itself.","3":"A team that deploys daily is hit hardest by a weekly gate; the book favors peer review, automated tests and monitoring."},"4":{"0":"Information moving only through official channels is Westrum's bureaucratic, rule-oriented culture.","1":"Hoarded information and punished messengers describe the pathological, power-oriented culture.","3":"Westrum's question is how information flows; a generative culture gets it to whoever needs it, it does not stop sharing."},"5":{"1":"A private talk still treats it as one person's fault; the book asks what in the process and tools allowed the incident.","2":"A manager sign-off is outside approval, which the research linked to slower delivery and did not find to cut failures.","3":"Taking deployments away punishes the team; a blame response teaches people to hide problems."},"6":{"0":"Comparing with other teams says nothing about product success, and it invites gaming the numbers.","1":"Waiting changes nothing: delivery metrics measure how fast and safely you reach customers, not whether they got value.","2":"An availability report is one more view of stability; it still does not tell whether customers actually use the Timeline."},"7":{"0":"A two-day lead time, from commit to production, means deployment is already fast; the weeks are not going there.","2":"Recovery comes after a release; it cannot explain two months of waiting for a feature that has not shipped yet.","3":"Automated tests sit inside those two days, so they are already fast; the missing weeks come before them."},"8":{"1":"Collection cost is not the book's objection; the problem is that these numbers count activity, not results.","2":"The book does not reject them by team size; it rejects them everywhere because they reward doing more, not achieving more.","3":"Volatility is not the issue; once people are rewarded on them they get inflated, like velocity turned into a target."},"9":{"0":"Culture matters, but the block here is structural: code and environment dependencies force a shared deploy window.","1":"Monitoring helps catch incidents fast, but the problem here comes before deploying: the team cannot deploy alone.","3":"A WIP limit shortens queues inside the team; it does not remove the need to coordinate a window with two other teams."},"10":{"0":"Sticking to the spec only because it was planned wastes a sprint on something already known not to work.","1":"The capability is freedom to change the spec without outside approval; asking leadership for each change is that approval.","2":"Halting the whole sprint for a full study is an overreaction; the team can update the spec and test it right away."},"11":{"0":"A stress workshop treats the symptom; the book traces the pain to how deploys are done, and fixes it there.","2":"The book shows deploy pain shrinks with small, automated deploys in working hours; it is not a fixed cost of B2B.","3":"Nothing points to team size; the book ties sleepless releases to deployment pain, eased by small automated deploys."},"12":{"0":"The book rests on surveys, not controlled experiments; it argues for prediction, and critics note the arrow may reverse.","1":"The link to business performance is the heart of the book: profitability, market share and productivity, as reported.","3":"The stage mentions no limit to public companies or headcount; and either way the data shows links, not proof."},"13":{"1":"Ranking teams turns the metrics into goals and invites gaming; the stage says outright not to rank teams.","2":"Tying them to a bonus rewards people on the numbers, and the book warns that rewarded numbers get inflated.","3":"Different teams, like one that relies on outside partners, are not meant to look alike; a uniform target becomes a number to game."},"14":{"0":"A layer split looks tidy, but no layer brings value or feedback until all are ready, so the real batch stays big.","2":"All at once is exactly the big batch the book argues against; an extra week of testing only delays it more.","3":"Splitting by developer gives everyone a piece, but no piece reaches a customer on its own and brings back feedback."}},"127":{"0":{"0":"Meetings led and documents written are activity; Grove counts them only if they move what comes out of the teams.","1":"A fast chat reply is activity, not output; Grove asks what actually came out of the teams she runs and influences.","3":"A personal task list ignores the formula: a manager's output is her team's plus that of the neighboring teams she influences."},"1":{"1":"Twenty chat replies feel productive, but each serves one person for one moment, which is low leverage.","2":"Polishing slide design changes nobody's work; high leverage touches many people or lasts for months.","3":"Just listening in gathers information but moves nothing, while a decision four teams are waiting for stays open."},"2":{"0":"Caution sounds responsible, but a whole team pays for it; a decision stuck with one person is negative leverage.","1":"The bottleneck is her pending decision, not the team; shifting the blame to them leaves the negative leverage in place.","2":"Two or three more weeks would stall the team even longer; Grove suggests deciding with what's known and setting a review date."},"3":{"0":"Deals stall in the security review, not in development; speeding up another step only lengthens the queue in front of it.","2":"More deals in the pipeline just pile up in front of the same slow review; the limiting step stays the same.","3":"The review protects customer trust and can't be dropped; Grove builds the plan around the limiting step, not past it."},"4":{"1":"After development the same misunderstanding costs a sprint, not a conversation, because the product is already worth more.","2":"A pilot shows real use, but it comes after the work is done, when a requirements mistake is already expensive.","3":"After launch the mistake costs development time and customer trust too, the most expensive stage to catch it."},"5":{"0":"Catalog size is not affected by the push, so it cannot reveal what forcing more connections cost.","1":"Time on the settings screen is not the expected side effect; broken connections show up as sync tickets to support.","3":"Sign-ups per channel measure the top of the funnel and say nothing about the cost of pushing connections."},"6":{"0":"Status can move into writing, but cancelling also wipes out the time meant for Yaara's problems and dilemmas.","1":"A ten-minute schedule check is still a status meeting; Grove frees the 1:1 for the employee's problems and dilemmas.","2":"If Michal sets the agenda it becomes her meeting; for Grove the 1:1 belongs to the employee, who brings the topics."},"7":{"0":"A mission meeting invites only the people needed; inviting everyone affected makes it costlier and harder to decide in.","2":"Grove says to agree beforehand what is decided, by when and who must hear; open talk with no prep guarantees no decision.","3":"A fixed rhythm is what defines a process meeting; a mission meeting is called once, for one specific output."},"8":{"1":"A dashboard link alone creates no discussion; the review works when teams explain deviations and ask for decisions.","2":"A wins-only review hides exactly what needs fixing; the learning is in the deviations.","3":"Personal reports on what each person did are activity, not results; the review compares each team's numbers to plan."},"9":{"0":"The open discussion is over; re-arguing in every forum erodes the decision before it is even tested.","1":"Backing it in public while promising customers otherwise is support for show only, and it undermines the decision.","3":"Waiting for it to fail is not support; Grove expects commitment to execution, even from those who disagreed."},"10":{"0":"For Grove, a polished document about the future that changes no action this week is not a plan.","2":"A signed forecast says what may happen; Grove's plan is the action that closes the gap, and it starts now.","3":"A list of initiatives is only raw material; planning decides which actions start today to close the gap."},"11":{"0":"Maturity is set task by task: seasoned in interviews, new to the metric; one level for both gets one of them wrong.","1":"Close direction on interviews she has mastered wastes leverage and teaches her to wait; maturity is per task, not tenure.","2":"Full freedom on a brand-new task is abandonment, not delegation; low maturity calls for structure."},"12":{"1":"Escalating skips Grove's first question, can't or won't, and that answer is what picks the right tool.","2":"Taking the deck saves the next review but leaves Yaara without the skill and Michal with more work.","3":"A warning is pressure, and pressure does not fix a capability gap; the stage notes Yaara is doing much of this for the first time."},"13":{"0":"Training is not a way out of daily work; its leverage comes from a few hours lifting several people for months.","1":"Training does not replace 1:1s, which serve a different purpose; that is not where its leverage comes from.","3":"What leadership sees is a side effect, not the reason; the leverage is the output of several people over months."},"14":{"0":"First-come leaves the conflict on Yaara's shoulders; the hybrid structure works only when the split of decisions is written.","2":"Moving her fully to Ido removes her functional home, which owns the craft, the standards and the evaluation.","3":"In the hybrid the team sets the daily order of work; letting one side win automatically defeats the structure."}},"128":{"0":{"0":"Sorting teammates into quadrants is exactly the labeling Scott warns against: it freezes people and excuses you.","1":"The quadrants describe any conversation, and Scott aims for a team where peers give each other feedback directly.","3":"Treating a quadrant as a fixed style is the opposite of the point: it describes a moment, which can change in the next talk."},"1":{"1":"Ruinous Empathy comes from caring, not indifference; a manager who doesn't care lands in the bottom quadrants.","2":"The stage blames no policy; the cause is human: silence feels good now and its price arrives later.","3":"The book traces it to the manager's own fear of an awkward moment, not to employees asking to be spared."},"2":{"0":"The team may pay too, but the main cost falls on Yaara: nobody tells her, so she cannot improve.","2":"Being too strict is the opposite problem; here nothing was said at all, which is Ruinous Empathy.","3":"Nothing says Michal knows the area less; the problem is not document quality but that Yaara is never told."},"3":{"0":"Accepting all good lets the polite answer stand; a general question invites exactly that kind of reply.","1":"A long survey skips the direct conversation that builds trust; the book suggests one specific question, then waiting.","2":"A go-between skips the direct talk; whoever carries messages between people becomes a bottleneck of trust."},"4":{"1":"A defense on the spot teaches Ronen not to bother next time; the book says to use follow-up questions, not explanations.","2":"A thank-you with no follow-up tells him his words changed nothing, so he has less reason to speak up again.","3":"A long written rebuttal is still a defense; rewarding candor means a thank-you and a change he can see."},"5":{"0":"The best designer we have is a general verdict about the person; it doesn't tell Noa what to do more of.","1":"Great job teaches nothing; good praise names what worked and why, and this one jumps straight to the next screen.","3":"Praise that slides into a though sounds like a wrapper for criticism, and nice screen names nothing specific."},"6":{"0":"Feedback is judged by how it landed, not by intent; a good intention doesn't fix what Elad heard.","2":"Taking the feedback back is Ruinous Empathy: you change the delivery, not the truth.","3":"Sending it through his manager adds a middleman to a conversation that should be direct."},"7":{"0":"Passing it on makes Michal a go-between, and the message loses detail on the way.","1":"Raising it in a public review turns a fixable work issue into criticism in front of everyone.","2":"Silence is not neutral here: the problem goes on, and Scott's first step is asking Uri whether he told Noa himself."},"8":{"0":"Pushing him into management as a reward can cost the team a great PM and give it a reluctant manager.","1":"Steady growth is a trajectory, not a flaw; calling him not ambitious ignores what he wants right now.","3":"A rock star wants to go deeper where he is; moving him to a new area every quarter blocks exactly that."},"9":{"1":"The problem is not who assigns the label but that it is fixed; a trajectory shifts with life, so you need to ask again.","2":"Rock stars don't have to become superstars; both trajectories are legitimate, and a team needs both.","3":"Nothing limits the terms to engineers; the warning is that any fixed label stops you from asking again."},"10":{"0":"Reopening a debate that was decided properly undermines the decision; the cycle moves on to persuade.","1":"Asking Danny to approve it again undermines a decision made properly, and explains nothing to those not in the room.","2":"Jumping straight to building skips persuade, a common reason decisions get reopened later."},"11":{"0":"Dugri is direct but doesn't always show care, and in the US it often sounds like Obnoxious Aggression.","2":"Dropping the criticism would be Ruinous Empathy; the truth stays, and only the delivery changes.","3":"A bigger audience only makes it worse; criticism belongs in private, not in another public channel."},"12":{"1":"Going over his head at leadership turns a work problem into an attack; without authority, trust is her only tool.","2":"Silence and a quiet squeeze are Ruinous Empathy toward Guy, and costly for the team's plan.","3":"A list of past mistakes is about pattern and character, not this case; it turns one work issue into an attack."},"13":{"0":"Radical Candor needs care and challenge together; a mocking tone drops the care half of the map.","1":"Scott wants peers to give each other feedback directly; the problem is the mockery, not his role.","3":"Writing is not required, and the book even warns that tone disappears in writing; the problem is the missing care."},"14":{"0":"All great from a direct report usually reflects the power gap, not a lack of things to say; stopping just loses it.","2":"Checking with Ido behind Yaara's back breaks the very trust Michal needs for Yaara to speak up.","3":"Asking only Sharon misses what only a direct report can see about Michal as a manager."}},"129":{"0":{"1":"Story points for a full feature list start from a fixed solution and ask how long, the very habit appetite replaces.","2":"Adding developers to hit a date still fixes the solution; appetite keeps the time fixed and makes the scope flexible.","3":"Last year's feature plus a buffer is an estimate, a guess that tends to stretch; appetite asks what the problem is worth."},"1":{"0":"Neither one is shaped: one is too detailed and boxes the team in, the other too vague to set any boundary.","1":"Pixel-level mockups box the team in, and adding acceptance criteria doesn't fix the level of detail of either one.","3":"A one-line ticket sets no boundaries, and a usability test won't fix mockups that leave the team no room."},"2":{"0":"A polished prototype pulls the talk into visual detail too early, the opposite of agreeing on the flow first.","2":"A Gantt chart breaks down tasks for work that hasn't been shaped yet; it says nothing about screens and actions.","3":"A list of user stories breaks down work before it is shaped, and it doesn't show what connects to what."},"3":{"0":"Leaving it to the team means betting on an unknown that can swallow weeks in the middle of the cycle.","1":"Adding time contradicts the appetite; the shapers defuse the hole, they don't stretch the project around it.","2":"Shape Up has no central backlog to park it in, and the open question would still sit inside the pitch."},"4":{"0":"Technical risks are handled as rabbit holes and defused in shaping; no-gos are things deliberately not built.","2":"No-gos are about this solution's boundaries, not a register of rejected customer requests.","3":"Parts that can be cut are marked with a tilde during the build; that is scope hammering, not no-gos."},"5":{"1":"The book never says requests don't matter; an important idea comes back as a pitch, and without a log, field signals get lost.","2":"The betting table picks among a few shaped pitches each cool-down; it does not keep a central backlog.","3":"The team owns how to build, but what to build is chosen at the betting table, not by the team alone."},"6":{"0":"We've already invested six weeks is the sunk-cost argument the circuit breaker exists to stop.","1":"Handing it to another team is an extension in disguise; the default is to stop and ask what went wrong in shaping.","3":"Stretching everyone's cycle breaks fixed time for every team; an unfinished project simply gets no extension."},"7":{"0":"A stuck dot is a request for help, not an indictment; a daily report won't answer a question that has no answer yet.","1":"Each scope moves on its own; uphill means it is not yet clear how to solve it, far from nearly done.","2":"The other scopes are going downhill, so volume is not the issue; a hill chart measures certainty, and here it is missing."},"8":{"1":"This is not ship now, fix later: what goes out must work well, just without edge cases that don't change the picture.","2":"Michal's argument is the comparison to today's spreadsheet, not the needs of one big customer.","3":"Michal herself is making a quality call here, against what customers have today; she is not staying out of it."},"9":{"0":"Overtime breaks the appetite; scope hammering cuts the nice-to-haves so the cycle doesn't stretch.","2":"Splitting into smaller tasks and estimating each one does not shrink the scope at all.","3":"Delaying the launch breaks fixed time; scope is the variable, so the question is whether drag-and-drop is essential."},"10":{"0":"A list prepared in advance is a guess, and estimating 40 tickets turns the team into ticket-takers.","1":"Ticketing only part of it still hands the team pre-split tasks; the book gives it the whole project.","3":"The betting table bets on shaped pitches, not ticket lists; and most real tasks only appear once work starts."},"11":{"0":"Building in separate layers pushes the discovery of what doesn't work to the end of the cycle.","1":"Nothing here shows shaping failed; the issue is build order, and one slice that works end to end fixes it.","2":"Test cases don't help when nothing is connected yet; what is missing is a working slice, not testing."},"12":{"0":"Bundling the small ones with the big one makes one blurry project; the book separates a small batch from a big batch.","2":"If time is left is exactly the thinking appetite replaces; each problem gets its time up front.","3":"Cool-down has no binding plan: it is for bugs, cleanup and exploring ideas, not for planned projects."},"13":{"1":"When there is a date, fixed time is actually an advantage; Shape Up suits it better than it seems.","2":"Promising every feature is a roadmap written by deals; scope is the variable, so you promise the problem.","3":"There is no need to wait: set the appetite by the demo date and promise the problem it will solve."},"14":{"0":"A small, senior team working on its own product is exactly the setting the method was built for.","1":"A team that holds every decision is exactly the autonomy the method assumes, so it fits well here.","3":"A clear problem that fits in six weeks matches one cycle exactly; that is the method's home ground."}},"130":{"0":{"0":"Price is a symptom: in an AI assistant frame, even a lower price gets measured against chatbots.","1":"Better product knowledge won't help while the frame itself sends buyers to the chatbot checklist.","3":"Nothing says the model is weaker; the frame turns the model into the question, and that is not where Velox stands out."},"1":{"1":"The tagline comes from positioning later; positioning is the decision about context that comes before any words.","2":"Branding comes after positioning; a logo and colors don't set what customers compare the product with.","3":"A feature list without context doesn't tell customers why those features matter; the book starts from alternatives."},"2":{"0":"Head-to-head is a deliberate choice to fight on existing criteria; here nobody chose, the old frame just stayed.","2":"A sub-segment is chosen on purpose; this frame was never re-chosen, even after the product and customers changed.","3":"No trend is involved here; the issue is the 2021 frame that stayed while Enterprise and Autopilot arrived."},"3":{"0":"A roadmap list is an inside view; uniqueness only means something against what customers would do without you.","1":"The category is the last link in the chain; it comes out of alternatives, attributes and value, not before them.","2":"A tagline is messaging, which is derived from positioning; starting there skips the whole chain."},"4":{"1":"A feature-by-feature fight with Asana answers a question these customers are not even asking.","2":"A spreadsheet is a real alternative even when it is free; the book counts what customers would actually do.","3":"Import is a feature idea, not an answer about uniqueness; the edge is defined against the spreadsheet and the call."},"5":{"0":"A logo without usage proves no value; the book builds around the customers who get the most out of the product.","1":"Many leads are volume, not a sign that the value burns there; heavy use and strong retention are.","3":"An average blurs exactly what makes the product different; the target is whoever cares most about the value."},"6":{"0":"Head-to-head means playing by the rules of whoever defined the category, and those criteria don't favor Velox.","2":"A new category takes years of market education and budget, a hard bet at about $7M ARR when a sub-segment works.","3":"Velox loses to Linear on developer experience; the stage says to leave Linear its turf, not to become a dev tool."},"7":{"0":"A free domain is a secondary detail that only matters once creating a category makes sense at all.","1":"Analysts' taste is secondary; first ask whether an existing frame already lets the value show.","2":"Fear of copycats is a later question; the first one is whether a new category is justified at all."},"8":{"1":"Deal size matters to the decision, but it doesn't answer what the module does to the positioning.","2":"Build cost matters too, but it doesn't say whether a heavy Gantt pulls Velox onto the turf of PMO tools.","3":"Copying what Monday and Asana have moves Velox closer to an alternative instead of sharpening its edge."},"9":{"0":"That reverses the order: a tagline that sets the positioning leaves every channel telling a different story.","1":"When a deck invents its own positioning, every rep tells a different story; the deck comes from the messaging.","3":"An A/B test can choose between phrasings, but it can't replace the decision about context."},"10":{"0":"An empty chat box teaches customers to frame Velox as a chatbot, the very frame the positioning moved away from.","2":"A pop-up can't fix an experience that tells a different story; the screens themselves must lead to the value.","3":"Positioning is a whole-product decision, and the first screens are where customers meet it; it can't be handed off."},"11":{"0":"A literal translation ignores that US buyers compare Velox with different things, like Asana and Copilot.","1":"A completely separate positioning per market splits the product and the messaging; the core stays one.","2":"US buyers need proof that fits them, such as an American customer as a reference."},"12":{"1":"A trend that becomes the category stops differentiating, because everyone is hanging on it.","2":"A trend in every message is no longer seasoning; delete the word, and if nothing is left, it was doing all the work.","3":"A trend can still explain why now, as long as it ties to the value; the risk is leaning on it, not using it."},"13":{"0":"Re-examining every sprint is like never choosing; positioning that changes all the time is as good as none.","1":"A rebrand is about the look; positioning returns to the table when the product, the audience or the comparisons shift.","3":"Freezing it forever leads straight into the default-positioning trap that Velox fell into."},"14":{"0":"Waiting for a thousand customers leaves the product in default positioning; a hypothesis can be tested much earlier.","2":"Velox's positioning was built from Velox's best-fit customers; a new product has its own alternatives to discover.","3":"A session with management only is the consensus theater the critique warns about; it needs evidence from customers."}},"131":{"0":{"1":"Minivation is an offer that is too modest and too cheap; here it is overloaded and expensive, the opposite problem.","2":"A hidden gem never reaches the market; this module did launch, and then stalled with customers.","3":"Undead means the need doesn't exist at all; here a need exists but is buried under overload and price."},"1":{"0":"Too few capabilities can hurt, but that cannot explain feature shock, where there are too many.","1":"Optimistic estimates affect schedules, not pricing; the shared root is a price question asked too late.","3":"Weak marketing doesn't explain why a good product ends up underpriced, as in minivation."},"2":{"0":"A direct price question at the start invites a polite guess, not a fact; the range is asked only at the end.","2":"A finished pricing page turns the talk into a reaction to your idea instead of facts about their pain.","3":"Leaving price until after launch is exactly the mistake the book warns against; by then the product can't change."},"3":{"0":"More size bands are still an outside description; two same-size companies can buy for opposite reasons.","1":"Industry is also an outside description; the book segments by need and value first, then checks the profile.","2":"Dropping segmentation takes you back to one offer that fits nobody, with every segment paying for what it doesn't need."},"4":{"1":"A capability that puts buyers off can lower what they pay for the whole bundle; in the top tier it hurts that tier.","2":"An add-on fits a capability a few people value highly, not one that few want and some even reject.","3":"In every tier, a capability that puts buyers off drags down willingness to pay across all of them instead of adding value."},"5":{"0":"Plus buyers rank it low, so it makes their tier pricier for something they don't want: feature shock in miniature.","1":"In every tier it is given to many who don't need it, while the minority would pay for it: that is minivation.","3":"Waiting for every segment ignores the minority that already values it highly and would pay for it now."},"6":{"0":"Charging for all 1,200 users quadruples the bill for the same value and is likely to stall in procurement.","2":"A flat fee doesn't grow with the customer, even though the value grows with every person added to the plan.","3":"Charging only for AI scenarios ignores where most of the value is: planning the people themselves."},"7":{"0":"A small segment that values it highly, with distant rivals, calls for skimming: start high, then step down.","1":"The revenue-maximizing price from WTP data is the maximize strategy, the default, not penetration.","2":"Early buyers willing to pay more point to skimming; penetration deliberately gives up that margin."},"8":{"0":"Anchoring is legitimate only with real options; a tier no one can buy and hidden limits are deception.","2":"Giving up all framing isn't required: presenting real options clearly is part of good work.","3":"Hidden limits damage trust when the customer finds them on the invoice; if you wouldn't explain it to them, don't do it."},"9":{"1":"A price of zero in the first deals becomes the add-on's market price, and it is very hard to raise later.","2":"Price integrity doesn't mean never: a planned, time-limited exception for a commitment protects price and deal.","3":"Letting each salesperson decide alone is exactly what scatters prices, and the eroded price becomes the real one."},"10":{"0":"Unlimited scenarios expose gross margin, exactly as heavy Autopilot use did in the Board & Company Economics stage.","1":"Raising the price for everyone loads the cost of a few heavy users onto the many.","2":"Waiting for the board means finding the margin problem after it already hurt; ask about the heavy user up front."},"11":{"0":"Stated willingness to pay is a starting point, not a purchase; asked a hypothetical question, people tend to overstate.","1":"Sounds fair doesn't prove the price is low; it is a hypothesis to test, not a reason to change it at once.","3":"Throwing it away is also a mistake: the conversations narrow the range you then test in behavior."},"12":{"1":"Conjoint does not always overstate; that risk arises when competitors are missing from the simulation.","2":"The stage raises no leak risk; the critique is about order: prove someone needs it before polishing the price.","3":"Marketing budget is not the issue; sophisticated price research simply can't answer whether the product is needed."},"13":{"0":"An attach rate the team hopes for is a top-down guess, not customer data, and it hides which assumption is weak.","2":"Cost-plus ignores value: it says what it cost to build, not what each segment will pay.","3":"A competitor's price ignores your own segments and their willingness to pay, and assumes every customer buys."},"14":{"0":"Polite enthusiasm is not data, and it is common on US calls; a lead needs facts about spend and budget.","1":"A survey will only repeat the same opinion in numbers; facts about past spending are what help.","3":"An immediate discount gives away the price before there is a deal, based on enthusiasm that isn't yet a buying signal."}},"132":{"0":{"0":"Adding it to the backlog answers the request as stated, before anyone understands the problem behind it.","2":"An instant refusal skips the need too; often an existing report already solves what the customer is after.","3":"An estimate prices a solution nobody has checked yet; first ask what the customer does with the file."},"1":{"0":"Meeting length is not the reason; tricks fail because the other side can check almost any claim alone.","1":"It applies to long-standing customers and colleagues too; an exposed trick costs trust with anyone who can check.","2":"There is no formal ban here; polishing the truth simply no longer pays when the buyer can verify it."},"2":{"1":"Never is a permanent, personal explanation; it leaves nothing to fix, so coming back with a proposal gets hard.","2":"Blaming herself makes the no permanent and personal; a temporary, specific reason points to what can change.","3":"The stage notes that they don't get product is not one of the options; going around leadership burns trust for next time."},"3":{"0":"A capability list with no link to burn and runway sounds like a general request for money.","1":"A quote may help later, but it doesn't speak to what Inbal owns: burn, runway and return on investment.","3":"A competitor price comparison doesn't answer what matters to a CFO here: the cost and risk of the experiment."},"4":{"0":"Repeating her solution until he's convinced is yes, but; it closes the talk instead of building on his idea.","1":"Giving in completely looks generous, but it loses hold of the problem the solution needs to solve.","3":"Escalating to Sharon closes the conversation; yes, and keeps it open between Michal and Ronen."},"5":{"1":"Easy to remember doesn't make it true; when buyers can check, an unmeasured claim puts trust at risk.","2":"A small asterisk doesn't turn an unsupported claim into an honest one, and it can even be a legal problem.","3":"The PM is the one who brings the proof, so an unmeasured claim is exactly her area."},"6":{"0":"It comes mainly from one field study of call-center reps; calling it a law stretches it much too far.","1":"A hiring policy built on one narrow study stretches the finding far beyond what it shows.","2":"Advice to change your personality before meetings goes far beyond one study of call-center reps."},"7":{"0":"Closing at any cost creates resistance; a good pitch opens the next conversation rather than shutting it.","2":"Presenting everything up front is the 20-slide deck that answered everything except the one question that mattered.","3":"A canned answer to every objection sounds like a speech, not a conversation about their situation."},"8":{"1":"A quick commitment looks like helping Guy, but it puts the roadmap and trust at risk come renewal.","2":"An instant refusal gives up the deal without checking; the customer may need something smaller that already exists.","3":"An estimate prices the request as stated; first find out what Corvane needs, which may already exist."},"9":{"0":"Redesigning everything is a big answer to a question nobody has asked yet; first find the exact moment and screen.","1":"A customer who left is information, not noise; the exact moment it got complicated is worth finding.","3":"A discount treats the symptom, not the cause; the product stays exactly as complicated as before."},"10":{"0":"Direct license sales belong mainly to Sales; a PM's everyday selling involves no purchase at all.","2":"Price negotiation with procurement is mainly Sales' work, not the PM's everyday persuading.","3":"Marketing materials belong to PMM; the PM brings the proof, but that is not most of the day's selling."},"11":{"0":"Danny has already approved it; approval is necessary, but it doesn't replace the belief of whoever builds it.","1":"Guy is not the one holding it back; the project slows if Uri, who builds it, isn't on board.","2":"A leadership decision without the team's belief becomes a project that moves slowly and quietly."},"12":{"1":"A misleading selection of accurate data still damages trust, and customers will probably find the comparison themselves.","2":"Customers can't ask for data they don't know exists; hiding what the decision needs is manipulation.","3":"The line here is about trust, not only law: legal sign-off doesn't make a misleading selection honest."},"13":{"0":"Agreeing confirms his permanent, personal explanation, the kind that makes people stop trying.","1":"Ignoring the loss teaches nothing; a debrief turns it into something temporary and specific that can be fixed.","2":"Another deck does not address what this specific deal lacked, and it leaves his I'm not good untouched."},"14":{"0":"More capability slides don't address leadership's concern, which is the size of the risk.","1":"A bigger ask raises the approver's risk; a small ask with a stopping condition moves people more.","3":"Going over leadership's heads damages exactly the trust needed for the next yes."}},"133":{"0":{"0":"Nothing points to a demo that ran long; the customer loved it, he just learned nothing new about his business.","1":"Price never came up; the customer loved the demo and still got no new reason to act now.","3":"In a complex deal the relationship is the price of entry, not a reason to buy; he enjoyed it and is still waiting."},"1":{"1":"A well-known outside source isn't enough if it points equally to every vendor; then it is free marketing for rivals.","2":"An insight meant only to frighten is already a misleading claim, and it crosses an ethical and legal line.","3":"Describing capabilities explains the product; an insight teaches the customer something new about their business."},"2":{"0":"Dramatic phrasing adds no proof; an insight that fails the CFO's or legal's scrutiny burns trust.","2":"A fast rollout looks efficient, but sending an unverified insight to every rep multiplies the risk.","3":"A round estimate swaps a provable number for a guess; the insight needs a source you can show."},"3":{"0":"One slide for everyone gives up tailoring: each role asks about something else, delays, cost or risk.","1":"The operations director and the head of IT also shape the decision; ignoring them leaves the deal exposed.","2":"Tailoring changes emphasis, not facts; the moment the audiences compare notes, credibility is gone."},"4":{"0":"Agreeing keeps the mood only until the promise breaks, and then CS is the one meeting the disappointment.","1":"Refusing without explanation abandons the conversation instead of leading it toward a way forward.","2":"A verbal promise is still a promise of a date that isn't on the approved roadmap; a PM doesn't make those."},"5":{"0":"Taking control serves the customer's decision; pressure serves the seller's quarter, and that is the whole difference.","2":"A hard deadline looks like control, but it serves the seller's quarter, not the customer.","3":"Control is not about airtime; Ryan led with a single question and let the customer think."},"6":{"1":"Ryan's relationships are already strong; a relationship is the price of entry, not an answer to why now.","2":"A discount gives a reason bought with money; it erodes the price and doesn't change how the problem is seen.","3":"A feature deck explains the product, not what waiting costs the customer, which is what why now needs."},"7":{"0":"Raw data sounds thorough, but without interpretation it won't make it into a sales call.","1":"A long demo of every screen explains the product, not the insight, its proof or its limits.","3":"One slogan repeated word for word gives up tailoring for each role in the buying group."},"8":{"0":"Agreement with no questions usually means nothing new was said, the opposite of a reframe.","2":"The test compares the customer's before and after, not how you sound next to competitors.","3":"Slide count is a matter of format; the test asks whether the customer now sees the problem differently."},"9":{"0":"Length is not the problem; a statistic with no source may mislead, however short it is.","1":"Campaign or call, the issue is the same: a claim without a provable source is a legal risk and a trust risk.","2":"Its place in the call is not the issue; the truth behind the number is, and without a source it can't be proven."},"10":{"0":"The approach was built for complex deals with several decision makers, not for a self-serve upgrade.","1":"In a self-serve upgrade, a clear pricing page and good onboarding are the conversation; no teaching call is needed.","3":"Not worth the effort misses the point: small customers matter, the tool just doesn't fit this kind of sale."},"11":{"1":"Blaming customers for misunderstanding ignores the source: the sales conversation painted a bigger product.","2":"Pushing it onto onboarding treats the symptom; every insight sold is a promise CS meets after signature.","3":"Fit is not the problem described; they expected more than Autopilot does because that is what they were sold."},"12":{"0":"The research covered salespeople in complex deals, not small startups; its limit is correlation, not the sample.","2":"The stage says nothing about phone-only data; the limit is that surveys and manager ratings show only correlation.","3":"The book is precisely about complex deals, not consumer goods; its weakness is showing correlation, not cause."},"13":{"1":"Closing deals is the AE's job, since the AE owns the customer and the forecast; the PM brings knowledge.","2":"Anything not on the approved roadmap doesn't enter the call as a promise; that is not why the PM is there.","3":"A commission would only distort the PM's judgment; the value is knowledge flowing in both directions."},"14":{"0":"One contact's enthusiasm isn't enough when seven people buy; they need a tailored insight to present alone.","1":"Jumping to leadership sounds like a shortcut, but it leaves the rest of the buying group outside.","2":"Waiting for consensus gives up the conversation; the contact needs tools to build that agreement from inside."}},"134":{"0":{"0":"A line on the home page helps, but without someone owning the story, the next launch will be missed too.","1":"The capability already exists; the gap is that nobody told the market, and a new one would fall into the same gap.","2":"The issue isn't who runs the demo; no salesperson was trained, and the launch was one newsletter paragraph."},"1":{"0":"That describes the usual Product-Market Fit; Market-Product Fit flips it and asks whether the market can buy now.","2":"Competitor pricing is a different question; this fit is about budget, habits and the buying process.","3":"Building on time is the team's readiness; the concept looks at the market's readiness to absorb the product."},"2":{"0":"A lower price doesn't shorten an approval process the bank set for itself; price was never the problem.","1":"Dropping a whole sector over a process gives up customers who loved the product; plan for the buying timeline instead.","3":"Extra security features may help later, but they won't shorten the approval process the bank set for itself."},"3":{"1":"A big launch now makes noise in front of customers whose budgets are already closed and who can't buy yet.","2":"A full, silent delay wastes two months of learning and proof from existing customers.","3":"The customers in question are US PMO teams; launching in Israel does nothing about their closed budgets."},"4":{"1":"Press and conference talks are not the ambassador's job; the ambassador brings the market's voice into decisions.","2":"Choosing the position is the strategist's hat; the ambassador feeds it insight from deals won and lost.","3":"Recruiting customers to tell the story is the evangelist's hat; the ambassador listens and carries it inside."},"5":{"0":"Fixing only the website treats this symptom; other touchpoints may carry the same kind of gap.","1":"When the site and the product tell different stories, customers believe the worse one, counting clicks or not.","3":"Asking Support to explain leaves the broken promise in place; the gap itself has to be closed."},"6":{"0":"A list of planned capabilities turns every plan into a promise that is hard to keep.","2":"Every technical detail still doesn't tell Ryan where the line is; what not to promise is the missing piece.","3":"Something in development may change or slip; promising it puts customer trust and the roadmap at risk."},"7":{"0":"A quote written in their name before they approve it can damage trust and even breach the contract.","1":"A post without agreed content skips both consent and Legal's approval of what may be published.","2":"Being a customer is not consent; a logo used without approval can damage trust and breach the contract."},"8":{"0":"The launch tier is the PMM's call; Michal supplies the product truth it is built on.","1":"Messaging belongs to the PMM, built on the PM's material about the problem and the evidence.","3":"Sales training sits on the PMM's side of the contract; the PM provides the facts behind it."},"9":{"1":"Marketing usually doesn't write the product screens; that is exactly why the first screen is a forgotten touchpoint.","2":"It isn't the only place; customers also build the picture from the site, the demo, the pricing page and emails.","3":"Design doesn't replace messaging; the first screen matters because it is one more touchpoint telling the story."},"10":{"0":"Waiting for a hire delays learning, and meanwhile the market fills in the story by itself.","1":"Handing everything to Sales leaves the story with people measured on closing deals, not on the product.","2":"Quality opens the door but doesn't explain where it leads; a strong product loses to one the market understands."},"11":{"0":"A similar capability already exists; rebuilding it is the most expensive way to solve a misunderstanding.","2":"Rep experience isn't the problem here; customers ask for what exists because nobody told them it's there.","3":"Nothing suggests a fit problem; they asked for something Velox already has, which points to the story."},"12":{"0":"Israeli holidays matter to the team, but they set when you are ready, not when US customers can buy.","1":"The sprint end is a readiness date, not a launch date; the market's calendar comes first.","3":"A big event date says nothing about whether customers' budgets and renewals let them buy at that time."},"13":{"0":"Teams start with what because that's what they built, then find they launched just when nobody could buy.","2":"Why is the second question; it comes after when customers are ready to buy at all.","3":"Starting with how skips both when and why; a launch starts from the customer's calendar."},"14":{"1":"The book assumes a dedicated PMM; with one PMM for several lines, it can't run exactly as written.","2":"The ideas work in a small company too; dropping them gives up the tools a small company needs most.","3":"Velox sells B2B, and this whole stage shows product marketing at work there, from the sales kit to the bank deal."}},"135":{"0":{"1":"This opens with Velox's pride, so the customer meets the company first instead of their own problem.","2":"Architecture puts the technology at center stage; the reader has to leave their own problem to follow it.","3":"Six months of team effort is Velox's story; the customer should be the hero, and this opening leaves them out."},"1":{"0":"Length isn't the issue, it is only two words; the trouble is that it never says what the customer gets.","1":"The product name isn't the problem; a headline must say what the customer gets, and this one doesn't.","3":"Naming rivals isn't required; the problem is a headline you could put on any product, since it says nothing specific."},"2":{"0":"A full capability list adds load exactly when the customer needs a single next step.","1":"Two equal buttons make the choice harder; in practice, two equal buttons are zero buttons.","2":"No projects yet is true but useless: it leaves the customer with no next step."},"3":{"0":"An unsourced threat is misleading and damages trust; showing a real cost is honest, inflating fear is not.","2":"Removing all risk leaves a story with no reason to act; the customer needs to see what is at stake.","3":"Moving the line doesn't fix the fact that it has no basis; it is still a misleading threat."},"4":{"0":"When the product is cast as the hero, the customer becomes an extra in someone else's story and loses interest.","2":"A guide isn't neutral: it shows empathy for the problem and authority to help, not just facts.","3":"The obstacle is the customer's problem, like a stuck task; the product is what helps them get past it."},"5":{"0":"Settings matter to someone already convinced; opening there loses the customer before the story starts.","1":"A customer list is proof that lands better once the problem is clear, not as the opening.","2":"How the model works matters to someone already convinced; it puts the technology at the center, not the customer."},"6":{"0":"CEO approval doesn't make it clear; the test is whether people can repeat it on calls.","1":"A sentence that holds every capability is no longer a one-liner, and nobody will be able to repeat it.","3":"Originality is not the goal; the one-liner that works often sounds a little boring to the person who wrote it."},"7":{"1":"Training may help, but the root is knowing too much: you can no longer see the product through first-time eyes.","2":"The messaging house doesn't fix it; without testing on newcomers, deep knowledge still hides the basics.","3":"More time helps, but the problem stays: whoever knows every corner skips the basic explanation."},"8":{"0":"A long video up front delays the moment the customer first sees value.","1":"A shorter tour still explains the product instead of leading the customer to an outcome.","2":"Emailing the tour later keeps the same menu tour, only delayed; there is still no short path to value."},"9":{"0":"Architecture belongs in the appendix; without the problem first, it is detail with no reason behind it.","2":"A feature list skips why it matters; leadership reads like a busy customer and decides in two minutes.","3":"A detailed timeline is the plan; it comes after the problem and what is at stake, not before them."},"10":{"1":"The team-lead message works, so you don't rewrite it; you add a story that fits the IT manager.","2":"A price cut doesn't answer the IT manager's own problem and fears; she needs her own story and proof.","3":"Each role in the buying group wants its own story; the IT manager simply needs a different one."},"11":{"0":"Going back to feature lists gives up clarity; the problem is sameness, not the approach itself.","1":"Copying the leader only increases the sameness that is already the problem; positioning is what sets you apart.","3":"Nothing says the template only fits cheap products; the point is that clarity alone does not differentiate."},"12":{"0":"An anonymous quote adds a feeling, but it doesn't show what actually changed.","1":"A team photo shows people, not the before-and-after change a guide needs as proof.","2":"A logo adds a sense of credibility, but it doesn't show what changed, or by what number."},"13":{"0":"Two equal actions force a choice, and the reader often doesn't choose at all.","1":"Different colors don't resolve the hesitation itself; one primary action with a smaller, softer option does.","3":"A third button only adds to the choice; the fix is one primary action, not more options."},"14":{"0":"A technical API description serves the few who keep reading; the opening is the change for the customer.","2":"A bug list belongs in the changelog, which comes later; the opening says what the customer can do today.","3":"A thank-you to the team puts Velox at the center; it can come later, if at all."}}}};
